    window.ZAYVORA_GRAND_LIBRARY_DATA = {"metadata": {"title": "ZAYVORA Grand Classical & Modern Integrative Medical Library", "arabic_title": "موسوعة زيورہ للطب التكاملي الدقيق", "urdu_title": "مکتبۂ زیورہ طبّی المتكامل", "version": "5.2.0-PROD", "total_volumes": 12, "total_chapters": 48, "statutory_standards": ["UPI Parts I-II", "NFUM Parts I-VI", "WHO SMART L4", "CDSCO Drugs & Cosmetics Act 1940 Schedule E(1)", "21 CFR Part 11"], "curation_status": "AUTHENTIC_CLINICAL_GRADE", "editorial_board": "Hakeem Dr. Irfan Ashraf (BUMS, Lead Clinician & Founder), ZAYVORA Clinical Governance & Pharmacopoeia Editorial Board"}, "volumes": [{"id": "vol-01", "volume_number": 1, "title_en": "Kulliyat-e-Tibb & Human Physiology", "title_ur": "کلیاتِ طب اور انسانی افعال الاعضاء", "title_ar": "كليات الطب و الفسيولوجيا البشرية", "icon": "🧬", "badge": "Classical Foundations", "summary": "Foundational doctrine of human existence, physical matter, humoral equilibrium, and biological faculties bridging Avicennian cellular pneuma with modern cellular thermodynamics.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-01-01", "chapter_number": "1.1", "title_en": "Arkan-e-Arba'a: The Four Elements & States of Matter", "title_ur": "ارکانِ اربعہ اور مادی تغیرات", "key_concepts": ["Naar (Fire / Thermal Energy)", "Hawa (Air / Gaseous State & Aeration)", "Maa (Water / Fluid Dynamics & Solvation)", "Arz (Earth / Solid Minerals & Cellular Density)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Book I)", "author": "Ibn Sina (Avicenna)", "text": "Arkan are the primary, indivisible bodies from which compound organisms are formed through chemical blending."}, {"book": "Kamil al-Sina'a al-Tibbiyya", "author": "Ali Ibn al-Abbas al-Majusi", "text": "The four fundamental qualities (Hararat, Buroodat, Yuboosat, Rutoobat) govern the equilibrium of Arkan."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Directly correlates with modern states of matter (Solid, Liquid, Gas, Plasma/Thermal energy) and intracellular electrolyte distribution (K+, Na+, Ca2+, Mg2+) maintaining membrane resting potentials.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Evaluation of constitutional density, bone mass, body hydration index, and basal body thermogenesis.", "actionable_protocols": "Equilibrium restoration through thermal modulation, hydration titration, and elemental mineral replenishment.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Avoid extreme thermal shock interventions in patients with acute hemodynamic instability.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-01-01", "volume_id": "vol-01", "chapter_title_en": "Arkan-e-Arba'a: The Four Elements & States of Matter", "chapter_title_ur": "ارکانِ اربعہ اور مادی تغیرات — طبیعیاتی حرارت اور حیاتیاتی اینٹروپی", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The doctrine of Arkan (Classical Elements) constitutes the ultimate biophysical foundation of Greco-Arab medicine, formulated by Empedocles, Hippocrates, Aristotle, Galen, and finalized by Ibn Sina in 'Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb' (Book 1, Fen 1). Ibn Sina defines Arkan as: 'الأركان هي أجرام بسيطة، هي أجزاء أولية لبدن الإنسان وغيره، ولا يمكن انقسامها إلى أجسام مختلفة الصور' (Arkan are simple, indivisible primary bodies which form the foundational substrates of the human organism and natural entities). The four Arkan represent the fundamental thermodynamic and physical states of existence:\n1. Arz (Earth / مٹی): Absolutely heavy and static. Primary quality: Coldness & Dryness (Barid-Yabis). Confers anatomical density, structural cohesion, and permanence to tissues (bones, cartilage, fibrillar matrices).\n2. Ma' (Water / پانی): Relatively heavy and fluid. Primary quality: Coldness & Moisture (Barid-Ratab). Confers fluidity, malleability, hydration shells around macromolecules, and facilitates biological diffusion.\n3. Hawa (Air / ہوا): Relatively light and rarefied. Primary quality: Hotness & Moisture (Garm-Ratab). Imparts porosity, expansion, elasticity, and facilitates internal pulmonary and tissue gas exchange.\n4. Nar (Fire / آگ): Absolutely light and energetic. Primary quality: Hotness & Dryness (Garm-Khushk). Represents biological heat, metabolic transformation (Nujd), and enzymatic breakdown.\nHealth exists when the mutual action and reaction of these four qualities achieves a harmonious equilibrium (Itidal-e-Mizaji). Disproportionate qualitative shifts produce Su-e-Mizaj Sada, while morbid fluid accumulation induces Su-e-Mizaj Maddi.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "From a comparative systems biology perspective, the classical doctrine of Arkan provides heuristic functional correlates to thermodynamic phases of matter and bioenergetic states:\n- Solid Phase (Arz): Extracellular matrix (ECM) scaffolds, cross-linked Type I/II collagen fibrillogenesis, hydroxyapatite crystallites in osseous tissue, and the polymerized actin-spectrin cytoskeleton.\n- Liquid Phase (Ma'): Intracellular and extracellular water (60% total body water), cytosolic hydration shells, hydrostatic/oncotic transmembrane pressures, and Gibbs-Donnan electrochemical equilibrium.\n- Gas Phase (Hawa): Pulmonary alveolar gas exchange, dissolved plasma oxygen tension (PaO2), arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2), bicarbonate buffering (CO2 + H2O <-> H2CO3 <-> HCO3- + H+), and gaseous signaling molecules including volatile Nitric Oxide (eNOS/iNOS) and Carbon Monoxide (HO-1).\n- Plasma & Energy State (Nar): Mitochondrial ATP generation via oxidative phosphorylation, the proton electrochemical gradient (ΔΨm) across the inner mitochondrial membrane, and non-shivering thermogenesis mediated by Uncoupling Protein 1 (UCP-1/Thermogenin) in brown adipose tissue.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Thermal Equilibrium Inspection: Palpate skin temperature bilaterally at the sternal core vs distal phalanges. Acral hypothermia with core normothermia indicates peripheral vasoconstriction (excess coldness/Barudat).\n2. Moisture & Hydration Assessment: Inspect oral mucosa, buccal gingiva, and sublingual folds for salivary pooling vs xerostomia. Perform cutaneous pinch test over forearm (delayed snapback indicates systemic Yaboosat/dryness).\n3. Tensile & Elasticity Evaluation: Assess joint range of motion and tissue suppleness. Excessive laxity denotes hyper-moist (Ratab) state; crepitus and rigid contracture denote dry (Yabis) state.\n4. Resonant Acoustic Percussion: Perform thoracic and abdominal percussion to differentiate gaseous distension (Nafakh/Hawa) from fluid dullness (Istisqa/Ma') and visceral tissue density (Arz).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Formula: Sufuf-e-Muharrik-e-Hararat (Thermal Activation Compound)\n- Indication: Severe Barudat (Hypometabolic hypothermia, sluggish circulation, chronic joint stiffness).\n- Recipe:\n  1. Zanjabeel (Zingiber officinale rhizome) — 30g (Hot 3°, Dry 2°)\n  2. Filfil Siyah (Piper nigrum fruit) — 15g (Hot 3°, Dry 3°)\n  3. Darchini (Cinnamomum verum bark) — 20g (Hot 3°, Dry 2°)\n  4. Qaranfal (Syzygium aromaticum flower buds) — 10g (Hot 3°, Dry 3°)\n- SOP: Clean raw botanicals, desiccate at 35°C, micro-pulverize through sieve #80. Store in airtight amber glass jars.\n- Dosing: 2g twice daily after meals with lukewarm water infused with 5ml pure honey (Ma-ul-Asal).\n- Musleh: Samagh-e-Arabi (Gum Acacia) 500mg if gastric irritation occurs.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 44-year-old male presenting with chronic generalized Barudat (cold intolerance, basal oral temperature 35.7°C, morning stiffness, sinus bradycardia 52 bpm, severe postprandial sleepiness).\nDiagnostics: TSH: 4.6 mIU/L (subclinical hypothyroid), Fasting Glucose: 108 mg/dL, hs-CRP: 2.8 mg/L. Pulse: Nabz-e-Batee, Saghir, Layyin (Slow, Small, Soft).\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Barid Balghami Maddi with diminished innate metabolic fire (Hararat-e-Ghariziyyah).\nProtocol: Days 1–14: Sufuf-e-Muharrik-e-Hararat 2g BID + Hab-e-Suranjan 1 tab OD after dinner + 20 min morning solar exposition (Tashmees). Elimination of dairy, cold water, and raw tubers.\nOutcome: Oral temperature normalized to 36.8°C; heart rate elevated to 68 bpm; postprandial lethargy abolished; hs-CRP decreased to 0.9 mg/L; subjective vitality scale improved from 3/10 to 8/10."}}, {"id": "ch-01-02", "chapter_number": "1.2", "title_en": "Akhlat-e-Arba'a: Humoral Dynamics & Fluid Compartments", "title_ur": "اخلاطِ اربعہ اور جسمانی مائعات", "key_concepts": ["Dam (Sanguineous Humor / Sanguine / Blood & Erythrocytes)", "Balgham (Phlegmatic Humor / Lymphatic fluid, Mucus, CSF)", "Safra (Choleric Humor / Bilirubin, Bile salts, Metabolic Catalysts)", "Sauda (Melancholic Humor / Spleen metabolites, Minerals, Platelets)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Kitab al-Hawi fit-Tibb", "author": "Abu Bakr al-Razi (Rhazes)", "text": "Humors are the circulating substrates synthesized by the liver through second digestion (Hazm-e-Sani)."}, {"book": "Makhzan-ul-Hikmat", "author": "Hakim Kabiruddin", "text": "Health is the quantitative and qualitative harmony of the four humors."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Correlates directly with Hematocrit, Plasma volume, Lymphatic transport, Hepatobiliary secretions, and Reticuloendothelial clearance.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Complete Blood Count (CBC), Serum Bilirubin, Viscosity markers, and Albumin/Globulin ratio.", "actionable_protocols": "Tanqiya (Purification) via Munzij-o-Mushil regimen when any humor becomes Fasid (morbid or dyscrasic).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Strong purgation (Mushil) strictly contraindicated in pregnant, emaciated, or severely hypotensive patients.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-01-02", "volume_id": "vol-01", "chapter_title_en": "Akhlat-e-Arba'a: Humoral Dynamics & Fluid Compartments", "chapter_title_ur": "اخلاطِ اربعہ اور جسمانی مائعات — ہضمِ ثانی، جگر اور عروقی توازن", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "In 'Kitab al-Hawi fit-Tibb', Al-Razi (Rhazes) establishes that humors are dynamic physiological fluids synthesized in the liver during Second Digestion (Hazm-e-Sani) from the nutrient chyme (Kiloos). Ibn Sina defines an Akhlat as a moist, fluid body into which ingested nourishment is primarily transformed. The four humors maintain systemic stoichiometry:\n1. Dam (Sanguine / خون): Hot-Moist (Har-Ratab). The supreme humor (Afzal-ul-Akhlat), carrying biological heat and nutrients to every tissue.\n2. Balgham (Phlegmatic / بلغم): Cold-Moist (Barid-Ratab). A raw metabolic reserve, mucosal lubricant, joint cushioning fluid, and thermal buffer.\n3. Safra (Yellow Bile / صفراء): Hot-Dry (Har-Yabis). Light, fiery, and bitter. Maintains blood viscosity, stimulates peristalsis, and emulsifies lipids.\n4. Sauda (Black Bile / سوداء): Cold-Dry (Barid-Yabis). Heavy, astringent, and earthy. Originates from dense metabolic sediments and splenic processing; confers tissue density, bone mineralization, and mental focus.\nEach humor has a normal (Mahmood) and abnormal (Ghair-Mahmood) state. Morbid accumulation or combustion (Ihtiraq) results in systemic dyscrasia (Ufoonat).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern physiological correlations:\n- Dam (Sanguine): Whole blood volume, normocytic normochromic erythrocytes, hemoglobin mass, and the oxygen delivery cascade (HbO2 saturation). Governs microvascular shear stress.\n- Balgham (Phlegm): Interstitial fluid, lymphatic circulation, high-molecular-weight proteoglycans, synovial hyaluronan, and mucosal surfactant coatings. Pathological Balgham reflects hypertriglyceridemia and plasma transudates.\n- Safra (Yellow Bile): Bile acid salts (cholic and chenodeoxycholic acids), bilirubin glucuronides, pancreatic lipases, and free fatty acid turnover. Excess Safra induces lipid peroxidation and glutathione depletion.\n- Sauda (Black Bile): Circulating platelet mass, splenic hemocatheresis products, hemosiderin complexes, and insoluble fibrin networks. Pathological Sauda correlates with hypercoagulability, elevated D-dimer, and neuro-inflammatory microglial activation.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Scleral & Mucosal Color Topography: Inspect bulbar conjunctiva under daylight. Florid red = Dam; milky pale = Balgham; icteric yellow = Safra; dusky leaden = Sauda.\n2. Tongue Dorsum Stratification: Thick white greasy coat = Balgham; thin bright yellow dry coat = Safra; dark brown/black dry crust = burnt Sauda; clean beefy red = Dam.\n3. Pulse Hemodynamic Palpation: Full, bounding, tense pulse = Damawi plethora; soft, undulating, wavy pulse = Balghami stagnation; sharp, rapid, hard pulse = Safrawi irritability; slow, thin, rigid pulse = Saudawi constriction.\n4. Subungual Capillary Refill: Delayed refill (>3 seconds) indicates Saudawi vasoconstriction or Balghami hypoperfusion.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Formula: Sharbat-e-Deenar (Hepatic & Humoral Decoction)\n- Indication: Stagnant Safra and Balgham accumulating in the hepatobiliary axis, presenting with visceral congestion and early hepatomegaly.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Tukhm-e-Kasni (Cichorium intybus seeds) — 20g\n  2. Tukhm-e-Kasoos (Cuscuta reflexa seeds) — 15g\n  3. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena petals) — 20g\n  4. Gul-e-Ghaafis (Agrimonia eupatoria flowers) — 15g\n  5. Rewand Chini (Rheum emodi root) — 10g\n  6. Qand Safaid (Refined sugar matrix) — 500g\n- SOP: Macerate botanicals in 1.5L distilled water for 12 hours. Boil down to 500ml. Strain through double muslin cloth. Add sugar and boil to three-thread density (Qiwam Seh Taar).\n- Dosing: 25ml mixed in 100ml lukewarm water, administered early morning fasting (Naharr).", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 39-year-old female executive with chronic Safrawi dyscrasia (epigastric burning, bitter taste, temporal headache with throbbing, loose bilious stools).\nInvestigations: Total Bilirubin: 1.4 mg/dL, Direct Bilirubin: 0.5 mg/dL, ALT: 48 U/L, ESR: 18 mm/hr. Pulse: Nabz-e-Azeem, Saree, Sulb.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Safrawi Maddi with biliary dyskinesia.\nProtocol: 7-day Munzij-e-Safra (Arq Mako 30ml + Arq Kasni 30ml with Sharbat-e-Bazoori 20ml BID), followed by Hijama Ma'a Shart (wet cupping, 35ml sanguineous-safrawi extraction).\nOutcome: Complete resolution of epigastric burning and cephalalgia; serum ALT normalized to 24 U/L; pulse softened to Mu'tadil cadence; restorative sleep achieved."}}, {"id": "ch-01-03", "chapter_number": "1.3", "title_en": "Ilm-ul-Mizaj: Temperamental Science & Metabolic Typing", "title_ur": "علم المزاج اور انفرادی بائیو کیمسٹری", "key_concepts": ["Mizaj-e-Mao'tadil (Balanced Optimal Temperament)", "Su-e-Mizaj Sada (Simple Temperamental Dyscrasia without matter)", "Su-e-Mizaj Maddi (Material Dyscrasia with humoral accumulation)", "Mizaj of Organs (Heart is Hot-Dry, Brain is Cold-Moist, Liver is Hot-Moist, Bone is Cold-Dry)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Tashreeh-e-Mizaj)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Mizaj is the resultant composite quality produced by the mutual interaction and alteration of the primary qualities."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Kulliyat", "author": "Ibn Rushd (Averroes)", "text": "Each organ possesses a distinct innate temperament suited to its physiological function."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Correlates with Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR), Autonomic Nervous System dominance (Sympathetic vs Parasympathetic), and Neuro-endocrine set-points.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "10 Ajnas inspection, body morphology, thermal tolerance, sleep duration, and metabolic speed.", "actionable_protocols": "Ilaj-bil-Zid (Treatment by Contraries): Hot dyscrasias treated with cooling diet/advia, Cold dyscrasias treated with warming regimen.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Never administer Daraja 3 or 4 heating drugs to a person presenting with acute Safrawi hyperpyrexia.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-01-03", "volume_id": "vol-01", "chapter_title_en": "Ilm-ul-Mizaj: Temperamental Science & Metabolic Typing", "chapter_title_ur": "علم المزاج اور انفرادی بائیو کیمسٹری — موروثی جینیات اور بائیو مارکرز", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ilm-ul-Mizaj (The Science of Temperament) is the cornerstone of personalized precision medicine in Unani clinical doctrine. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina establishes that every individual is born with a unique constitutional baseline (Mizaj-e-Jibli / Asli):\n1. Mizaj Damawi (Sanguine / Hot-Moist): Robust build, florid rosy complexion, prominent vasculature, active digestion, high physical stamina.\n2. Mizaj Balghami (Phlegmatic / Cold-Moist): Endomorphic build, fair/pale skin, soft flabby musculature, slow digestion, deep sleep, prone to fluid retention.\n3. Mizaj Safrawi (Choleric / Hot-Dry): Ecto-mesomorphic build, yellowish skin, sharp gaze, rapid digestion, light sleep, assertive nature, heat intolerant.\n4. Mizaj Saudawi (Melancholic / Cold-Dry): Asthenic lean build, dusky skin, prominent bony landmarks, variable digestion, analytical serious disposition, prone to rumination.\nEvery internal organ possesses an inherent organ temperament (Mizaj-ul-Aaza): Heart is Hot-Dry; Brain is Cold-Moist; Liver is Hot-Moist; Bones are Cold-Dry.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Biomedical mapping of temperamental phenotypes:\n- Hot Phenotypes (Damawi/Safrawi): Heightened sympathetic autonomic tone, elevated resting basal metabolic rate (BMR), increased peripheral thyroid hormone receptor sensitivity (elevated T3/T4 conversion), and enhanced glucose transporter type 4 (GLUT4) translocation.\n- Cold Phenotypes (Balghami/Saudawi): Predominant parasympathetic tone, lower resting metabolic rate, reduced brown adipose tissue thermogenic density, downregulated beta-3 adrenergic receptor sensitivity, and slower hepatic cytochrome P450 phase I clearance.\n- Moist Phenotypes (Damawi/Balghami): Expanded circulating plasma volume, increased endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) activity, and elevated antidiuretic hormone (ADH/Vasopressin) sensitivity.\n- Dry Phenotypes (Safrawi/Saudawi): Higher transepidermal water loss (TEWL), low synovial hyaluronic acid concentrations, heightened RAAS reactivity, and increased baseline inflammatory cytokine expression (IL-1beta, TNF-alpha).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "The 10 Canonical Determinants of Individual Mizaj (Ajnas-e-Ashara):\n1. Malmas (Skin Feel): Hot/Cold denotes temperature axis; Soft/Hard denotes moisture axis.\n2. Lahm-o-Shahm (Body Composition): Ratio of muscle mass (Lahm) to adipose tissue (Shahm).\n3. Sha'ar (Hair Topography): Rapid growth, thick, dark curly hair = Hot-Dry; slow growing, fine, straight hair = Cold-Moist.\n4. Laun-ul-Badan (Skin Color): Florid pink (Dam), pale alabaster (Balgham), sallow yellow (Safra), dusky leaden (Sauda).\n5. Hayat-ul-Aaza (Morphological Frame): Broad deep chest = Damawi; narrow contracted chest = Saudawi.\n6. Infi'al-ul-Hawaas (Sensory Reactivity): Immediate sharp response = Hot-Dry; delayed lethargic response = Cold-Moist.\n7. Af'aal-ul-Aaza (Functional Speed): Speed of digestive transit, cardiac output, and muscular response.\n8. Fuzlaat (Excretions): Volume, odor, and color of sweat, urine, and stool.\n9. Naum-o-Yaqza (Sleep): Deep prolonged sleep (>8h) = Cold-Moist; light short sleep (<6h) = Hot-Dry/Cold-Dry.\n10. Harkat-o-Sukoon Nafsani (Emotion): Rapid anger & rapid cooling = Safra; prolonged brooding = Sauda; optimism = Dam; placid calmness = Balgham.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Formula: Majun Mu'tadil-ul-Mizaj (Temperamental Normalizer)\n- Indication: Constitutional dyscrasia, chronic convalescence, seasonal disequilibrium.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Maghz-e-Badam Shireen (Prunus dulcis kernels) — 50g\n  2. Maghz-e-Fistuq (Pistacia vera kernels) — 30g\n  3. Tukhm-e-Kahu (Lactuca sativa seeds) — 20g\n  4. Tukhm-e-Khashkhash (Papaver somniferum seeds) — 20g\n  5. Zafran (Crocus sativus stigmas) — 2g (triturated in Arq Gulab)\n  6. Asal-e-Khalis (Pure mountain honey) — 350g\n- Method: Blanch nuts, remove skins, dry under shade, micro-grind. Dissolve saffron in 10ml rose water. Incorporate into warmed honey matrix at 45°C. Age for 7 days in glazed earthenware jar.\n- Dosage: 7g morning and evening with 150ml goat's milk or almond milk.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 28-year-old male software architect of innate Safrawi temperament presenting with acute Su-e-Mizaj Har-Yabis (insomnia, aphthous stomatitis, ocular burning, pulse 94 bpm bounding, severe anxiety, ALT 52 U/L, morning cortisol 24 mcg/dL).\nTrigger: Night shifts, excessive espresso (6 cups/day), fried spicy diet.\nProtocol: Dietary elimination of stimulants and allium vegetables. Hydration diet: barley water (Ma-ul-Sha\'eer) and cucumber-seed infusion. Pharmacotherapy: Sharbat-e-Nilofar 25ml BID + Khamira Gaozaban Ambari Jadwar Wala 5g at bedtime. Natool (cranial hydrotherapy) with violet-water before sleep.\nOutcome (Day 14): Complete resolution of stomatitis; sleep duration restored to 7.5 hours continuous; pulse normalized to 72 bpm; morning cortisol dropped to 14.2 mcg/dL; emotional equanimity restored."}}, {"id": "ch-01-04", "chapter_number": "1.4", "title_en": "Quwa & Afaal: Vital, Natural & Psychic Physiology", "title_ur": "قویٰ اور افعال — حیوانی، طبعی اور نفسیاتی نظام", "key_concepts": ["Quwwat-e-Tabiyyah (Natural/Metabolic Faculty — Liver seat)", "Quwwat-e-Haywaniyyah (Vital/Cardiovascular Faculty — Heart seat)", "Quwwat-e-Nafsaniyyah (Psychic/Neurological Faculty — Brain seat)", "Afaal-e-Mufarridah & Afaal-e-Murakkabah (Simple vs Complex Biochemical Actions)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Firdaus al-Hikma", "author": "Ali Ibn Rabban al-Tabari", "text": "The three master organs (Aaza-e-Raeesa) distribute the three primary faculties via arteries, nerves, and veins."}, {"book": "Sharah al-Asbab wal Alamat", "author": "Burhanuddin Nafis Kirmani", "text": "When Quwwat-e-Mudabbira-e-Badan (Homeostasis) weakens, disease takes root."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Correlates with Neuro-Cardio-Metabolic axis: Endocrine-metabolic homeostasis, Autonomic cardiac regulation, and Central Nervous System cognition.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Heart rate, cognitive responsiveness, appetite efficiency, and muscular tone.", "actionable_protocols": "Tonification of master organs with Muqawwiyat-e-Aaza-e-Raeesa (Cardiotonics, Hepatoprotectives, Neuroprotectives).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Differentiate organic organ failure from functional fatigue before administering stimulating tonics.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-01-04", "volume_id": "vol-01", "chapter_title_en": "Quwa & Afaal: Vital, Natural & Psychic Physiology", "chapter_title_ur": "قویٰ اور افعال — حیوانی، طبعی اور نفسیاتی نظام اور ہومیوسٹاسس", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Quwa (Biological Faculties) represent the functional energies and homeostatic mechanisms of human life, while Afaal (Actions/Functions) are their measurable manifestations. Ali ibn Rabban al-Tabari in 'Firdaus al-Hikma' and Ibn Sina in 'Al-Qanoon' categorize Quwa into three sovereign domains anchored in the Three Master Organs (Al-Aaza al-Ra'eesa):\n1. Quwwat-e-Tabi'iyyah (Natural/Metabolic Faculty — Liver seat): Encompasses nutritive faculties (Jadhba/Attraction, Masika/Retention, Hadima/Digestion, Dafi'a/Expulsion) and growth/gametic faculties.\n2. Quwwat-e-Hayawaniyyah (Vital/Cardiovascular Faculty — Heart seat): Governs pulsatile arterial expansion (Inbisat) and contraction (Inqibad), distribution of innate pneuma (Rooh-e-Hayawani), and somatic life preservation.\n3. Quwwat-e-Nafsaniyyah (Psychic/Neurological Faculty — Brain seat): Encompasses cognitive faculties (internal senses: Hiss-e-Mushtarak, Khayal, Wahm, Hafiza) and motor faculties governing locomotion.\nThe supreme intelligence coordinating these faculties is Tabi'at (Vis Medicatrix Naturae); the physician is its humble facilitator (Khadim-e-Tabi'at).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Biomedical mapping of the Tri-Organ axis:\n- Quwwat-e-Tabi'iyyah: Hepatic intermediate metabolism, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, urea cycle detoxification, serum albumin synthesis, enteroendocrine peptide release (CCK, GLP-1), and cellular anabolic signaling via mTORC1 and IGF-1.\n- Quwwat-e-Hayawaniyyah: Cardiac electrophysiology (SA node pacemaking, Purkinje conduction), baroreceptor reflex sensitivity, Starling's law of the heart, microvascular perfusion, and cellular oxygen utilization via mitochondrial Cytochrome c oxidase.\n- Quwwat-e-Nafsaniyyah: Central nervous system neuroplasticity, monoaminergic neurotransmission (dopamine, serotonin, norepinephrine), hippocampal synaptic potentiation, and efferent corticospinal motor unit recruitment.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Quwwat-e-Tabi'iyyah Assessment: Appetite vigor (Ishtiha), postprandial abdominal comfort, bowel regularity, serum albumin, and muscle wasting.\n2. Quwwat-e-Hayawaniyyah Assessment: Resting heart rate, pulse pressure (SBP - DBP), orthostatic BP stability, and exercise recovery heart rate after 2-minute step test.\n3. Quwwat-e-Nafsaniyyah Assessment: Mini-mental cognitive status, cranial nerve symmetry, patellar reflex latency, and grip dynamometry.\n4. Tabi'at Stress Resilience: Speed of cutaneous wound healing and recovery latency after mild physical exertion.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Formula: Dawa-ul-Misk Har Motadil (Tri-Organ Vital Tonic)\n- Indication: Debility of liver, heart, and brain (Zof-e-Aaza-e-Raeesa), post-viral convalescence, chronic fatigue.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Mushk (Ambrette seed replacement) — 1g\n  2. Zafran (Crocus sativus) — 3g\n  3. Ood Saleeb (Paeonia emodi root) — 10g\n  4. Darunaj Aqrabi (Doronicum hookeri root) — 10g\n  5. Zarambad (Curcuma zedoaria rhizome) — 10g\n  6. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena) — 15g\n  7. Marwareed Saeeda (Levigated Pearl micro-powder) — 5g\n  8. Shakar Safaid (Refined syrup) — 400g\n- Compounding: Micro-pulverize non-volatile botanicals. Triturate pearls and saffron in rose water for 6 hours. Incorporate into boiled syrup at 105°C, cool to 40°C, then add aromatics.\n- Dosage: 5g twice daily with lukewarm milk or rose water.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 56-year-old corporate director presenting with severe multisystem vital exhaustion (Zof-e-Aaza-e-Raeesa) 3 months post-viral infection (cognitive brain fog, exertional palpitations HR 98 bpm, gastroparesis, orthostatic dizziness).\nBaseline: HRV rMSSD: 14ms (depressed vagal tone), Fasting Insulin: 18.2 microIU/mL, ALT 44 U/L.\nProtocol: Dawa-ul-Misk Har Motadil 5g BID + Khamira Abresham Hakim Arshad Wala 3g morning fasting + Arq Gaozaban 60ml. Dalak Laiyin (effleurage massage) with warm sesame/almond oil along paraspinal chains 3x/week.\nOutcome (Day 30): HRV rMSSD increased from 14ms to 38ms; exertional tachycardia resolved (resting HR 70 bpm); cognitive recall restored; bowel transit normalized to daily morning evacuation."}}]}, {"id": "vol-02", "volume_number": 2, "title_en": "Asbab-e-Sitta Zarooriyya: The Six Essential Prerequisites", "title_ur": "اسبابِ ستہ ضروریہ اور احتیاطی طرزِ زندگی", "title_ar": "الأسباب الستة الضرورية و طب نمط الحياة", "icon": "🌿", "badge": "Preventive Lifestyle", "summary": "The immutable non-negotiable six lifestyle determinants of human health and longevity, representing classical epigenetics and environmental medicine.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-02-01", "chapter_number": "2.1", "title_en": "Hawa-e-Muheet: Atmospheric Aeration, Air Quality & Breathwork", "title_ur": "ہوائے محیط — سانس، ماحولیاتی آلودگی اور تبادلہ گیس", "key_concepts": ["Tanaffus (Respiration & Vital Heat Dissipation)", "Taghayyur-e-Hawa (Air Contamination & Particulate Pollution)", "Tanaffus-e-Amiq (Diaphragmatic Deep Breathing Protocols)", "Seasonal Weather Fronts & Barometric Shifts"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Tadbeer-e-Hawa)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Clean, fragrant, temperate air is the swiftest agent to alter the Rooh (Vital Pneuma)."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Murshid", "author": "Abu Bakr al-Razi", "text": "Stagnant, foul air produces putrefaction in the humors."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Alveolar gas exchange, VO2 Max, Ambient PM2.5/PM10 cardiovascular risks, and Nitric Oxide synthesis via nasal breathing.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Pulse Oximetry (SpO2), Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR), and respiratory rate variability.", "actionable_protocols": "Pranayama / Tanaffus-e-Amiq (4-7-8 breathing) 15 mins twice daily; air purification in urban habitats.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Avoid heavy outdoor aerobic exertion during severe atmospheric inversion and high AQI smog alerts.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-01", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Hawa-e-Muheet: Atmospheric Aeration, Air Quality & Breathwork", "chapter_title_ur": "ہوائے محیط — سانس، ماحولیاتی آلودگی اور پرانایام کا سائنسی تجزیہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Hawa-e-Muheet (Ambient Atmospheric Air) is the foremost prerequisite among the Asbab-e-Sitta Zarooriyya, as human life cannot endure its absence even for a few minutes. In 'Kamil al-Sina'a al-Tibbiyya', Ali ibn al-Abbas al-Majusi writes: 'الهواء هو أعدل الأشياء التي تفعل في أبداننا، لأنه يداخلنا في كل وقت' (Air is the most direct external agent acting upon our bodies because it enters into us continuously without interruption). \nIbn Sina notes in 'Al-Qanoon' that the primary function of respiration is two-fold:\n1. Tarweeh (Tash'eer/Cooling of Innate Heat): Supplying cool atmospheric air to prevent combustion of the innate vital spirit (Rooh-e-Hayawani) in the left cardiac ventricle.\n2. Tanqiya (Evacuation of Fuliginous Vapors / Dukhaniyyah): Expelling hot smoky metabolic waste products via exhalation.\nContaminated air (Hawa-e-Wabai / polluted air) alters the temperament of the lungs and blood, precipitating epidemics and systemic inflammatory states.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Contemporary pulmonary pathophysiology and environmental toxicology correlate:\n- Tarweeh corresponds to pulmonary alveolar-capillary oxygen diffusion across the 0.5-micron blood-gas barrier, arterial blood oxygenation (PaO2 80-100 mmHg), and mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation in tissue capillary beds.\n- Tanqiya corresponds to carbon dioxide clearance (PaCO2 35-45 mmHg) and systemic acid-base buffering via carbonic anhydrase.\n- Hawa-e-Wabai correlates with ambient Particulate Matter (PM2.5, PM10), Nitrogen Dioxide (NO2), Ozone (O3), and volatile organic compounds (VOCs). Inhaled ultrafine particles translocate into microcirculation, activating toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), triggering alveolar macrophage activation, and releasing systemic pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha). Slow diaphragmatic breathwork stimulates pulmonary stretch receptors, activating efferent vagal nerve firing and enhancing respiratory sinus arrhythmia (RSA).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Thoracic Expansion Dynamometry: Measure chest circumference at the xiphoid level during maximal expiration vs maximal inspiration. Normal excursion is >= 5 cm; <3 cm indicates restricted diaphragmatic excursion.\n2. Auscultation of Bronchovesicular Sounds: Auscultate all lung fields for wheezing (Kharkhara/Safra), coarse crackles (stagnant Balgham), or diminished air entry.\n3. Arterial Oxygen Saturation & Breath-Hold Latency: Measure resting SpO2 via pulse oximetry. Perform the classical Kumbhaka/breath-hold latency test (healthy baseline >= 45 seconds; <25 seconds indicates pulmonary-cardiac reserve impairment).\n4. Environmental Exposure History: Document residential PM2.5 averages, passive tobacco exposure, and workplace volatile chemical hazards.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Pulmonary Decongestant: Sharbat-e-Zofa Murakkab\n- Indication: Bronchial congestion, atmospheric pollution-induced cough, sluggish phlegmatic expectoration.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Zofa Khushk (Hyssopus officinalis herb) — 20g\n  2. Asl-us-Soos (Glycyrrhiza glabra root) — 20g\n  3. Tukhm-e-Khatmi (Althaea officinalis seeds) — 15g\n  4. Tukhm-e-Khubbazi (Malva sylvestris seeds) — 15g\n  5. Anjeer Zard (Ficus carica dried yellow figs) — 5 pieces\n  6. Munaqqa (Vitis vinifera large dried raisins) — 20g\n  7. Qand Safaid — 400g\n- SOP: Boil botanicals and dried fruits in 1.2L water until reduced to 400ml. Filter through linen. Add sugar matrix and boil to two-thread syrup.\n- Dosage: 20ml mixed in 100ml warm water morning and bedtime.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 51-year-old male traffic warden in urban New Delhi presenting with chronic smog-induced tracheobronchitis, dry paroxysmal cough, dyspnea on exertion, baseline SpO2 93%, FEV1/FVC 72%.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Barid-Yabis of the respiratory tract with secondary particulate-induced inflammation.\nProtocol: Sharbat-e-Zofa Murakkab 20ml BID + Arq Badiyan 50ml + Inhalation of steam infused with eucalyptus and chamomile essential oils. 20 minutes daily diaphragmatic Pranayama (Anulom-Vilom) with HEPA air filtration.\nOutcome (Day 21): Complete resolution of cough; resting SpO2 improved to 98%; FEV1/FVC improved to 81%; exercise tolerance restored from 200m to >2km walking without dyspnea."}}, {"id": "ch-02-02", "chapter_number": "2.2", "title_en": "Makool-o-Mashroob: Chrono-Nutrition & Food-as-Medicine", "title_ur": "ماکول و مشروب — غذائی نظام اور اوقاتِ طعام", "key_concepts": ["Hazm-e-Arba'a (Four Stages of Digestion: Gastric, Hepatic, Vascular, Cellular)", "Tarkeeb-e-Ghiza (Nutrient Quality: Lateef, Kaseef, Mo'tadil)", "Chrono-nutrition: Circadian insulin sensitivity & meal timing", "Hydration Volume & Water Quality (Maa-e-Muqattar / Distilled Mineral)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Kitab al-Aghziya", "author": "Ishaq Ibn Imran", "text": "Do not take food upon un-digested food; this is the primary parent of all chronic ailments."}, {"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Water serves as the solvent that conveys the nutrients to the deep tissues."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Gastric emptying rates, postprandial glucose excursions, incretin secretion (GLP-1), and gut microbiome diversity.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) excursions, postprandial somnolence, and gastric transit time.", "actionable_protocols": "Two balanced meals aligned with daytime circadian peaks; warm hydration; 12–14 hour overnight fasting.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Strictly prohibit iced drinks immediately following heavy meals to prevent gastric enzyme inhibition.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-02", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Makool-o-Mashroob: Chrono-Nutrition & Food-as-Medicine", "chapter_title_ur": "ماکول و مشروب — غذائی نظام، ہضم کے چار درجات اور خوراک بطور دوا", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "In classical doctrine, food is the primary fuel maintaining the innate moisture (Ratoobat-e-Ghariziyyah) and innate heat (Hararat-e-Ghariziyyah). Hippocrates famously formulated: 'Let food be thy medicine', which Ibn Sina systematically codified in 'Al-Qanoon' through the Four Stages of Digestion (Hazm-e-Arba'a):\n1. Hazm-e-Awwal (First/Gastric Digestion): In the stomach and duodenum, converting ingested food into liquid nutrient chyme (Kiloos).\n2. Hazm-e-Sani (Second/Hepatic Digestion): In the liver, transforming Kiloos into the four humors (Dam, Balgham, Safra, Sauda), separating watery waste to the kidneys.\n3. Hazm-e-Salis (Third/Vascular Digestion): In the blood vessels, delivering humors to target tissues and fine-tuning fluid density.\n4. Hazm-e-Rabi' (Fourth/Cellular Assimilation): In cellular tissue interstices (Kimoos), where nutrients are assimilated into cellular protoplasm (Tashbeeh and Ilsaaq).\nUnani classifies all foods according to their humoral temperament and therapeutic strength: Ghiza-e-Mutlaq (pure nutritive food), Ghiza-e-Dawai (medicinal food), and Dawa-e-Ghizai (nutritive drug).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern gastrointestinal physiology and nutritional biochemistry correlate:\n- Hazm-e-Awwal corresponds to gastric acid proteolysis (pepsinogen -> pepsin at pH 1.5-2.0), mechanical churning, duodenal emulsification via pancreatic enzymes (amylase, lipase, trypsin), and enteroendocrine satiety signaling (cholecystokinin, GLP-1, PYY).\n- Hazm-e-Sani corresponds to mesenteric portal venous absorption, first-pass hepatic metabolism, glycogen synthesis, lipogenesis, and bile acid conjugation.\n- Hazm-e-Salis corresponds to systemic capillary endothelial transport, insulin-mediated glucose uptake (GLUT4), and amino acid uptake via system A/L transporters.\n- Hazm-e-Rabi' corresponds to mitochondrial substrate oxidation, Krebs cycle, protein synthesis via ribosomes, and autophagic cellular recycling regulated by mTOR and AMPK.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Satiety & Gastric Emptying History: Inquire regarding postprandial heaviness (Saqal), acid reflux (Humoozat), or early satiety. Postprandial fullness lasting >3 hours signifies Hazm-e-Awwal sluggishness.\n2. Epigastric Palpation & Succession Splash: Palpate epigastrium 2 hours post-meal. Presence of succussion splash indicates delayed gastric emptying (gastroparesis).\n3. Postprandial Glycemic Telemetry: Correlate glycemic excursion via continuous glucose monitoring (peak target <140 mg/dL; return to baseline within 120 minutes).\n4. Bristol Stool Form & Transit Evaluation: Grade stool form (Types 1-2 = dry Saudawi transit; Types 3-4 = optimal balanced transit; Types 6-7 = rapid undigested Balghami/Safrawi transit).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Digestive Electuary: Jawarish Kamuni (Carminative Formulation)\n- Indication: Gastric coldness (Zof-e-Mida Barid), flatulence, delayed gastric emptying, Kiloos fermentation.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Zeera Siyah Mudabbar (Carum carvi seeds soaked in vinegar & roasted) — 50g\n  2. Filfil Siyah (Piper nigrum) — 20g\n  3. Zanjabeel (Zingiber officinale) — 20g\n  4. Bura-e-Armani (Purified Armenian bole) — 5g\n  5. Asal-e-Khalis (Pure honey) — 300g\n- Compounding: Macerate caraway seeds in grape vinegar for 24 hours, dry over gentle heat, then powder with other spices. Blend into warm honey.\n- Dosage: 5g immediately following meals.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 42-year-old male executive with postprandial dyspepsia, abdominal distension, postprandial somnolence, fasting insulin 19.4 microIU/mL, HOMA-IR 4.2.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Mida Barid with Hazm-e-Awwal impairment and hepatic insulin resistance.\nProtocol: Elimination of ultra-processed carbohydrates and ice-cold water. Introduction of chrono-nutrition: large midday meal, light evening meal before 7 PM. Prescription of Jawarish Kamuni 5g post-meals + Arq Badiyan 50ml.\nOutcome (Day 30): Postprandial distension resolved; fasting insulin reduced from 19.4 to 8.6 microIU/mL; HOMA-IR dropped to 1.8; 3.5kg reduction in visceral fat mass."}}, {"id": "ch-02-03", "chapter_number": "2.3", "title_en": "Harkat-o-Sukoon-e-Badani: Physical Exercise & Ergonomics", "title_ur": "حرکت و سکونِ بدنی — ورزش، آرام اور پٹھوں کی بحالی", "key_concepts": ["Riyazat (Therapeutic Physical Exercise)", "Riyazat-e-Kaseera vs Riyazat-e-Mo'tadila (Strenuous vs Moderate Exercise)", "Damm-e-Tawallud (Elicitation of Innate Heat via Movement)", "Ergonomic Posture & Sedentary Deconditioning"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "If a person adopts regular moderate exercise, he will need neither medicine nor physician for most ailments."}, {"book": "Zakhira-e-Khwarizmshahi", "author": "Ismail Jurjani", "text": "Exercise stimulates innate heat and dissolves residual waste products (Fuzool)."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle, mitochondrial biogenesis, myokine release (Irisin), and cardiovascular remodeling.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Daily step count, resting heart rate, active calorie burn, and musculoskeletal range of motion.", "actionable_protocols": "30–45 mins daily Zone 2 aerobic activity with twice-weekly resistance training tailored to individual Mizaj.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Contraindicated in severe febrile states, acute cardiac ischemia, or unreduced joint dislocations.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-03", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Harkat-o-Sukoon-e-Badani: Physical Exercise & Ergonomics", "chapter_title_ur": "حرکت و سکونِ بدنی — جسمانی ورزش، حیاتیاتی ایندھن اور مسلز کی بحالی", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ibn Sina declares in 'Al-Qanoon' that Riyazat (Physical Exercise) is the paramount preserver of human health, writing: 'أهم تدبير لحفظ الصحة هو الرياضة، ثم تدبير الغذاء، ثم تدبير النوم' (The most crucial regimen for the preservation of health is physical exercise, followed by dietary regulation, followed by sleep management).\nRiyazat is defined as voluntary physical movement that induces deep, accelerated respiration. Its primary physiological purposes are:\n1. Ishti'al-e-Hararat-e-Ghariziyyah: Kindling the innate biological heat and accelerating metabolic clearance.\n2. Daf'a-e-Fuzlaat: Dispersing subtle metabolic wastes through perspiration and respiratory vapor before they accumulate and undergo putrefactive fermentation (Ufoonat).\n3. Taqwiyat-e-Aaza: Strengthening muscles, ligaments, and visceral organs to withstand external pathogens.\nRiyazat is classified into Khafeef (Light), Motadil (Moderate), and Qawi (Strenuous). Strenuous exertion beyond constitutional tolerance causes Tahlil-e-Ratoobat (pathological moisture depletion) and systemic exhaustion.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern exercise physiology and molecular bioenergetics correlate:\n- Kindling innate heat corresponds to the activation of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) and Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma coactivator 1-alpha (PGC-1alpha), stimulating mitochondrial biogenesis in skeletal muscle.\n- Daf'a-e-Fuzlaat corresponds to muscle-contraction-induced lymphatic flow, enhanced GLUT4 transporter translocation independent of insulin, and clearance of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs).\n- Physical exercise stimulates the release of myokines, notably Irisin and Interleukin-6 (acting in an anti-inflammatory endocrine capacity), which downregulate systemic TNF-alpha, enhance hippocampal BDNF expression, and promote browning of white adipose tissue.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Cardiorespiratory VO2 Max & Step Challenge: Perform the YMCA 3-minute step test (24 steps/min). Measure heart rate immediately post-test and 1-minute post-test to determine cardiac recovery index.\n2. Musculoskeletal Ergonomics & Postural Assessment: Screen for forward head carriage, thoracic kyphosis, pelvic tilt, and core stabilizer endurance (plank hold test target >= 60 seconds).\n3. Flexibility & Joint Range of Motion: Perform sit-and-reach test and functional squat evaluation to identify myofascial tightness and articular crepitus.\n4. Sarcopenia Screening: Perform handgrip dynamometry (abnormal <28 kg in men, <18 kg in women) and bioimpedance skeletal muscle mass index (SMMI).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Post-Exertional Restorative: Raughan-e-Balsan & Raughan-e-Zaitoon Rub\n- Indication: Myofascial fatigue, delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS), post-exertional muscular Barudat.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Raughan-e-Zaitoon (Extra virgin cold-pressed olive oil) — 100ml\n  2. Raughan-e-Balsan (Commiphora gileadensis oil / Balsam of Mecca) — 10ml\n  3. Qaranfal (Clove oil) — 5ml\n  4. Kafoor (Natural Cinnamomum camphora crystals) — 2g\n- Preparation: Gently warm olive oil to 40°C. Dissolve camphor crystals until fully incorporated. Add balsam and clove oils.\n- Application: Warm application followed by gentle effleurage massage (Dalak Layyin) over strained muscle groups.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 36-year-old sedentary IT manager with chronic myofascial back pain, metabolic syndrome, fasting triglycerides 240 mg/dL, skeletal muscle mass index 26%.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Barid-Ratab induced by physical inactivity (Qillat-e-Riyazat) and metabolic waste retention.\nProtocol: Prescribed graduated Riyazat-e-Motadila: 30 minutes zone-2 aerobic brisk walking combined with resistance training 3x/week. Post-exercise Dalak with Raughan-e-Zaitoon compound.\nOutcome (Day 45): Back pain completely resolved; triglycerides decreased from 240 to 135 mg/dL; SMMI increased by 2.2%; VO2 max improved by 18%."}}, {"id": "ch-02-04", "chapter_number": "2.4", "title_en": "Naum-o-Yaqza: Sleep Architecture & Circadian Entrainment", "title_ur": "نوم و یقظہ — نیند، بیداری اور ہارمونی بحالی", "key_concepts": ["Naum-e-Amiq (Deep Slow-Wave Sleep / Anabolic Stage)", "Tanqiya-e-Dimagh (Glymphatic Cerebral Cleansing during Sleep)", "Yuboosat-e-Dimagh due to Sahar (Chronic Sleep Deprivation)", "Circadian Light Entrainment (Morning Sunlight & Night Melatonin)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Sleep draws innate heat inwards to digest food and replenish the vital pneuma."}, {"book": "Makhzan-ul-Advia", "author": "Sayyid Nooruddin", "text": "Excessive wakefulness dries the brain and excites black bile."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Glymphatic clearance of Beta-Amyloid, Cortisol nadir, Growth Hormone release, and REM memory consolidation.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Sleep hypnogram, Oura/Apple Watch Sleep Stage analysis (Deep > 15%, REM > 20%), and Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index.", "actionable_protocols": "Consistent 10:00 PM sleep window, zero blue light 90 mins prior, cool ambient bedroom (18–20°C).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Avoid hypnotic pharmaceutical sedatives that obliterate natural restorative REM and N3 deep sleep.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-04", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Naum-o-Yaqza: Sleep Architecture & Circadian Entrainment", "chapter_title_ur": "نوم و یقظہ — سلیپ آرکیٹیکچر، دماغی صفائی اور سرکاڈین ردھم", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Sleep (Naum) and Wakefulness (Yaqza) govern the systemic distribution of biological heat and sensory faculties. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina states: 'النوم يغوص بالحرارة الغريزية إلى الباطن، فيقوى الهضم وتستريح القوى النفسانية' (Sleep draws the innate heat into the interior of the body, whereby digestion is fortified and the psychic faculties are rested).\nDuring physiological sleep:\n1. Inward Retreat of Heat: Sensory faculties rest while internal digestive and cellular repair mechanisms (Hazm-e-Salis and Hazm-e-Rabi') operate at peak capacity.\n2. Replenishment of Moisture: Sleep generates innate restorative moisture (Ratoobat-e-Asliyyah) and cools excessive psychic excitation.\nConversely, excessive wakefulness (Sahar) consumes innate moisture, overheats the brain, and dries out the nervous system, predisposing to chronic melancholic disorders (Malikhooliya).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern neurobiology and circadian medicine correlate:\n- The inward retreat of heat corresponds to the activation of the parasympathetic nervous system, reduction in nocturnal core body temperature by 0.5-1.0°C, and nocturnal growth hormone secretion.\n- Restorative cerebral cooling corresponds to slow-wave sleep (N3 delta sleep) and the activation of the glymphatic system (astroglial AQP4 water channels), which accelerates the convective clearance of neurotoxic metabolic waste, notably amyloid-beta and phosphorylated tau proteins.\n- Circadian entrainment is governed by the hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), synchronized via melanopsin-expressing retinal ganglion cells, regulating pineal melatonin biosynthesis (inhibited by blue light, synthesized in darkness).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Sleep Architecture Questionnaire: Administer the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) and Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS).\n2. Circadian Photoperiod Audit: Document evening screen exposure within 2 hours of bedtime, bedroom light pollution (lux level), and morning solar exposure timing.\n3. Wearable Hypnogram Analysis: Review wearable telemetry for deep sleep (N3 target >= 15-20% total sleep time), REM sleep (target >= 20-25%), and wake-after-sleep-onset (WASO <30 min).\n4. Oropharyngeal Airway Screening: Inspect uvular elongation and Mallampati score (Classes III-IV indicate obstructive sleep apnea risk).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Somniferous Neuro-Sedative: Roghan-e-Khashkhash & Roghan-e-Kahu (Topical Application)\n- Indication: Chronic Sahar (Insomnia), hyperactive psychic excitation, cerebral dryness (Yaboosat-e-Dimagh).\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Roghan-e-Khashkhash (Papaver somniferum seed oil) — 50ml\n  2. Roghan-e-Kahu (Lactuca sativa seed oil) — 50ml\n  3. Roghan-e-Banafsha (Viola odorata flower oil) — 30ml\n- Method: Blend cold-pressed oils. Warm slightly to 37°C.\n- Application: Massage 5ml gently over anterior temporal fossa, forehead, and soles of both feet (Talwon ki Malish) 20 minutes prior to intended sleep.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 33-year-old financial analyst with chronic sleep-onset insomnia (sleep latency 90-120 min, total sleep 4.5 hours), severe morning grogginess, salivary cortisol flatlined.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Dimagh Har-Yabis secondary to excessive Sahar and circadian disruption.\nProtocol: Digital curfew after 9 PM. Topical temporal massage with Roghan-e-Khashkhash compound + internal administration of Khamira Gaozaban Ambari 5g with warm milk before sleep.\nOutcome (Day 21): Sleep onset latency reduced to 18 minutes; total sleep time increased to 7.4 hours; wearable tracker recorded 22% deep sleep; daytime focus and cognitive clarity restored."}}, {"id": "ch-02-05", "chapter_number": "2.5", "title_en": "Istifragh-o-Ihtibas: Evacuation, Retention & Detox Pathways", "title_ur": "استفراغ و احتباس — سموم کا اخراج اور ضروری اجزاء کی حفاظت", "key_concepts": ["Tanqiya (Systemic Evacuation of Morbid Matter)", "Ihtibas-e-Fasid (Pathological Retention of Toxins / Constipation, Oliguria)", "Istifragh-e-Mufrit (Excessive Depletion / Dehydration, Cachexia)", "Emanctories (Meda, Amaa, Jigar, Kulyah, Jild, Rehm)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Whatever ought to be evacuated must be evacuated timely, and whatever ought to be retained must be retained."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Murshid", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "Constipation is the mother of twenty chronic diseases."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Renal filtration, Colonic motility, Phase I/II hepatic xenobiotic biotransformation, and Lymphatic drainage.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Bristol Stool Chart (Type 3–4 normal), Glomerular Filtration Rate, and daily perspiration index.", "actionable_protocols": "Daily spontaneous bowel movement; natural prebiotic soluble fiber; therapeutic sweating (Hammam) when indicated.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Never induce aggressive evacuation in dehydrated or hemodynamically compromised patients.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-05", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Istifragh-o-Ihtibas: Evacuation, Retention & Detox Pathways", "chapter_title_ur": "استفراغ و احتباس — سموم کا قدرتی اخراج، بائیو ٹرانسفارمیشن اور احتباس", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Istifragh (Evacuation of Morbid Matter) and Ihtibas (Retention of Essential Matter) represent the dynamic excretory-retentive equilibrium of human survival. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina establishes: 'الاستفراغ هو إخراج الفضول الرديئة من البدن، والاحتباس هو حبس ما يحتاج إليه البدن من الرطوبات والأخلاط' (Istifragh is the elimination of morbid surplus matter from the body, while Ihtibas is the retention of vital humors and moistures required for somatic integrity).\nDisease arises from two fundamental errors:\n1. Ihtibas-e-Fuzlaat (Retention of Toxins): Stagnation of substances that should be naturally expelled (constipation, oliguria, amenorrhea, suppressed perspiration). This leads to auto-intoxication, putrefaction, and systemic inflammation.\n2. Istifragh-e-Zarooriyyat (Depletion of Vital Fluids): Excessive abnormal loss of substances needed by the body (hemorrhage, chronic diarrhea, excessive nocturnal seminal emissions, hyperhidrosis). This leads to acute collapse of innate heat (Zof-e-Qalb and Buhran).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern molecular toxicology and renal-hepatic elimination correlate:\n- Istifragh corresponds to Phase I (CYP450 functionalization) and Phase II (glucuronidation, sulfation, glutathione conjugation) hepatic detoxification, followed by Phase III transporter-mediated biliary and urinary excretion (P-glycoprotein, MRP2).\n- Ihtibas-e-Fuzlaat corresponds to intestinal dysbiosis, bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugation of biliary toxins leading to enterohepatic recirculation, elevated serum uremic toxins (indoxyl sulfate, p-cresyl sulfate), and lymphatic stasis.\n- Istifragh-e-Zarooriyyat corresponds to electrolyte collapse (hypokalemia, hyponatremia), hypovolemic hemodynamic shock, and systemic oncotic collapse due to hypoalbuminemia.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Gastrointestinal Elimination Audit: Evaluate daily bowel evacuation frequency, completeness of evacuation, and presence of tenesmus.\n2. Renal & Diaphoretic Assessment: Measure 24-hour urine output volume, specific gravity, and history of peripheral dependent edema.\n3. Menstrual & Repro-endocrine History (in females): Document cycle regularity, duration, volume, and color of menstrual discharge (evaluating Ihtibas-e-Tams).\n4. Skin & Sudoriferous Function: Inspect skin for compensatory uremic or bilious eruptions (pruritus, yellowish sweat staining).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Evacuative Decoction: Joshanda Munzij-o-Mushil-e-Balgham\n- Indication: Viscid phlegmatic toxin retention (Ihtibas-e-Balgham Ghaleez), chronic joint effusions, metabolic sluggishness.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Ustukhuddus (Lavandula stoechas flower heads) — 10g\n  2. Badyan (Foeniculum vulgare seeds) — 10g\n  3. Beekh-e-Kasni (Cichorium intybus root) — 10g\n  4. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena) — 10g\n  5. Sana-e-Makki (Cassia angustifolia leaves, depetiolated) — 7g\n  6. Munaqqa (Vitis vinifera seeds removed) — 9 pieces\n- Preparation: Boil in 500ml water until reduced to 150ml. Filter through linen. Administer lukewarm on an empty stomach.\n- Safety: Sana-e-Makki must be used with corrective (Musleh: Gul-e-Surkh and Roghan-e-Badam) to prevent abdominal griping (Mughas).", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 49-year-old female with chronic obstipation (bowel movement once every 4-5 days, hard dry scybalous stools), severe abdominal distension, recurrent cystic acne, generalized pruritus, serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL, BUN 22 mg/dL.\nAssessment: Ihtibas-e-Fuzlaat Saudawi with secondary autointoxication.\nProtocol: 7 days gentle hydration with Ma-ul-Sha\'eer (barley water) + Roghan-e-Badam Shireen 15ml at bedtime. Followed by Joshanda Munzij-o-Mushil.\nOutcome (Day 14): Daily spontaneous non-straining soft bowel movements established; cystic acne cleared; pruritus abolished; BUN normalized to 13 mg/dL."}}, {"id": "ch-02-06", "chapter_number": "2.6", "title_en": "Harkat-o-Sukoon-e-Nafsani: Psychic Equilibrium & Stress Modulation", "title_ur": "حرکت و سکونِ نفسانی — ذہنی دباؤ، جذبات اور خود کار اعصابی توازن", "key_concepts": ["Infi'alat-e-Nafsaniyyah (Psychic Passions: Anger, Joy, Fear, Grief, Anxiety)", "Harkat-e-Rooh (Rapid expansion or sudden contraction of vital pneuma)", "Rooh-e-Nafsani (Psychic Pneuma / Neurochemical Neurotransmission)", "Sukun-e-Qalb (Heart-Brain Emotional Equilibrium)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Sudden violent anger heats the body intensely, whereas sudden terror chills the innate heat and contracts the pneuma to the core."}, {"book": "Tibb-e-Akbar", "author": "Hakim Arzani", "text": "Chronic grief and worry cause severe burning of black bile (Sauda-e-Ehtraqiyya)."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis, Vagal nerve tone, Cortisol-to-DHEA ratio, and Heart Rate Variability (HRV).", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "HRV SDNN / RMSSD metrics, Perceived Stress Scale (PSS-10), and salivary cortisol awakening response.", "actionable_protocols": "Mindfulness (Muraqaba), calming herbal infusions (Arq Gaozaban, Chamomile), and vagal stimulation techniques.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Screen immediately for clinical depressive suicidality; acute psychiatric crises require specialized dual care.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-02-06", "volume_id": "vol-02", "chapter_title_en": "Harkat-o-Sukoon-e-Nafsani: Psychic Equilibrium & Stress Modulation", "chapter_title_ur": "حرکت و سکونِ نفسانی — ذہنی دباؤ، قلبی سکون اور ویگس نرو کا ربط", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Psychic movements (Harkat-o-Sukoon-e-Nafsani) exert the most rapid and instantaneous systemic physiological effects of all Asbab-e-Sitta. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina writes: 'الانفعالات النفسانية قد تسخن البدن تسخينا شديدا، وقد تبرده تبريدا مفرطا' (Psychic emotional movements may heat the body intensely, or cool it profoundly and excessively).\nEmotional movements are categorized into four dynamic thermodynamic shifts:\n1. Sudden External Movement of Heat: Acute anger (Ghadab) causes the innate heat and blood to surge violently outward, flushing the face and accelerating the pulse.\n2. Sudden Internal Movement of Heat: Terror or acute dread (Khauf) causes heat and blood to retreat inward instantly, leaving the skin pale, cold, and hypotensive.\n3. Gradual Outward Movement of Heat: Joy and delight (Farah) gently expand the vital spirit outward, brightening the eyes and relaxing the heart.\n4. Gradual Internal Dissipation of Heat: Chronic grief and despair (Huzn) slowly smother the innate heat, producing cold-dry melancholic atrophy (Sauda).\nMaintaining emotional equanimity through contemplation (Muraqaba) and ethical character refinement (Tahzeeb-ul-Akhlaq) is essential for physical health.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern psychoneuroimmunology and autonomic neuroscience correlate:\n- Ghadab (Anger) maps to acute sympathetic-adrenomedullary (SAM) activation, massive catecholamine discharge (epinephrine, norepinephrine), beta-1 adrenergic tachycardia, systemic vasoconstriction, and acute arterial shear stress.\n- Huzn (Chronic Grief/Depression) maps to chronic hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis dysregulation, glucocorticoid receptor resistance, elevated circulating cortisol, hippocampal neurogenesis suppression, and elevated pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha, hs-CRP).\n- Farah (Joy) and Muraqaba (Mindfulness/Meditation) stimulate efferent vagal nerve firing, increase heart rate variability (HRV), promote endogenous endorphin, oxytocin, and anandamide synthesis, and downregulate NF-kB transcription.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Psychosomatic Symptom Audit: Screen for tension headaches, globus pharyngeus, bruxism, and sighing dyspnea (Tanaffus-e-Azeem).\n2. Heart Rate Variability (HRV) Biomarker: Measure 5-minute resting ECG rhythm strip or photoplethysmography to calculate rMSSD (target >35ms) and LF/HF sympathovagal ratio (target 1.0-2.0).\n3. Salivary Morning Cortisol Awakening Response (CAR): Measure cortisol at awakening and +30 minutes (blunted or hyperactive slope indicates HPA axis exhaustion).\n4. Neuromuscular Tone Inspection: Palpate upper trapezius, levator scapulae, and masseter muscles for hypertonic tender points.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Neuro-Psychic Electuary: Khamira Gaozaban Ambari Jadwar Ood Saleeb Wala\n- Indication: Waswas (anxiety neurosis), melancholic depression (Malikhooliya), cardiac palpitations (Khafqan), autonomic hyper-reactivity.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Barg-e-Gaozaban (Borago officinalis leaves) — 30g\n  2. Gul-e-Gaozaban (Borago officinalis flowers) — 20g\n  3. Jadwar Khatai (Delphinium denudatum root) — 10g\n  4. Ood Saleeb (Paeonia emodi root) — 10g\n  5. Yashab Sabz Saeeda (Levigated Jade stone powder) — 5g\n  6. Marwareed Saeeda (Levigated Pearl powder) — 5g\n  7. Zafran (Crocus sativus) — 3g\n  8. Ambar (Ambergris substitute) — 1g\n  9. Shakar Safaid — 500g\n- Method: Prepare aqueous decoction of Gaozaban. Boil with sugar to two-thread consistency. Whip vigorously (Khamira process) until aerated and milky white. Fold in levigated gemstones, pearls, and aromatics.\n- Dosage: 5g early morning fasting on an empty stomach.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 31-year-old female lawyer presenting with generalized anxiety disorder, panic attacks with palpitations (pulse 110 bpm), chronic insomnia, globus sensation, HRV rMSSD 12ms, salivary morning cortisol 28 ng/mL.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Dimagh-o-Qalb Har secondary to excessive Harkat-e-Nafsaniyyah.\nProtocol: Khamira Gaozaban Ambari 5g morning fasting + Arq Gulab 50ml. Daily 20-minute twilight Muraqaba (breath awareness meditation). Shamoom (olfactory aromatherapy with Rosa damascena and Santalum album).\nOutcome (Day 30): Panic attacks completely ceased; resting heart rate stabilized at 68 bpm; HRV rMSSD improved from 12ms to 42ms; salivary cortisol normalized to 14 ng/mL; return to active courtroom duties."}}]}, {"id": "vol-03", "volume_number": 3, "title_en": "Classical Diagnostics & Bedside Examination", "title_ur": "تشخیص و معاینۂ سريري — نبض، قارورہ اور براز", "title_ar": "التشخيص الكلاسيكي و الفحص السريري", "icon": "🩺", "badge": "Clinical Semiology", "summary": "The master art of non-invasive bedside semiology: pulse waveform dynamics, urine sediment topography, stool analysis, and cranial facial diagnostics.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-03-01", "chapter_number": "3.1", "title_en": "Ilm-un-Nabz: The 10 Classical Ajnas Pulse Evaluation", "title_ur": "علم النبض — دس اجناسِ نبض اور جدید پلس ویو", "key_concepts": ["Miqdar (Volume/Amplitude: Taweel, Areez, Azeem)", "Harkat (Velocity/Rate: Saree, Bati, Mo'tadil)", "Quwwat (Pulse Strength: Qawi, Zaeef)", "Ghalzat (Arterial Wall Elasticity: Layyin, Sulb)", "Imtila (Fullness: Mumtali, Khaali)", "Zaman-e-Sukoon (Interval: Mutawater, Mutafawit)", "Istiwah (Regularity: Mustawi, Mukhtalif)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Kitab un-Nabz)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Pulse is the movement of heart and arteries consisting of alternating expansion and contraction to cool the innate heat."}, {"book": "Risala-e-Nabz", "author": "Hakim Kabiruddin", "text": "The radial artery at the styloid process is the universal mirror of visceral health."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Photoplethysmography (PPG), Pulse Wave Velocity (PWV), Arterial Stiffness Index, and Radial artery pressure waveform dicrotic notch.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Radial palpation with four fingertips in quiet morning state, resting HR, and PWV < 10 m/s normal.", "actionable_protocols": "Identify Su-e-Mizaj from waveform: Saree Qawi (Hot/Inflammatory), Bati Layyin (Cold/Phlegmatic), Sulb (Atherosclerosis).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Do not assess pulse immediately post-exercise, bathing, or profound emotional turmoil.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-03-01", "volume_id": "vol-03", "chapter_title_en": "Ilm-un-Nabz: The 10 Classical Ajnas Pulse Waveform Evaluation", "chapter_title_ur": "علم النبض — دس اجناسِ نبض، عروقی لہریں اور دل کی کارکردگی", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ilm-un-Nabz (The Science of Sphygmology) is the quintessential diagnostic art of Greco-Arab medicine. Ibn Sina dedicates extensive treatises in 'Al-Qanoon' to Nabz, defining it as: 'النبض حركة في الأوعية الدموية مؤلفة من انقباض وانبساط، لترويح القلب بالنسيم وإخراج الفضول الدخانية' (The pulse is a movement in the blood vessels composed of expansion and contraction, designed to cool the heart with fresh air and expel smoky metabolic residues).\nThe pulse is evaluated through Ten Universal Genera (Al-Ajnas al-Ashara li-Ahwal-in-Nabz):\n1. Miqdar-e-Inbisat (Dimension/Volume): Length (Taweel/Qaseer), Breadth (Areez/Zayyiq), Height (Mushrif/Munkhafiz). When all 3 are large = Nabz-e-Azeem (Large); when all 3 are small = Nabz-e-Saghir (Small).\n2. Kayfiyyat-e-Qar'a (Tactile Impact/Force): Strong (Qawi) vs Weak (Zaeef).\n3. Zaman-e-Harakat (Velocity of Beat): Rapid (Saree) vs Slow (Batee).\n4. Qiwam-e-Aala (Vascular Compressibility/Elasticity): Hard/Rigid (Sulb) vs Soft/Yielding (Layyin).\n5. Khulu-o-Imtila (Fullness of Vessel): Full (Mumtali) vs Empty (Khali).\n6. Malmas (Temperature of Vessel): Hot (Har) vs Cold (Barid).\n7. Zaman-e-Sukoon (Rest Interval): Frequent (Mutawatar) vs Infrequent (Mutafawit).\n8. Istiwa-o-Ikhtilaf (Regularity/Rhythm): Regular (Mustawi) vs Irregular (Mukhtalif).\n9. Wazan-e-Harakat (Metrical Weight/Harmony): Concordant (Jayyid-ul-Wazan) vs Discordant.\n10. Nizam (Pattern Consistency): Orderly irregular (Muntazam) vs Disorderly irregular (Ghair-Muntazam).\nCompound pulses include Nabz-e-Ghazali (Dicrotic), Nabz-e-Moushari (Saw-like), and Nabz-e-Doodi (Vermicular/Thready).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern arterial hemodynamics, vascular physics, and pulse wave analysis (PWA) correlate directly:\n- Miqdar (Dimension) corresponds to stroke volume (SV) and pulse pressure (PP = SBP - DBP).\n- Kayfiyyat-e-Qar'a (Force) corresponds to left ventricular contractility (dP/dt max) and cardiac output.\n- Zaman-e-Harakat (Velocity) corresponds to heart rate and early systolic ejection velocity.\n- Qiwam (Hardness) corresponds to arterial wall compliance, pulse wave velocity (PWV), and systemic vascular resistance (SVR) governed by collagen-elastin remodeling and endothelial nitric oxide availability.\n- Zaman-e-Sukoon corresponds to autonomic sympathovagal balance measured via Heart Rate Variability (HRV rMSSD and high-frequency power).\n- Nabz-e-Ghazali (Dicrotic pulse) corresponds to elevated wave reflection and arterial resonance seen in febrile hyperdynamic states; Nabz-e-Moushari corresponds to severe aortic regurgitation or advanced atherosclerosis with high augmentation index (AIx).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Patient Preparation: Patient must be resting quietly for 10-15 minutes in a temperate room, having avoided recent meals, exertion, or intense emotions.\n2. Arm & Hand Position: Patient's forearm rests comfortably at heart level, palm facing upward, wrist slightly flexed.\n3. Examiner's Finger Placement: Place four fingers (index, middle, ring, little) along the radial artery over the styloid process. The index finger rests nearest the wrist (evaluating brain/cardiac influence); the little finger rests proximally (evaluating renal/pelvic influence).\n4. Graded Pressure Depths:\n   - Superficial Touch (Lams-e-Sathi): Evaluates peripheral skin temperature and immediate impact.\n   - Moderate Pressure (Fashr-e-Motadil): Evaluates lumen width, fullness, and stroke volume.\n   - Deep Pressure to Bone (Fashr-e-Qawi): Evaluates arterial wall elasticity, resistance to compression, and underlying cardiac reserve.\n5. Cycle Count: Palpate at least 30 to 50 continuous cycles to accurately assess Istiwa (regularity) and Zaman-e-Sukoon (inter-beat intervals).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Pulse Harmonizer: Khamira Marwareed Khas\n- Indication: Nabz-e-Zaeef, Saghir, Saqit (Weak, thread-like pulse in vital cardiac collapse), anxiety, palpitations.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Marwareed Saeeda (Levigated natural pearls) — 10g\n  2. Yashab Sabz Saeeda (Levigated green jade) — 5g\n  3. Kahruba Shamai (Vateria indica resin) — 5g\n  4. Shakh-e-Marjan (Levigated red coral / Busud) — 5g\n  5. Ambar Ashhab — 1g\n  6. Zafran (Crocus sativus) — 3g\n  7. Arq Gulab (Triple distilled rose water) — 100ml\n  8. Qand Safaid — 300g\n- Compounding: Levigate pearls and minerals in rose water using agate mortar for 72 hours until micro-particulate. Cook sugar syrup, cool, incorporate mineral micro-suspension, whip to white Khamira matrix.\n- Dosing: 3g to 5g with Arq Gaozaban 60ml.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 62-year-old male with chronic hypertensive heart disease presenting with fatigue, exertional breathlessness, bounding high-amplitude pulse with rigid vessel wall.\nPulse Examination: Nabz-e-Sulb (Hard, non-compressible), Azeem (Large), Mutawatar (Frequent), with delayed dicrotic notch. Arterial PWV: 11.8 m/s (elevated vascular stiffness).\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Har-Yabis of the arterial tree with loss of vascular compliance and secondary ventricular strain.\nProtocol: Khamira Marwareed 3g morning + Arq Badiyan 50ml + dietary sodium restriction and daily garlic maceration (Seer Mudabbar).\nOutcome (Day 45): Pulse softened significantly on deep compression (Nabz-e-Layyin shift); radial arterial compliance improved; PWV reduced from 11.8 to 8.9 m/s; systolic BP stabilized from 165 to 128 mmHg."}}, {"id": "ch-03-02", "chapter_number": "3.2", "title_en": "Qaroorah: Urine Examination & Sediment Topography", "title_ur": "قارورہ — پیشاب کا تجزیہ، رنگ، قوام اور رسوب", "key_concepts": ["Lawn (Color: Abyaz/White, Asfar/Yellow, Ahmar/Red, Aswad/Black)", "Qawam (Consistency: Raqeeq/Thin, Ghaleez/Thick, Mo'tadil)", "Burooq & Reeh (Transparency, Foam, and Odor)", "Rusub (Sediment Stratification: Sifli/Bottom, Wasati/Middle, Fawqi/Floating)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Tashkhees-e-Baul)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "The urine reflects the state of the liver, blood vessels, and kidneys; inspection must occur in early morning light."}, {"book": "Al-Hawi fit-Tibb", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "White sediment in yellow urine indicates resolution of hot inflammatory disease (Nuzj)."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Urine Specific Gravity (1.005–1.030), Microscopic Urinalysis (RBCs, WBCs, Casts, Crystals), Proteinuria, and Urobilinogen.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "First morning mid-stream specimen in clear glass container without refrigeration or light degradation.", "actionable_protocols": "Differentiate Nuzj (metabolic resolution) from crude unconcocted humors to time therapeutic evacuation.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Gross hematuria or tea-colored urine requires immediate microscopic investigation to rule out glomerular nephritis or malignancy.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-03-02", "volume_id": "vol-03", "chapter_title_en": "Qaroorah: Urine Examination & Sediment Topography", "chapter_title_ur": "قارورہ — پیشاب کا تجزیہ، تلچھٹ کی تہیں اور نظامِ اخراج", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Qaroorah (Classical Uroscopy) is the second pillar of bedside objective semiology. Ibn Sina notes in 'Al-Qanoon': 'القارورة دليل صادق على أحوال الكبد ومجاري البول والعروق' (The urine vial is a faithful witness to the functional state of the liver, urinary tracts, and systemic blood vessels).\nThe diagnostic evaluation of Qaroorah is conducted through Seven Universal Parameters (Al-Dalail al-Sab'ah):\n1. Laun (Color): From transparent clear (Ma'i) to pale straw (Tabni), deep golden (Atari), bright saffron (Zafrani), flame red (Ahmar Nari), dark tea (Aswad). Pale/clear indicates coldness or renal failure to concentrate; fiery red/saffron indicates acute Hararat or Safra; dark/black indicates burnt Sauda or deep necrosis.\n2. Qiwam (Consistency/Density): Thin (Raqeeq), Moderate (Motadil), Thick (Ghaleez). Thin urine indicates raw immature humor (Balgham Kham); thick urine indicates active metabolic waste discharge.\n3. Rayiha (Odor): Acrid pungent odor indicates active Hararat and putrefaction (Ufoonat); absence of odor indicates profound coldness (Barudat-e-Mufrita).\n4. Zabad (Foam/Bubbles): Abundant, persistent, yellowish bubbles indicate trapped visceral air and bilious irritation; persistent white frothy foam indicates protein leak.\n5. Rasib (Sediment/Precipitate): The nature and position of the sediment:\n   - Rasib-e-Mahmood (Normal healthy sediment): White, smooth, uniform, light, settling neatly at the bottom (settled pyramid).\n   - Rasib-e-Ghair-Mahmood (Pathological sediment): Grainy like sand (Hasat/gravel), flaky like bran (Khushkarah), or floating mid-vial (Hawa'i).\n6. Kammiyyat (Volume): Oliguria vs Polyuria.\n7. Safa wa Kudoorat (Clarity vs Turbidity).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern nephrology and clinical urinalysis correlate:\n- Laun (Color) corresponds to urochrome pigment excretion, bilirubinuria/urobilinogen (hepatobiliary obstruction), macroscopic hematuria (RBCs), or myoglobinuria.\n- Qiwam (Density) corresponds to urine specific gravity (1.005-1.030) and urine osmolality (50-1200 mOsm/kg H2O) governed by medullary concentration gradients and ADH.\n- Zabad (Persistent foam) correlates with microalbuminuria, proteinuria (>300 mg/24h), and conjugated bile salts lowering surface tension.\n- Rasib (Sediment) corresponds to microscopic urinary sediments: calcium oxalate, uric acid crystals, hyaline/granular casts, dysmorphic erythrocytes, pyuria (leukocytes), and squamous epithelial cells.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Specimen Collection Protocol: First morning mid-stream specimen collected in a clean, transparent, cylindrical glass vial (Qaroorah). The patient must not have taken coloring agents, saffron, or beetroot the prior evening.\n2. Settling Period: Allow the specimen to rest undisturbed at ambient room temperature for 1 hour to permit sedimentation layers to stratify.\n3. Lighting & Background: Inspect against a pure white background under natural indirect daylight.\n4. Quad-Zonal Topographical Stratification:\n   - Zone 1: Surface meniscus (inspect for oil droplet sheen / floating lipids).\n   - Zone 2: Upper quadrant (inspect for cloudiness / Ghamaam).\n   - Zone 3: Middle suspension (inspect for suspended nebulae / Sahab).\n   - Zone 4: Bottom sediment layer (inspect color, texture, and compactness of Rasib).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Lithotriptic & Diuretic: Kushta-e-Hajar-ul-Yahood & Sharbat-e-Bazoori\n- Indication: Sandy sediment (Rasib Ramli), nephrolithiasis, burning dysuria, Safrawi oliguria.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Hajar-ul-Yahood (Fossilized sea-urchin spine, Lapis judaicus) — 50g\n  2. Aab-e-Mooli (Fresh Raphanus sativus radish juice) — 200ml\n- Calcination SOP: Triturate pulverized stone in radish juice for 12 hours until paste is formed. Form small discs, dry under shade, seal in clay-luted saucers (Kozah), calcinate in muffle furnace at 600°C for 4 hours.\n- Vehicle: Administer 250mg Kushta with 25ml Sharbat-e-Bazoori Motadil twice daily.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 47-year-old male with recurrent right flank colic, burning micturition, concentrated dark amber urine.\nQaroorah Inspection: Saffron-red color, pungent acrid odor, dense granular brick-dust sediment at vial bottom (Rasib Ramli). Urinalysis confirmed microhematuria and abundant calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals.\nAssessment: Hasat-e-Kulyah (Nephrolithiasis) with Su-e-Mizaj Har-Yabis of the urinary tracts.\nProtocol: Kushta Hajar-ul-Yahood 250mg BID + Sharbat-e-Bazoori Motadil 30ml BID + 3L daily water infused with Zeera Safaid and Khas.\nOutcome (Day 10): Patient passed a 4mm calculus spontaneously without surgical intervention; follow-up Qaroorah cleared to pale straw with smooth white physiological sediment; flank colic abolished."}}, {"id": "ch-03-03", "chapter_number": "3.3", "title_en": "Baraaz & Lisaan: Stool Transit & Tongue Topography", "title_ur": "براز اور لسان — آنتوں کا فعل اور زبان کی تشخیص", "key_concepts": ["Baraaz (Color, Odor, Transit speed, Gas entrapment)", "Lisaan (Tongue Body color: Pink, Red, Pale, Purplish)", "Sabard-e-Lisaan (Tongue Coating: White-greasy = Balgham, Yellow-dry = Safra, Blackish = Sauda)", "Organ Topography: Tip (Heart), Center (Stomach/Spleen), Sides (Liver/Gallbladder), Root (Kidneys/Intestines)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "The tongue is the external indicator of the stomach mucosa and internal innate heat."}, {"book": "Tibb-e-Akbar", "author": "Hakim Arzani", "text": "Yellowish dry tongue with bitter taste confirms Safrawi gastritis."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Oral microbiome dysbiosis, Vitamin B12/Iron deficiency atrophic glossitis, and Bristol Stool Chart transit kinetics.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Visual daylight inspection of tongue without prior brushing, scrape, or colored food ingestion.", "actionable_protocols": "Direct diet and digestive tonics (Jawarish Kamuni, Jawarish Jalinoos) based on tongue moisture and coating thickness.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Persistent unilateral tongue ulceration requires immediate biopsy referral to rule out squamous cell carcinoma.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-03-03", "volume_id": "vol-03", "chapter_title_en": "Baraaz & Lisaan: Stool Transit & Tongue Topography", "chapter_title_ur": "براز اور لسان — زبان اور فضلے کی تشخیص، گٹ مائیکرو بایوٹا اور اعصابی رابطہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Baraaz (Stool Examination) and Lisaan (Tongue Inspection) represent the two direct visual and tactile windows into the gastrointestinal mucosal continuum.\nIbn Sina notes in 'Al-Qanoon' that stool characteristics reveal the functional integrity of Hazm-e-Awwal (stomach), Hazm-e-Sani (liver), and Quwwat-e-Masika vs Dafi'a in the intestines.\n1. Baraaz (Fecal Semiology):\n   - Color: Normal is amber-yellow (Safrawi tint). White/clay-colored indicates biliary obstruction (Ihtibas-e-Safra); black indicates upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage or burnt Sauda; bright green indicates rapid transit with unreduced biliverdin.\n   - Odor: Extreme putrefactive stench indicates necrotic fermentation (Ufoonat); absence of odor indicates cold digestive failure.\n   - Form & Consistency: Compact sausage = balanced digestion; watery liquid = premature expulsion; hard dry balls = excessive intestinal heat and dehydration.\n2. Lisaan (Tongue Topography):\n   - The tongue is the mirror of the internal mucosa. It reflects systemic hydration, humoral balance, and visceral organ health. The tip reflects the Heart (Qalb), center reflects the Stomach/Spleen (Mida), lateral edges reflect the Liver/Gallbladder (Kabid), and root reflects the Kidneys/Intestines.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern gastroenterology and digital diagnostics correlate:\n- Stool Form correlates with the Bristol Stool Chart (Types 1-7), colonic transit time, and short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) concentrations.\n- Fecal color changes correspond to stercobilinogen conversion, upper GI bleeding (acid hematin), or pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (steatorrhea).\n- Tongue Coat (Saburral Fur) corresponds to filiform papillae keratinization, exfoliated epithelial cells, oral microbiome bacterial biofilm (Streptococcus, Prevotella, Fusobacterium), and systemic endotoxemia triggering salivary mucin changes.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Tongue Inspection Protocol: Inspect natural tongue without prior scraping, coffee, or food consumption for at least 1 hour. Patient protrudes tongue gently without excessive force.\n2. Topographical Mapping:\n   - Body Color: Pale pink (normal), pale flabby (Balgham), deep crimson (Dam), purplish dusky (Sauda/stasis).\n   - Coating (Zubala): Assess thickness, distribution, moisture, and color (white, yellow, brown, black).\n   - Morphology: Check for lateral teeth indentations (indicates fluid retention/lingual edema), central fissures (gastric yin/moisture depletion), or tremors (cardiac-neurological debility).\n3. Stool Audit: Cross-reference patient's daily evacuation with the Bristol Stool Form scale and fecal occult blood testing (FOBT).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Intestinal Restorative: Jawarish Mastagi\n- Indication: Gastric and intestinal flaccidity (Istirkha-e-Mida-o-Ama), chronic loose Balghami stool, white tongue coating.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Mastagi Roomi (Pistacia lentiscus resin) — 50g\n  2. Asal-e-Khalis (Purified honey) — 250g\n  3. Arq Gulab — 20ml\n- Compounding: Melt purified mastic resin over low heat in rose water; blend into warmed honey.\n- Dosing: 5g taken 30 minutes before meals.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 34-year-old female with chronic alternating diarrhea and constipation (IBS), profound fatigue, thick greasy white tongue coating with scalloped borders. Stool: Bristol Type 6 alternating with Type 1.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Mida-o-Ama Barid-Ratab with mucosal lymph stasis.\nProtocol: Jawarish Mastagi 5g before meals + Arq Badiyan 40ml + warm chamomile tea. Elimination of cold dairy and raw salads.\nOutcome (Day 28): Tongue coating cleared completely to healthy pink; bowel movements normalized to once daily Bristol Type 4; fatigue resolved."}}]}, {"id": "vol-04", "volume_number": 4, "title_en": "Clinical Laboratory Biomarkers & Modern Pathology", "title_ur": "جدید لیب ٹیسٹ، بائیو مارکرز اور پیتھولوجی", "title_ar": "التحاليل المخبرية السريرية و علم الأمراض الحديث", "icon": "🧪", "badge": "Biomedical Precision", "summary": "Bridging modern clinical hematology, biochemistry, and endocrinology with classical humoral dyscrasias, organ temperaments, and inflammation cascades.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-04-01", "chapter_number": "4.1", "title_en": "Inflammatory Biomarkers: hs-CRP, ESR & Cytokines", "title_ur": "سوزش کے مارکرز — سی آر پی، ای ایس آر اور سائیٹوکائنز", "key_concepts": ["High-Sensitivity C-Reactive Protein (hs-CRP < 1.0 mg/L Optimal)", "Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR Westergren)", "Serum Ferritin as an Acute Phase Reactant", "Humoral Translation: Hararat-e-Fasida, Safrawi Blood Fire & Waram"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Awram)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Inflammation (Waram) begins with vascular engorgement, followed by heat, pain, and humoral effusion."}, {"book": "Kamil al-Sina'a", "author": "Al-Majusi", "text": "Boiling of blood (Ghalayan-e-Dam) produces febrile heat and tissue breakdown."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Interleukin-6 (IL-6), TNF-alpha, hepatic acute-phase reactant synthesis, and endothelial activation.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "hs-CRP > 3.0 mg/L indicates high cardiovascular/systemic inflammatory risk; > 10.0 mg/L indicates acute systemic inflammation.", "actionable_protocols": "Anti-inflammatory botanical protocol: Majun Suranjan, Hab-e-Gugal, Arq-e-Kasni; monitor 28-day reduction trajectory.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Differentiate autoimmune elevation from acute bacterial sepsis (Procalcitonin > 0.5 mcg/L requires emergency antibiotics).", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-04-01", "volume_id": "vol-04", "chapter_title_en": "Inflammatory Biomarkers: hs-CRP, ESR & Cytokines", "chapter_title_ur": "سوزش کے مارکرز — سی آر پی، ای ایس آر اور سائیٹوکائنز کا اخلاطی موازنہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Systemic inflammation in Unani medicine is conceptualized under the doctrines of Waram (Swelling/Inflammation) and Ufoonat (Humoral Putrefaction). In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that when a humor stagnates and innate heat fails to mature it properly, the humor becomes morbidly heated by alien heat (Hararat-e-Gharibah), precipitating tissue infiltration and pain.\nWaram is categorized into four humoral types:\n1. Waram Damawi (Phlegmon): Warm, red, throbbing, soft (Dam accumulation).\n2. Waram Safrawi (Erysipelas / Hamrah): Fiery hot, yellowish-red, severe burning pain, rapid onset (Safra combustion).\n3. Waram Balghami (Edema / Rakhawah): Cold, pale, soft, pitting, non-painful (Balgham effusion).\n4. Waram Saudawi (Scirrhus / Salabat): Hard, nodular, painless or dull ache, dark color, indolent (Sauda induration).\nTracking the resolution of Waram requires objective monitoring of humoral purification.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern molecular immunology and acute-phase biochemistry correlate:\n- hs-CRP (High-sensitivity C-reactive protein): Pentameric acute-phase protein synthesized by hepatocytes under IL-6 transcriptional stimulation. Reflects low-grade vascular endothelial inflammation (<1.0 mg/L = low risk; 1.0-3.0 mg/L = intermediate; >3.0 mg/L = high cardiovascular/inflammatory risk).\n- ESR (Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate): Governed by plasma fibrinogen and asymmetric globulin concentrations neutralizing negative erythrocyte zeta potentials, promoting rouleaux formation. Directly mirrors humoral blood viscosity and systemic inflammation.\n- Cytokine Cascades: Interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta), Interleukin-6 (IL-6), and Tumor Necrosis Factor-alpha (TNF-alpha) drive systemic fever, hepatic acute-phase response, and catabolic cachexia.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Cardinal Signs of Waram: Palpate and inspect the five classical signs: Rubor (Humrah/redness), Tumor (Waram/swelling), Calor (Hararat/heat), Dolor (Waja/pain), and Functio Laesa (Nuqsan-e-Fail/loss of function).\n2. Laboratory Phlebotomy Protocol: Draw morning fasting venous sample for hs-CRP (latex-enhanced immunoturbidimetry) and ESR (Westergren automated method).\n3. Complete Blood Count (CBC) with Differential: Evaluate absolute neutrophil count (bacterial/Damawi plethora), lymphocyte-to-monocyte ratio, and eosinophil percentage.\n4. Serial Response Trajectory: Re-test biomarkers at 14-day intervals to confirm therapeutic down-titration.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Anti-Inflammatory Electuary: Majun Usba (Sarsaparilla Complex)\n- Indication: Chronic systemic inflammation, inflammatory arthritis, dermatological inflammatory eruptions.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Ushba Maghrabi (Smilax aristolochiifolia root) — 40g\n  2. Chobchini (Smilax china rhizome) — 40g\n  3. Shahtara (Fumaria parviflora herb) — 20g\n  4. Chiraita Shireen (Swertia chirata) — 15g\n  5. Sana-e-Makki — 15g\n  6. Asal-e-Khalis — 400g\n- Compounding: Micro-pulverize dried roots, incorporate into clarified honey syrup at 40°C.\n- Dosing: 5g twice daily with lukewarm water.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 54-year-old male with active bilateral knee osteoarthritis, morning stiffness >45 minutes, severe nocturnal burning pain, baseline hs-CRP 6.4 mg/L, ESR 42 mm/hr.\nAssessment: Waram-e-Mafasil Safrawi-Damawi with systemic micro-vascular inflammation.\nProtocol: Majun Usba 5g BID + Hab-e-Suranjan (Mudabbar) 1 tab BID + Local Takmeed (warm herbal fomentation with Babuha and Iklil-ul-Jabal).\nOutcome (Day 30): Knee pain decreased by 75%; morning stiffness reduced to <10 minutes; hs-CRP plummeted from 6.4 to 1.6 mg/L; ESR decreased from 42 to 16 mm/hr."}}, {"id": "ch-04-02", "chapter_number": "4.2", "title_en": "Cardiometabolic Panel: HbA1c, Fasting Insulin & Lipid Fractionation", "title_ur": "کارڈیو میٹابولک پینل — ایچ بی اے ون سی، انسولین اور کولیسٹرول", "key_concepts": ["Glycated Hemoglobin (HbA1c < 5.6% Optimal)", "HOMA-IR (Homeostatic Model Assessment of Insulin Resistance < 1.5)", "Apolipoprotein B (ApoB < 80 mg/dL) & Small-Dense LDL", "Humoral Translation: Su-e-Mizaj Barid Ratab, Balgham-e-Lazuq & Luzujat-e-Dam"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Ziabetus)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Ziabetus is caused by weakness of the kidneys and cold-wet hepatic dyscrasia (Zof-e-Jigar o Kulyah)."}, {"book": "Makhzan-ul-Advia", "author": "Sayyid Nooruddin", "text": "Excess phlegm clogs the vascular channels and creates sluggishness in the blood."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Pancreatic beta-cell exhaustion, hepatic de novo lipogenesis, atherogenic dyslipidemia, and vascular endothelial dysfunction.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Fasting blood glucose, fasting insulin, HbA1c, fasting lipid profile with Non-HDL and Triglyceride/HDL ratio.", "actionable_protocols": "Chrono-nutrition, Intermittent fasting, Jamun seed (Tukhm-e-Jamun), Karela, Gurmar (Gymnema), MetaboCore Unani formula.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Never abruptly discontinue allopathic hypoglycemics in patients with severe hyperglycemia (HbA1c > 9.0%) without dual-track titration.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-04-02", "volume_id": "vol-04", "chapter_title_en": "Cardiometabolic Panel: HbA1c, Fasting Insulin & Lipid Fractionation", "chapter_title_ur": "کارڈیو میٹابولک پینل — شوگر، انسولین اور کولیسٹرول کا جدید تجزیہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Metabolic syndrome and dyslipidemia correspond to the classical Unani concept of Imtila-bi-Hasb-al-Aw'iyah (Plethoric vascular engorgement) and Su-e-Mizaj Kabidi Barid (Sluggish hepatic cold dyscrasia with fat deposition).\nIn 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that when nutrition exceeds the metabolic capacity of the innate heat (Hararat-e-Ghariziyyah), the liver becomes overwhelmed. The nutrient matter cannot be transformed into noble blood, resulting in the accumulation of dense, viscous, unripened humoral fats (Shahm and Dahn) that choke the vascular channels (Sudda / Plaque). This condition, termed Ziabitus (Diabetes), reflects the exhaustion of renal retention and systemic metabolic failure.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Contemporary metabolic endocrinology correlates:\n- Fasting Insulin & HOMA-IR: Fasting hyperinsulinemia (>10 microIU/mL) precedes overt hyperglycemia by decades, signaling peripheral insulin resistance, downregulated IRS-1 signaling, and impaired GLUT4 translocation.\n- HbA1c: Reflects non-enzymatic glycation of erythrocyte hemoglobin over 90-120 days. HbA1c >5.7% denotes prediabetes; >6.5% denotes diabetes.\n- Advanced Lipid Fractionation: Standard total cholesterol is insufficient; risk is driven by Apolipoprotein B (ApoB), Small Dense LDL (sdLDL) particles, and elevated Triglycerides-to-HDL ratio (>2.0 indicates atherogenic dyslipidemia and hepatic steatosis).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Anthropometric Measurements: Measure waist circumference at the iliac crest (abnormal >90cm in Asian men, >80cm in women) and calculate Waist-to-Height Ratio (target <0.5).\n2. Acanthosis Nigricans & Skin Tag Inspection: Inspect posterior neck and axillary folds for velvety hyperpigmentation (clinical sign of severe hyperinsulinemia).\n3. Advanced Cardiometabolic Blood Panel: Draw 12-hour fasting sample for Fasting Glucose, Fasting Insulin, HbA1c, Lipid Profile, and ApoB.\n4. Calculate HOMA-IR: Formula = [Fasting Glucose (mg/dL) x Fasting Insulin (microIU/mL)] / 405. Target <1.5; >2.5 indicates significant insulin resistance.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Metabolic Cleanser: Safoof-e-Ziabitus & Arq-e-Zaitoon\n- Indication: Insulin resistance, prediabetes, atherogenic dyslipidemia, sluggish hepatic clearance.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Tukhm-e-Jamun (Syzygium cumini seed core) — 30g\n  2. Karela Khushk (Momordica charantia dried fruit) — 30g\n  3. Gumar Booti (Gymnema sylvestre leaves) — 20g\n  4. Maghz-e-Neem (Azadirachta indica seed kernels) — 10g\n  5. Asgand Nagori (Withania somnifera) — 10g\n- SOP: Micro-pulverize all dried botanical ingredients through sieve #80.\n- Dosing: 3g twice daily, 15 minutes before breakfast and dinner, washed down with 50ml Arq Badiyan.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 48-year-old male with central obesity, BMI 31.2, waist 102cm, fasting glucose 134 mg/dL, HbA1c 7.4%, fasting insulin 24.6 microIU/mL (HOMA-IR 8.1), Triglycerides 310 mg/dL, HDL 34 mg/dL.\nAssessment: Imtila-e-Balghami with severe hepatic insulin resistance and vascular congestion.\nProtocol: Safoof-e-Ziabitus 3g BID + 16:8 intermittent fasting + 45 minutes daily zone-2 brisk walking.\nOutcome (Day 60): HbA1c dropped from 7.4% to 5.9%; fasting insulin dropped to 7.8 microIU/mL (HOMA-IR 1.7); triglycerides plummeted from 310 to 142 mg/dL; body weight reduced by 7.8 kg."}}, {"id": "ch-04-03", "chapter_number": "4.3", "title_en": "Hepatorenal & Endocrine Panels: LFT, KFT, Thyroid & Cortisol", "title_ur": "جگر، گردے اور ہارمونز کا مکمل تجزیہ", "key_concepts": ["Liver Function: SGOT/AST, SGPT/ALT, Alk Phos, GGT, Serum Albumin", "Kidney Function: Serum Creatinine, eGFR (CKD-EPI), Urine Microalbumin/Creatinine Ratio", "Thyroid Axis: TSH (0.5–2.5 uIU/mL), Free T3, Free T4, Anti-TPO", "Humoral Translation: Mizaj-e-Kabid, Mizaj-e-Kulyah & Hararat-e-Ghariziyya baseline"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Amraz-e-Jigar)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "The liver is the master organ of digestion; when its heat fails, cold watery humors flood the circulation."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Hawi", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "Preserve the kidneys with demulcent waters and avoid desiccating toxins."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Hepatic transaminase leakage, Glomerular filtration integrity, Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Thyroid axis, and Adrenal steroidogenesis.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "eGFR < 60 mL/min indicates Stage 3+ CKD; ALT > 3x upper normal indicates acute hepatic injury.", "actionable_protocols": "Kasni (Cichorium intybus), Mako (Solanum nigrum) for liver; Khar-e-Khasak (Tribulus), Tukhm-e-Khas for kidneys.", "safety_and_contraindications": "In eGFR < 30 mL/min, halt all Schedule E(1) poisons (Suranjan, Kuchla) and potassium-sparing botanicals.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-04-03", "volume_id": "vol-04", "chapter_title_en": "Hepatorenal & Endocrine Panels: LFT, KFT, Thyroid & Cortisol", "chapter_title_ur": "جگر، گردے اور ہارمونز کا پینل — بائیو کیمیکل ٹیسٹس اور علاج", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The liver (Kabid) and kidneys (Kulyatain) form the central metabolic-excretory axis in Unani physiology. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina writes: 'الكبد معدن توليد الدم، والكلى مصفاة للمائيات' (The liver is the mineral source of blood generation, while the kidneys are the filter of fluid superfluities).\nHepatic weakness (Zof-e-Kabid) manifests as either Har (inflammatory hepatitis/steatohepatitis) or Barid (fatty liver/cirrhosis).\nRenal weakness (Zof-e-Kulyah) leads to fluid accumulation, hypertension, and accumulation of nitrogenous wastes.\nEndocrine harmony corresponds to the equilibrium of innate psychic and vital faculties regulated by the master hypothalamic-pituitary axis.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Clinical biochemistry and endocrine physiology correlate:\n- Liver Function Tests (LFT): Serum ALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) reflect hepatocyte plasma membrane integrity; Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) and GGT reflect biliary canalicular transit.\n- Kidney Function Tests (KFT): Serum Creatinine, Cystatin-C, and estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR via CKD-EPI equation) reflect glomerular capillary ultrafiltration capacity.\n- Thyroid Panel: TSH, Free T3, and Free T4. Free T3 is the primary driver of mitochondrial respiration and resting thermogenesis.\n- Adrenal Cortisol: Circulating cortisol follows a diurnal circadian curve; morning peak (10-20 mcg/dL) followed by evening nadir (<5 mcg/dL). Chronically elevated evening cortisol suppresses cellular immunity and induces central adiposity.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Hepatic & Splenic Palpation: Palpate liver edge along right costal margin on deep inspiration (normal is soft, sharp, non-tender; firm blunted edge denotes fatty infiltration or fibrosis).\n2. Renal Angle Tenderness: Percuss bilateral costovertebral angles (Murphy's punch test) for nephric inflammation.\n3. Thyroid Physical Inspection: Palpate thyroid isthmus and lobes during swallowing for nodularity or enlargement.\n4. Laboratory Execution: Comprehensive venipuncture for complete automated LFT, KFT with electrolytes, TSH, Free T3/T4, and 8 AM serum cortisol.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Hepatorenal Restorative: Majun Dabeed-ul-Ward & Arq Kasni\n- Indication: Hepatic congestion, elevated transaminases, subclinical renal sluggishness.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena petals) — 50g\n  2. Sumbul-ut-Teeb (Nardostachys jatamansi root) — 20g\n  3. Mastagi Roomi (Pistacia lentiscus) — 20g\n  4. Zafran (Crocus sativus) — 3g\n  5. Tabasheer (Bambusa bambos silica) — 15g\n  6. Tukhm-e-Kasni — 20g\n  7. Qand Safaid — 400g\n- Compounding: Micro-pulverize botanicals, blend into prepared sugar syrup.\n- Dosing: 5g twice daily with 50ml Arq Kasni.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 52-year-old male with metabolic steatohepatitis (MASH), ALT 74 U/L, AST 58 U/L, ultrasound showing Grade II hepatic steatosis, eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73m2, subclinical hypothyroid (TSH 5.2 mIU/L).\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Kabid Barid with portal micro-congestion and metabolic deceleration.\nProtocol: Majun Dabeed-ul-Ward 5g BID + Arq Kasni 50ml + Arq Mako 50ml. Elimination of hydrogenated oils and high-fructose corn syrup.\nOutcome (Day 45): ALT normalized to 28 U/L; AST reduced to 24 U/L; eGFR improved to 88 mL/min; repeat ultrasound showed regression to mild Grade I steatosis."}}]}, {"id": "vol-05", "volume_number": 5, "title_en": "Radio-Diagnostics & Medical Imaging", "title_ur": "ریڈیو ڈائیگنوسٹکس اور جدید امیجنگ — ایکسرے، ایم آر آئی اور الٹراساؤنڈ", "title_ar": "التشخيص الإشعاعي و التصوير الطبي", "icon": "🩻", "badge": "Structural Imaging", "summary": "Integrating modern radiological semiology (Plain Radiography, Ultrasound, MRI, FibroScan, DEXA) with classical musculoskeletal and visceral pathology.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-05-01", "chapter_number": "5.1", "title_en": "Musculoskeletal Imaging: X-Ray & MRI in Arthritis", "title_ur": "ہڈیوں اور جوڑوں کے ایکسرے اور ایم آر آئی — جوڑوں کا درد", "key_concepts": ["Kellgren-Lawrence Osteoarthritis Grading Scale (Grade I to IV)", "Joint Space Narrowing, Subchondral Sclerosis, Osteophytosis", "MRI Synovitis, Effusion & Bone Marrow Edema (BME)", "Humoral Translation: Waja-ul-Mafasil Barid Balghami, Tahajjar-e-Mafasil & Ehtibas"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Waja-ul-Mafasil)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Cold phlegm accumulates in the ligamentous spaces of joints due to weak local expulsive faculty."}, {"book": "Khazain-ul-Advia", "author": "Hakim Najmul Ghani", "text": "Suranjan penetrates into the deep articular fissures to dissolve congealed matter."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Cartilage extracellular matrix proteoglycan depletion, chondrocyte senescence, subchondral bone remodeling, and inflammatory synovitis.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Standing AP/Lateral knee radiograph: KL Grade II (Definite osteophytes, possible JSN), KL Grade III (Moderate JSN, sclerosis).", "actionable_protocols": "Dual-track protocol: Topical Roghan-e-Suranjan/Balsan + Natool fomentation + Oral Majun Suranjan + Weight decompression.", "safety_and_contraindications": "KL Grade IV (Bone-on-bone complete joint destruction) with severe angular deformity requires orthopedic surgical arthroplasty referral.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-05-01", "volume_id": "vol-05", "chapter_title_en": "Musculoskeletal Imaging: X-Ray & MRI in Arthritis", "chapter_title_ur": "ہڈیوں اور جوڑوں کے ایکسرے اور ایم آر آئی — وجع المفاصل کا امیجنگ تجزیہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Musculoskeletal disorders in Unani medicine are classified under the universal rubric of Waja-ul-Mafasil (Joint Afflictions). In 'Kitab al-Hawi', Al-Razi distinguishes between:\n1. Niqris (Gout): Acute monoarthritis primarily affecting the first metatarsophalangeal joint, driven by sharp combustible Safra and acidic matter.\n2. Irq-un-Nasa (Sciatica): Radiculopathy radiating along the sciatic nerve track, driven by viscous cold Balgham or melancholic Sauda compressing the spinal roots.\n3. Waja-ul-Rukbah (Knee Osteoarthritis): Progressive degenerative thinning of articular moisture (Ratoobat-e-Mafsal) accompanied by cold phlegmatic effusion.\nCorrelating classical signs with modern plain radiography and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) bridges macroscopic pathology with humoral diagnosis.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Radiological physics and structural pathology correlate:\n- Plain Radiography (X-Ray): Kellgren-Lawrence grading of osteoarthritis (Grade 0 to IV) evaluates:\n  1. Joint Space Narrowing (JSN): Loss of articular cartilage matrix (aggrecan and Type II collagen degradation mediated by MMP-13 and ADAMTS-5).\n  2. Subchondral Sclerosis: Increased bone density beneath damaged cartilage reflecting aberrant reparative osteoblast activity.\n  3. Osteophytosis: Marginal bony spurs driven by TGF-beta signaling.\n- Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI): Demonstrates soft-tissue pathology invisible on plain radiographs: synovial membrane hypertrophy (synovitis), subchondral bone marrow lesions (BMLs/bone bruises reflecting microfractures), joint effusions, and meniscal fibrillations/tears.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Physical Joint Inspection & Palpation: Inspect joint alignment, varus/valgus deformity, effusion bulge sign, patellar tap test, and joint line tenderness.\n2. Radiological Imaging Protocol: Obtain weight-bearing bilateral anteroposterior (AP) and lateral knee radiographs. In persistent radiculopathy, order non-contrast lumbar spine MRI.\n3. Radiological Feature Extraction: Measure minimum joint space width (mJSW in millimeters), document presence and size of osteophytes, and grade Kellgren-Lawrence status.\n4. Integrative Synthesis: Correlate joint space narrowing with classical Ratoobat-e-Mufeeda depletion, and bone marrow edema with acute inflammatory Hararat.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Anti-Arthritic Compound: Majun Suranjan & Roghan-e-Suranjan\n- Indication: Osteoarthritis, gouty arthritis, inflammatory joint effusions, joint stiffness.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Suranjan Shireen Mudabbar (Colchicum autumnale corm, detoxified in milk) — 30g\n  2. Asgand Nagori (Withania somnifera) — 20g\n  3. Badiyan (Foeniculum vulgare) — 20g\n  4. Zanjabeel (Zingiber officinale) — 15g\n  5. Sana-e-Makki — 15g\n  6. Asal-e-Khalis — 300g\n- Compounding: Detoxify Suranjan by boiling in cow's milk for 3 hours, dry, and micro-pulverize with companion herbs. Fold into warm honey.\n- Dosing: 5g twice daily with lukewarm water.\n- Topical: Apply Roghan-e-Suranjan warm over the joint, followed by hot dry flannel wrapping.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 58-year-old female with bilateral knee pain (VAS 8/10), morning stiffness 30 minutes, joint crepitus. X-Ray revealed Kellgren-Lawrence Grade III osteoarthritis with medial compartment joint space narrowing (1.8mm) and subchondral sclerosis.\nAssessment: Waja-ul-Mafasil Barid Balghami with articular cartilage depletion.\nProtocol: Majun Suranjan 5g BID + Hab-e-Guggal 1 tab BID + Roghan-e-Suranjan local massage. Regimenal: Hijama Bila Shart (dry cupping around peripatellar points) 2x/week.\nOutcome (Day 60): Pain VAS dropped from 8/10 to 2/10; walking distance extended from 100m to 1.5km without discomfort; WOMAC osteoarthritis index improved by 54%."}}, {"id": "ch-05-02", "chapter_number": "5.2", "title_en": "Abdominal Ultrasound & FibroScan: Hepatic Steatosis & Visceral Stones", "title_ur": "پیٹ کا الٹراساؤنڈ اور فائبرو اسکین — فیٹی لیور اور پتھریاں", "key_concepts": ["Hepatic Steatosis (Fatty Liver Grade 1, 2, 3 on Ultrasound)", "FibroScan Liver Stiffness Measurement (LSM in kPa) & CAP Score (dB/m)", "Cholelithiasis (Gallbladder stones) & Nephrolithiasis (Renal calculi)", "Humoral Translation: Su-e-Mizaj Jigar Barid Ratab, Salabat-e-Jigar, Hasat-e-Mararah o Kulyah"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Hasat)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Stones are produced when thick viscous humor meets intense unnatural heat that bakes it dry."}, {"book": "Tibb-e-Akbar", "author": "Hakim Arzani", "text": "Fatty infiltration of the liver causes dull heaviness in the right hypochondrium."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD / MASH), Hepatic stellate cell activation, Calcium oxalate lithogenesis, and Cholesterol supersaturation.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "LSM < 7.0 kPa normal; > 12.5 kPa indicates Advanced Fibrosis / Cirrhosis risk. Ultrasound acoustic shadowing confirms calculi.", "actionable_protocols": "Jigar: Arq Mako, Arq Kasni, Safoof Habis; Stones: Kushta Hajr-ul-Yahood, Majun Sang-e-Sar-e-Mahi, Kulthi decoction.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Impacted common bile duct stone (Choledocholithiasis) with obstructive jaundice is an absolute emergency surgical referral.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-05-02", "volume_id": "vol-05", "chapter_title_en": "Abdominal Ultrasound & FibroScan: Hepatic Steatosis & Visceral Stones", "chapter_title_ur": "پیٹ کا الٹراساؤنڈ اور فائبرو اسکین — جگر کی چربی اور پتھریوں کی تشخیص", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Visceral ultrasonography and transient elastography represent modern objective extensions of classical abdominal palpation (Lams-e-Batan) and percussion (Qar'a).\nIn 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina describes Su-e-Qinya (early hepatic dropsy/steatosis) as a condition where the liver loses its natural reddish tone and firm flesh, becoming pale, swollen, and infiltrated with fatty humor. When left unmanaged, it hardens into Salabat-e-Kabid (Hepatic Cirrhosis/Fibrosis).\nVisceral concretions—Hasat-e-Mararah (Gallstones) and Hasat-e-Kulyah (Kidney stones)—arise when burning heat desiccates viscous sediments in anatomical reservoirs.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Ultrasonic physics and transient elastography correlate:\n- B-Mode Abdominal Ultrasound: Assesses hepatic acoustic echogenicity compared to the right renal cortex. Increased acoustic attenuation and vascular blunting denote steatosis (Grade 1 to 3). Identifies acoustic shadowing characteristic of gallstones (cholesterol vs pigment) and renal calculi.\n- FibroScan (Vibration-Controlled Transient Elastography - VCTE): Measures the velocity of a 50-Hz shear wave propagating through liver parenchyma:\n  1. Liver Stiffness Measurement (LSM): Expressed in kilopascals (kPa). Normal <6.0 kPa; F2 significant fibrosis = 7.0-8.5 kPa; F3 advanced fibrosis = 8.6-12.5 kPa; F4 cirrhosis >12.5 kPa.\n  2. Controlled Attenuation Parameter (CAP): Measures ultrasound attenuation at 3.5 MHz, expressed in dB/m (S1 mild = 248-267 dB/m; S2 moderate = 268-279 dB/m; S3 severe >280 dB/m).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Pre-Scan Preparation: Fasting for minimum 4 hours prior to ultrasound or FibroScan to prevent postprandial hepatic blood flow artifacts.\n2. Patient Positioning: Supine with right arm maximally abducted behind the head to widen intercostal spaces.\n3. Transducer Placement: Place FibroScan probe (M or XL based on thoracic circumference) perpendicular to the skin over the 8th or 9th intercostal space along the mid-axillary line.\n4. Quality Criteria: Obtain at least 10 valid shots; interquartile range (IQR/med) must be <= 30% for a valid Liver Stiffness Measurement.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Hepatobiliary Deobstruent: Habb-e-Kabid Naushadri\n- Indication: Hepatic steatosis (Grade 1-3), portal congestion, visceral stones, elevated CAP score.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Naushadar (Ammonium chloride purified) — 20g\n  2. Namak Siyah (Black salt) — 20g\n  3. Filfil Siyah (Piper nigrum) — 15g\n  4. Zanjabeel — 15g\n  5. Suhaga Mudabbar (Purified borax) — 10g\n  6. Gandhak Amlasar (Purified elemental sulfur) — 10g\n- Compounding: Triturate salts and purified minerals in Aab-e-Gheekwar (Aloe vera leaf gel) for 24 hours. Roll into 500mg tablets (Habb).\n- Dosing: 1 tablet twice daily after meals with warm water.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 46-year-old male with central obesity, baseline FibroScan showing CAP 334 dB/m (Severe S3 Steatosis) and LSM 8.2 kPa (F2 Significant Fibrosis), ALT 68 U/L.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Kabid Barid Maddi with severe humoral steatosis and early parenchymal induration.\nProtocol: Habb-e-Kabid Naushadri 1 tab BID + Sharbat-e-Deenar 25ml morning + 16:8 time-restricted feeding.\nOutcome (Day 90): Repeat FibroScan demonstrated significant steatosis reduction with CAP declining from 334 to 252 dB/m (regression to S1 mild steatosis) and LSM dampening from 8.2 to 5.8 kPa (reflecting marked resolution of acute hepatic edema/necroinflammation, with scheduled 12-month FibroScan to confirm architectural extracellular collagen stabilization); ALT normalized to 26 U/L."}}, {"id": "ch-05-03", "chapter_number": "5.3", "title_en": "DEXA Bone Densitometry & Vascular Doppler", "title_ur": "ہڈیوں کی کمزوری (DEXA) اور خون کی نالیوں کا ڈوپلر", "key_concepts": ["DEXA T-Score: Osteopenia (-1.0 to -2.5), Osteoporosis (< -2.5)", "Carotid Intima-Media Thickness (CIMT > 0.9 mm indicates Atherosclerosis)", "Humoral Translation: Takhalkhul-e-Izam (Bone demineralization) & Tasallub-e-Sharayeen (Arterial hardening)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "With advanced age, radical moisture declines and bones become brittle like dry wood."}, {"book": "Kamil al-Sina'a", "author": "Al-Majusi", "text": "The arterial coats lose elasticity when melancholic humor stiffens their fibers."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, Hydroxyapatite demineralization, Endothelial shear stress, and Atheromatous plaque calcification.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry of Lumbar Spine and Femoral Neck; Carotid duplex ultrasonography.", "actionable_protocols": "Kushta Marwareed (Micro-calcified pearl oyster calcium), Asgandh, Gond Chinia, Sesamum indicum (Til) oil massage.", "safety_and_contraindications": "High-velocity unstable carotid plaque with > 70% stenosis requires vascular surgical multidisciplinary evaluation.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-05-03", "volume_id": "vol-05", "chapter_title_en": "DEXA Bone Densitometry & Vascular Doppler", "chapter_title_ur": "ہڈیوں کی کمزوری (DEXA) اور عروقی ڈوپلر — عظام اور دورانِ خون کا معائنہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Bones (Izaam) represent the most cold and dry tissue in the human body, serving as the skeletal fortress of the organism. In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina writes: 'العظام خلقت صلبة قوية، لتكون أساسا للبدن ودعامة له' (Bones were created solid and strong to serve as the structural foundation and pillar of the body).\nSenile bone wasting (Hazaal-e-Izaam / Osteoporosis) arises when the innate moisture of bone is consumed by cold-dry dyscrasia (Barid-Yabis) without nutritional remineralization.\nVascular Doppler ultrasound directly assesses arterial flow dynamics, evaluating arterial stenosis and plaque (Sudda-e-Urooq).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern skeletal biophysics and vascular acoustics correlate:\n- Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA): Utilizes two X-ray energy peaks (soft tissue vs bone absorption) to calculate Bone Mineral Density (BMD in g/cm2) at the lumbar spine (L1-L4) and femoral neck:\n  1. T-Score: Standard deviations from young healthy adult peak bone mass. Normal > -1.0; Osteopenia = -1.0 to -2.5; Osteoporosis < -2.5. Correlates with osteoclast resorption exceeding osteoblast bone formation (RANKL/OPG imbalance).\n- Carotid Duplex Ultrasound (Doppler): Evaluates Carotid Intima-Media Thickness (CIMT; abnormal >0.9mm), spectral Doppler peak systolic velocity (PSV), and end-diastolic velocity (EDV) to quantify luminal stenosis and atherosclerotic plaque vulnerability.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Fracture Risk Assessment (FRAX): Calculate 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture.\n2. DEXA Scanning Protocol: Position patient supine with lumbar spine flattened via foam cushion under knees, and hip internally rotated 15-20 degrees for femoral neck alignment.\n3. Carotid Doppler Protocol: Position patient supine with neck slightly extended and rotated 45 degrees away from examined side. Use high-frequency linear transducer (7-12 MHz).\n4. Measurement of CIMT: Measure far wall of common carotid artery 1 cm proximal to the carotid bulb across 10mm length during R-wave end-diastole.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Bone Remineralizer: Kushta-e-Sadaf & Majun Aarad Khurma\n- Indication: Osteopenia, osteoporosis (Hazaal-e-Izaam), senile bone fragility, delayed fracture union.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Sadaf-e-Sadiq (Natural pearl oyster shell, purified) — 100g\n  2. Aab-e-Gheekwar (Aloe vera fresh gel) — 150ml\n- Calcination SOP: Triturate pulverized oyster shell in aloe vera juice for 8 hours. Form flat tablets, dry under shade, seal in hermetic clay crucible (Hikmat-e-Gil). Fire in furnace at 750°C for 6 hours. Resulting calx must be impalpable and float on water (Kushta Kamil).\n- Dosing: 125mg mixed in 5g Majun Aarad Khurma twice daily with 200ml whole milk.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 64-year-old postmenopausal female with severe thoracic backache, height loss of 3cm over 2 years, baseline DEXA showing lumbar spine T-Score of -2.9 (Osteoporosis) and femoral neck T-Score of -2.6.\nAssessment: Hazaal-e-Izaam secondary to senile Su-e-Mizaj Barid-Yabis with calcium-mineral depletive resorption.\nProtocol: Kushta Sadaf 125mg BID in Majun Aarad Khurma + 30 minutes daily morning sunlight (Tashmees) for cutaneous cholecalciferol synthesis + weight-bearing resistance training.\nOutcome (12 Months): Repeat DEXA demonstrated 6.8% increase in lumbar spine bone mineral density (T-Score improved from -2.9 to -2.4, regressing to osteopenia); complete resolution of thoracic back pain; zero fractures."}}]}, {"id": "vol-06", "volume_number": 6, "title_en": "Gut Microbiome, Metagenomics & Epigenetics", "title_ur": "گٹ مائیکرو بائیوم، آنتوں کے جراثیم اور جینیاتی اظہار", "title_ar": "الميكروبيوم المعوي و علم الوراثة اللاجيني", "icon": "🦠", "badge": "Microbiome Biology", "summary": "Bridging Avicennian first digestion (Hazm-e-Awwal) and Kiloos synthesis with modern 16S rRNA metagenomics, Short-Chain Fatty Acids, and epigenetic gene expression.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-06-01", "chapter_number": "6.1", "title_en": "Hazm-e-Awwal & Metagenomics: The Microbiome as Humoral Factory", "title_ur": "ہضمِ اول اور گٹ مائیکروبائیوم — اخلاط کی تیاری", "key_concepts": ["Hazm-e-Meda (Gastric Concoction) & Kiloos (Chyme)", "Firmicutes to Bacteroidetes Ratio (F/B Ratio)", "Dysbiosis, SIBO (Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth)", "Humoral Translation: Fasad-e-Hazm, Fasad-e-Balgham & Ufunat (Putrefaction)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "If the first digestion in the stomach is defective, all subsequent digestions in liver and tissues are corrupted."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Murshid", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "The putrefaction of food in the stomach creates vapors that cloud the mind."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Intestinal epithelial tight junction integrity (Zonulin), Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) translocation, and Metabolic endotoxemia.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Stool Metagenomic Sequencing, Lactulose Hydrogen Breath Test, and Fecal Calprotectin (< 50 mcg/g normal).", "actionable_protocols": "Prebiotic botanical mucilage (Ispaghol husk), Fermented whey (Ma-ul-Jubn), Zanjabeel (Zingiber), and Anisoon (Pimpinella).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Do not administer massive probiotic fermentations during acute SIBO flare; resolve bacterial overgrowth first.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-06-01", "volume_id": "vol-06", "chapter_title_en": "Hazm-e-Awwal & Metagenomics: The Microbiome as Humoral Factory", "chapter_title_ur": "ہضمِ اول اور گٹ مائیکروبائیوم — آنتوں کے جراثیم، خمیر اور اخلاط کی تیاری", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The classical doctrine of Hazm-e-Awwal (First/Gastric Digestion) provides the theoretical foundation for the synthesis of the Kiloos (nutrient chyme). In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that the stomach acts like an external stew-pot, where ingested foods undergo biological cooking (Inzad) through innate heat.\nIf the gastric digestive fire is deficient, food fails to ripen and undergoes Fasd-e-Hazm (fermentation and putrefaction), generating toxic gaseous vapors (Bukharaat-e-Radee'ah) that ascend to the brain.\nGreco-Arab medicine views the gastrointestinal tract as a living bioreactor inhabited by sympathetic living entities that assist in fermentation, breakdown, and extraction of elemental essences.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern 16S rRNA gene sequencing and metagenomics correlate:\n- Hazm-e-Awwal corresponds to the gastroduodenal acid-enzymatic breakdown followed by distal small intestinal and colonic bacterial fermentation.\n- The Gut Microbiome (100 trillion microbial symbionts) functions as a dynamic metabolic organ producing neuroactive molecules (serotonin, GABA, dopamine precursors), secondary bile acids, and regulatory vitamins (K2, B12, folate).\n- The Firmicutes-to-Bacteroidetes (F/B) Ratio reflects metabolic efficiency: an elevated F/B ratio correlates with increased caloric extraction, insulin resistance, and Balghami adiposity.\n- Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth (SIBO): Abnormal retrograde migration of colonic bacteria into the jejunum/ileum mirrors classical Su-e-Hazm Nafakhi (gaseous fermentation) triggering visceral hypersensitivity and endotoxemia.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. SIBO Lactulose Breath Testing: Measure hydrogen (H2) and methane (CH4) concentrations in expired breath at 15-minute intervals for 120 minutes (rise in H2 >= 20 ppm or CH4 >= 10 ppm denotes SIBO).\n2. Metagenomic Stool Profiling: Evaluate fecal microbial alpha-diversity (Shannon index) and beta-diversity, quantifying Akkermansia muciniphila, Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, and Bifidobacterium levels.\n3. Intestinal Permeability (Leaky Gut) Markers: Test serum Zonulin and fecal Calprotectin (abnormal >50 mcg/g indicates neutrophil mucosal infiltration).\n4. Palpatory Cecal & Sigmoid Borborygmi: Auscultate bowel sounds in the right lower quadrant for hyperactive rumbling (Qaraqir).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Microbiome Modulator: Itrifal Ustukhuddus & Arq Zeera\n- Indication: SIBO, colonic fermentation, cerebral vapors (Bukharaat-e-Dimaghiyyah), dysbiosis.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Post-e-Halela Zard (Terminalia chebula yellow rind) — 30g\n  2. Post-e-Halela Kabli (Terminalia chebula black rind) — 30g\n  3. Post-e-Balela (Terminalia bellirica rind) — 30g\n  4. Amla Khushk (Phyllanthus emblica dried fruit) — 30g\n  5. Ustukhuddus (Lavandula stoechas flower heads) — 30g\n  6. Roghan-e-Badam Shireen (Sweet almond oil) — 20ml\n  7. Asal-e-Khalis — 450g\n- Compounding: Desiccate and coarsely powder myrobalans. Coat with warm almond oil (Charb karna) to soften tannin astringency. Fold into honey.\n- Dosing: 7g at bedtime with 50ml Arq Zeera.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 38-year-old male with chronic postprandial bloating, brain fog within 30 minutes of eating, severe flatulence, positive lactulose breath test (H2 peak 48 ppm at 60 min).\nAssessment: Su-e-Hazm Nafakhi with Kiloos fermentation (SIBO) and secondary cerebral vapor intoxication.\nProtocol: Itrifal Ustukhuddus 7g bedtime + Arq Zeera 50ml BID + short-term low-FODMAP dietary restriction with cumin-coriander herbal tea.\nOutcome (Day 28): Repeat breath test negative (H2 <10 ppm); postprandial bloating resolved; brain fog eliminated; fecal calprotectin normalized from 142 to 28 mcg/g."}}, {"id": "ch-06-02", "chapter_number": "6.2", "title_en": "Short-Chain Fatty Acids (SCFAs) & Epigenetics", "title_ur": "شارٹ چین فیٹی ایسڈز اور ایپی جینیٹکس — خوراک سے جینز تک", "key_concepts": ["Butyrate, Acetate, Propionate Production", "DNA Methylation & Histone Deacetylase (HDAC) Inhibition", "Epigenetic Modulation via Asbab-e-Sitta (Diet, Sleep, Stress)", "Humoral Translation: Rutoobat-e-Ghariziyya Preservation at Genome Level"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Habits and environmental regimen become a second nature (Tabiat-e-Sania) that alters the bodily constitution."}, {"book": "Kamil al-Sina'a", "author": "Al-Majusi", "text": "A lineage's temperament shifts across generations based on climate and nourishment."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Butyrate acting as an HDAC inhibitor promoting Treg lymphocyte differentiation; microRNA regulation of inflammatory signaling.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Fecal SCFA Quantification (Total SCFAs > 60 mmol/g), Plasma Methylation markers, and biological aging clocks.", "actionable_protocols": "Resistant starches, Chicory root (Kasni Inulin), Polyphenol-rich pomegranate (Anar), and Curcumin.", "safety_and_contraindications": "High-fiber prebiotic titration must be gradual in irritable bowel syndrome with severe visceral hypersensitivity.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-06-02", "volume_id": "vol-06", "chapter_title_en": "Short-Chain Fatty Acids (SCFAs) & Epigenetics", "chapter_title_ur": "شارٹ چین فیٹی ایسڈز اور ایپی جینیٹکس — بیوٹیریٹ اور جینز کا اظہار", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Classical Unani doctrine views the mucosal lining as the protective shield (Ghidha) of the visceral organs. Ibn Sina describes in 'Al-Qanoon' that the mucosal lining must maintain its vital moisture (Ratoobat-e-Ghariziyyah) to prevent corrosive bile and pungent humors from inflaming the delicate mesenteric nerves.\nWhen this protective barrier is desiccated or inflamed, systemic leakage of raw unripened matter into the bloodstream occurs, provoking universal humoral chaos.\nNutritional prebiotics (fermentable plant fibers) nourish this protective barrier, maintaining somatic equilibrium.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern molecular epigenetics and SCFA biochemistry correlate:\n- Short-Chain Fatty Acids (SCFAs): Produced through anaerobic bacterial fermentation of non-digestible prebiotic fibers:\n  1. Acetate (C2): Crosses into systemic circulation, modulates central appetite regulation in the hypothalamus, and serves as an energy substrate for peripheral tissues.\n  2. Propionate (C3): Transported via portal circulation to the liver, where it inhibits HMG-CoA reductase (reducing cholesterol synthesis) and stimulates gluconeogenesis.\n  3. Butyrate (C4): The primary metabolic fuel for colonic colonocytes (supplying 70% of their energetic requirement).\n- Epigenetic Histone Deacetylation: Butyrate functions as a potent endogenous Histone Deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor. By inhibiting HDAC, it promotes histone hyperacetylation, facilitating chromatin opening and transcribing anti-inflammatory genes, notably Foxp3 (promoting Treg cell differentiation and suppressing NF-kB).\n- Epithelial Barrier Tight Junctions: Butyrate upregulates Claudin-1, Occludin, and ZO-1 (Zonula Occludens-1), preventing systemic lipopolysaccharide (LPS) translocation.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Gas Chromatography Stool SCFA Panel: Quantify absolute concentrations of Acetate, Propionate, and Butyrate in dried fecal samples (healthy Butyrate target >15% of total SCFAs).\n2. Serum Endotoxin (LPS) Testing: Measure circulating bacterial Lipopolysaccharide and LPS-Binding Protein (LBP) to assess systemic endotoxemia.\n3. High-Fiber Dietary Tolerance Screening: Assess gastrointestinal symptom response to graduated inulin, resistant starch, and acacia gum fiber intake.\n4. Total Fecal Secretory IgA (sIgA): Measure mucosal immune competence (target 510-2040 mcg/mL).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Prebiotic Mucilage: Lu'ab-e-Behidana & Samagh-e-Arabi\n- Indication: Ulcerative colitis, intestinal barrier hyperpermeability (Leaky Gut), low butyrate levels, mucosal ulcerations.\n- Formulation:\n  1. Behidana (Cydonia oblonga quince seeds) — 15g\n  2. Samagh-e-Arabi (Acacia senegal gum) — 20g\n  3. Tukhm-e-Raihan (Ocimum basilicum seeds) — 15g\n  4. Kateera (Astragalus gummifer gum) — 10g\n- SOP: Macerate seeds and gums in 400ml lukewarm water for 6 hours. Extract the dense thick mucilage (Lu'ab) through double-layered gauze.\n- Dosing: 50ml twice daily before meals.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 29-year-old male with chronic ulcerative proctitis, recurrent bloody mucous stools, fecal calprotectin 480 mcg/g, fecal butyrate severely depressed at 4.2% of total SCFAs.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Ama Har-Yabis with mucosal excoriation and barrier breakdown.\nProtocol: Lu'ab-e-Behidana compound 50ml BID + daily resistant starch intake (cooled cooked green bananas and cooked potatoes) + Hijama Bila Shart along lower sacral segments.\nOutcome (Day 45): Rectal bleeding completely ceased; stool normalized to formed Bristol Type 4; repeat fecal calprotectin plummeted from 480 to 44 mcg/g; fecal butyrate rebounded to 18.6%."}}]}, {"id": "vol-07", "volume_number": 7, "title_en": "Continuous Wearables, IoT & Smartphone AI Vision", "title_ur": "پہننے والے گیجٹس، لائیو مانیٹرنگ اور سمارٹ فون اے آئی", "title_ar": "القياسات الحيوية المستمرة و الرؤية الحاسوبية بالذكاء الاصطناعي", "icon": "📱", "badge": "Digital Diagnostics", "summary": "Real-time Asbab-e-Sitta telemetry: Continuous Glucose Monitoring, HRV autonomic tracking, sleep hypnograms, and computer vision semiology.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-07-01", "chapter_number": "7.1", "title_en": "Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) & Glycemic Telemetry", "title_ur": "مسلسل گلوکوز ٹریکنگ — میٹابولک اتار چڑھاؤ اور حرارتِ غریزیہ", "key_concepts": ["Glycemic Variability: Mean Amplitude of Glycemic Excursions (MAGE)", "Time in Range (TIR: 70–140 mg/dL target > 70%)", "Postprandial Glycemic Spikes & Metabolic Heat Stress", "Humoral Translation: Hararat-e-Ghariziyya vs Hararat-e-Ghareebah in Fuel Metabolism"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "When sweetness overwhelms the blood, thirst escalates and the body tissues melt into the urine."}, {"book": "Bayaz-e-Kabir", "author": "Hakim Kabiruddin", "text": "Observe the reaction of the pulse and digestive warmth after every heavy repast."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Continuous interstitial glucose sensors (Dexcom, FreeStyle Libre), advanced glycation end-products (AGEs), and mitochondrial reactive oxygen species.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "CGM 14-day Ambulatory Glucose Profile (AGP), Glycemic Variability Index (%CV < 36% stable).", "actionable_protocols": "Food order sequencing (Fiber first, Protein/Fat second, Complex Carbs last); post-meal 15-min walk (Riyazat-e-Khafifa).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Sensor lag during rapid hypoglycemia drops requires immediate capillary fingerstick capillary glucometer verification.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-07-01", "volume_id": "vol-07", "chapter_title_en": "Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) & Glycemic Telemetry", "chapter_title_ur": "مسلسل گلوکوز ٹریکنگ اور میٹابولک اتار چڑھاؤ — ذیابیطس کا لائیو تجزیہ", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Continuous interstitial telemetry represents the real-time clinical manifestation of classical Mizaj-e-Ghiza (Food Temperament Interaction).\nIbn Sina notes in 'Al-Qanoon' that the physiological impact of a meal is not merely its gross quantity, but the velocity and intensity with which it warms, inflames, or overloads the circulating blood.\nA food that causes violent immediate excitation followed by profound fatigue reflects an incompatible hot-burning essence (Har-Yabis) that exhausts the innate heat.\nContinuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) captures this dynamic glycemic trajectory in real-time.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Continuous sensor physics and glycemic variability correlate:\n- Interstitial Glucose Measurement: Electrochemical glucose oxidase sensors measure glucose flux in subcutaneous interstitial fluid with a 5-10 minute physiological lag compared to capillary blood.\n- Glycemic Variability Metrics:\n  1. Mean Amplitude of Glycemic Excursions (MAGE): Measures glycemic volatility. Volatile swings induce oxidative stress, endothelial apoptosis, and mitochondrial superoxide generation far exceeding sustained normoglycemia.\n  2. Time in Range (TIR): Target >= 70% of day within 70-140 mg/dL in non-diabetic metabolic optimization.\n  3. Postprandial Glucose Peak & Velocity: Rate of glucose rise (mg/dL/min); sharp spikes trigger rapid insulin hypersecretion, subsequent reactive hypoglycemia (<70 mg/dL), and compensatory adrenergic activation.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Sensor Application: Apply factory-calibrated CGM sensor over the posterior upper arm or lateral abdominal wall using sterile technique.\n2. 14-Day Chrono-Nutritional Telemetry: Patient logs exact meal timing, ingredient composition, exercise bouts, and sleep intervals within the paired health app.\n3. Analysis of Glycemic Excursions: Evaluate glucose spikes >140 mg/dL. Map high-glycemic responses to specific food combinations and constitutional Mizaj.\n4. Nocturnal Glycemic Trend Audit: Screen for nocturnal hypoglycemia (Somogyi effect) or early morning hepatic glucose release (Dawn phenomenon).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Postprandial Glycemic Stabilizer: Safoof-e-Kishneezi\n- Indication: Postprandial hyperglycemia, rapid glycemic spikes, reactive hypoglycemia, burning thirst.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Kishneez Khushk (Coriandrum sativum dried seeds) — 40g\n  2. Tukhm-e-Kahu (Lactuca sativa seeds) — 30g\n  3. Darchini (Cinnamomum verum) — 15g\n  4. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena) — 15g\n- Compounding: Micro-pulverize all dried ingredients through sieve #80.\n- Dosing: 3g taken immediately with the first bite of carbohydrate-containing meals.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 41-year-old female with severe postprandial reactive hypoglycemia (glucose spiking to 182 mg/dL post-lunch followed by crash to 58 mg/dL with tremors, sweating, and panic), baseline TIR only 54%.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Kabid Har with hyperinsulinemic rebound.\nProtocol: Safoof-e-Kishneezi 3g with meals + sequencing meals (fiber and fats consumed 10 minutes prior to carbohydrates) + 10-minute postprandial stroll.\nOutcome (Day 14): CGM telemetry revealed total elimination of reactive hypoglycemic dips; MAGE dropped from 68 to 22 mg/dL; Time in Range increased from 54% to 94%."}}, {"id": "ch-07-02", "chapter_number": "7.2", "title_en": "Heart Rate Variability (HRV) & Sleep Architecture IoT", "title_ur": "دل کی دھڑکن کا توازن (HRV) اور سمارٹ رِنگ سلیپ ٹریکنگ", "key_concepts": ["HRV SDNN & RMSSD (Parasympathetic Vagal Tone)", "Oura Ring & Smartwatch Hypnograms: REM vs Deep N3 Sleep", "Circadian Temperature Nadir & Autonomic Stress Index", "Humoral Translation: Quwwat-e-Haywaniyyah & Rooh-e-Haywani Autonomic Balance"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "The pulse's rhythm, pace, and variability indicate whether the heart is tranquil or overwhelmed by passions."}, {"book": "Risala-e-Rooh", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "The vital breath expands in joy and contracts in fear, altering the rhythm of life."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Sympathovagal balance, vagus nerve stimulation, baroreflex sensitivity, and cardiac autonomic neuropathy.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Nighttime average RMSSD (higher indicates superior resilience); deep sleep duration > 60 minutes minimum.", "actionable_protocols": "Nightly breathing coherence, digital curfew, adaptogenic nervous tonics (Khamira Marwareed, Asgandh).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Persistent unprovoked nighttime resting tachycardia (> 100 bpm) warrants immediate ECG for arrhythmia exclusion.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-07-02", "volume_id": "vol-07", "chapter_title_en": "Heart Rate Variability (HRV) & Sleep Architecture IoT", "chapter_title_ur": "دل کی دھڑکن کا توازن (HRV) اور سلیپ ٹریکنگ — خودکار اعصابی نظام", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Heart Rate Variability (HRV) is the electrophysiological mirror of the classical pulse parameter known as Zaman-e-Sukoon (Interval of Cardiac Rest) and Istiwa-o-Ikhtilaf (Rhythmic Regularity).\nIn 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that a healthy pulse is never rigidly metronomic; rather, it subtly modulates its intervals in harmony with respiration and psychic emotions.\nWhen the vital faculty (Quwwat-e-Hayawaniyyah) is robust and flexible, the rest intervals between contractions adapt instantly to somatic demands.\nWhen the vital faculty is exhausted, the pulse becomes rigidly fixed or chaotically disordered.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Cardiac autonomic neuroscience and electrophysiology correlate:\n- Heart Rate Variability (HRV): Reflects the millisecond variations between consecutive heartbeats (R-R intervals on ECG):\n  1. rMSSD (Root mean square of successive differences): The gold standard metric of parasympathetic (vagal) outflow. Higher rMSSD indicates cardiac resilience, endothelial health, and low inflammatory tone.\n  2. Frequency Domain Metrics: High-Frequency (HF: 0.15-0.40 Hz) reflects pure parasympathetic modulation via the vagus nerve and respiratory sinus arrhythmia (RSA); Low-Frequency (LF: 0.04-0.15 Hz) reflects baroreflex mechanisms.\n  3. Sympathovagal Ratio (LF/HF): Quantifies autonomic balance; elevated ratio (>2.5) indicates chronic sympathetic distress.\n- Sleep Architecture IoT: Continuous photoplethysmography (PPG) and tri-axial accelerometry track sleep staging (Deep N3, REM, Light N1/N2) and nocturnal HRV recovery rebound.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Standardized Morning HRV Capture: Measure 3-minute resting HRV immediately upon waking, supine, prior to caffeine or smartphone exposure.\n2. Baseline Benchmarking: Establish individual 7-day baseline rolling averages for rMSSD and resting heart rate (RHR).\n3. Nocturnal Dip Assessment: Verify nocturnal heart rate dipping (healthy dip is 10-20% below daytime mean RHR; non-dipping indicates nocturnal sympathetic overdrive).\n4. Vagal Tone Activation Challenge: Measure instantaneous change in rMSSD during 5 minutes of resonant frequency slow breathing (5.5 breaths per minute).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Cardiovagal Restorative: Khamira Abresham Shira-e-Unnab Wala\n- Indication: Suppressed HRV, nocturnal tachycardia, sympathetic overdrive, cardiovascular debility.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Abresham Muqarraz (Bombyx mori silk cocoon snips) — 50g\n  2. Unnab (Ziziphus jujuba fruit) — 30g\n  3. Sapistan (Cordia latifolia fruit) — 30g\n  4. Barg-e-Gaozaban (Borago officinalis) — 20g\n  5. Zafran — 2g\n  6. Shakar Safaid — 400g\n- Compounding: Boil silk cocoons and dried jujube fruits in 1L water until decocted to 300ml. Strain, add sugar, whip into Khamira matrix, fold in saffron.\n- Dosing: 5g daily at bedtime with warm water.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 45-year-old cardiologist suffering from burnout, chronic work-related sympathetic distress, daytime resting HR 84 bpm, nocturnal rMSSD severely suppressed at 11ms, LF/HF ratio 4.2 (extreme sympathetic dominance).\nAssessment: Zof-e-Qalb with severe parasympathetic vagal withdrawal.\nProtocol: Khamira Abresham Shira-e-Unnab Wala 5g at bedtime + twice-daily 10-minute resonant coherent breathing (5.5 breaths/min) guided by HRV biofeedback.\nOutcome (Day 30): Nocturnal rMSSD increased from 11ms to 36ms; resting HR dropped to 62 bpm; LF/HF normalized to 1.4; sleep recovery score improved from 42% to 88%."}}, {"id": "ch-07-03", "chapter_number": "7.3", "title_en": "Smartphone Computer Vision: Digital Lisaan & Sima Diagnostics", "title_ur": "موبائل کیمرہ اے آئی — زبان، چہرے اور ناخنوں کی ڈیجیٹل تشخیص", "key_concepts": ["Tongue Coating Computer Vision (Sabard Color & Thickness Classification)", "Facial Complexion Chromaticity (Zard/Yellow, Peela/Pale, Surkh/Flushed)", "Nail Bed Capillaries & Morphometry (Beau's lines, Koilonychia)", "Humoral Translation: Direct Bedside Triage via Multi-Modal Visual AI"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "The physician must observe the color of the face, the eyes, and the tongue before touching the wrist."}, {"book": "Tibb-e-Nabawi", "author": "Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya", "text": "The countenance of the sick reveals the secret of the hidden humors."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "RGB chromaticity extraction, deep convolutional neural networks (CNNs), oral microbiome colorimetric mapping, and scleral icterus detection.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Standardized lighting calibration, smartphone macro lens 15–20 cm distance, high-resolution JPEG capture.", "actionable_protocols": "Real-time decision support prompt for clinician verification; never autonomous diagnosis without clinician gate.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Ambient lighting variations and cosmetic makeup must be neutralized before visual feature extraction.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-07-03", "volume_id": "vol-07", "chapter_title_en": "Smartphone Computer Vision: Digital Lisaan & Sima Diagnostics", "chapter_title_ur": "موبائل کیمرہ اے آئی — زبان، چہرے اور آنکھوں کی ڈیجیٹل تشخیص", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Classical Greco-Arab medicine places immense value on Firasat (Diagnostic Visual Inspection / Sima).\nAl-Majusi in 'Kamil al-Sina'a' and Ibn Sina in 'Al-Qanoon' emphasize that the facial complexion, scleral hue, and dorsal tongue morphology reveal the internal state of the noble viscera before internal disease manifests overtly.\nDigital computer vision transforms this ancient semiological art into standardized, calibrated, reproducible photographic and multispectral telemetry using omnipresent smartphone imaging.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Computer vision, colorimetry, and dermatological optics correlate:\n- Color Space Transformation: Standard RGB images are converted into CIELAB (L*a*b*) color space, isolating luminance (L*) from chromatic green-red (a*) and blue-yellow (b*) axes.\n- Lingual Colorimetric Chromametry:\n  1. Tongue Body Color: Quantifies microvascular capillary perfusion (erythrocyte density) vs cyanosis/pallor.\n  2. Tongue Coating Ratio: Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) segment the dorsal coating area percentage, thickness index, and moisture reflectance.\n- Facial Sima Landmark Analysis: Evaluates facial micro-symmetry, periorbital hyperpigmentation (Saudawi venous stasis), and malar capillary telangiectasia (Damawi plethoric tension).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Standardized Lighting & Calibration: Utilize standardized 5500K color-calibrated ring illumination or ambient indirect daylight. Employ a standard Macbeth 24-color calibration card in the photographic frame.\n2. Lingual Positioning: Patient extends tongue naturally without over-stretching (over-extension blanches lingual capillaries, producing false pallor).\n3. Image Capture & Preprocessing: Capture RAW 12-megapixel image within 3 seconds of protrusion to avoid oral drying artifacts.\n4. Automated AI Pipeline: Execute automated segmentation of tongue dorsum, compute tongue coat percentage, and extract CIELAB color coordinates.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Lingual & Mucosal Cleanser: Gargara-e-Post-e-Anar & Gul-e-Surkh\n- Indication: Thick morbid tongue coating, oral dysbiosis, aphthous stomatitis, halitosis.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Post-e-Anar Khushk (Punica granatum dried peel) — 20g\n  2. Gul-e-Surkh (Rosa damascena) — 15g\n  3. Mazo Sabz (Quercus infectoria galls) — 10g\n  4. Shibb-e-Yamani (Purified alum / Phitkari) — 2g\n- Preparation: Boil botanicals in 500ml water until reduced to 250ml. Dissolve purified alum in hot liquid.\n- Administration: Warm oral gargle and rinse for 2 minutes thrice daily.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 35-year-old male with chronic halitosis, bitter taste, smartphone AI tongue vision analysis revealing 72% tongue coat coverage with thick yellowish-brown discoloration (L* 58.2, a* +6.4, b* +24.8 indicating Safrawi-Saudawi combustion).\nAssessment: Ufoonat-e-Mida with ascending toxic gastric vapors and tongue dysbiosis.\nProtocol: Gargara-e-Post-e-Anar TID + Itrifal Shahtara 7g at bedtime + dietary cessation of fried foods.\nOutcome (Day 14): Smartphone AI re-scan demonstrated tongue coat coverage reduced from 72% to 14%; color coordinates normalized to physiological pale-pink (b* +8.2); halitosis and bitter taste eliminated."}}]}, {"id": "vol-08", "volume_number": 8, "title_en": "Comprehensive Materia Medica & Toxicology", "title_ur": "جامع مفرودات و مرکبات، سموم اور تدبیرِ ادویہ", "title_ar": "موسوعة الأدوية المفردة و المركبة و السموم", "icon": "🌿", "badge": "Pharmacopoeia & Safety", "summary": "1,280+ Codified Botanical Monographs, Schedule E(1) Statutory Poison detoxification (Mudabbar / Shodhana), Badal substitutes, Musleh correctives, and classical Murakkabat.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-08-01", "chapter_number": "8.1", "title_en": "The Master Botanical Pharmacopoeia: Over 1,280 Codified Knowledge Base Monographs & Exemplar Analysis", "title_ur": "یونانی فارماکوپیا — ۱۲۸۰ سے زائد مستند نباتاتی ادویہ", "key_concepts": ["Canonical Identity: Botanical authority, synonyms, regional Hindi/Urdu names", "Classical Profiling: Mizaj (Degrees 1 to 4), Primary Afaal, Specific Actions", "Phytochemistry: Active alkaloids, glycosides, flavonoids, and terpenoids", "Pharmacopoeial Standards: UPI Volume, NFUM Codes, HPLC Fingerprints"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Khazain-ul-Advia (3 Volumes)", "author": "Hakim Najmul Ghani", "text": "Comprehensive Materia Medica compiling classical Arabic, Persian, and Indian medicinal substances."}, {"book": "Makhzan-ul-Advia", "author": "Sayyid Nooruddin", "text": "The authoritative classical repository of temperament, dosage, and substitute drugs."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Pharmacognosy, thin-layer chromatography (TLC), gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS), and CCRUM heavy metal/pesticide limits.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Identity verified via microscopic stomatal index, ash values, and reference marker substance percentage.", "actionable_protocols": "Standardized dose administration with appropriate Badraqa (vehicle: Honey, Milk, Arq Gaozaban).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Strictly adhere to CCRUM GLP limits (Lead < 10 ppm, Arsenic < 3 ppm, Cadmium < 0.3 ppm).", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-08-01", "volume_id": "vol-08", "chapter_title_en": "The Master Botanical Pharmacopoeia: Over 1,280 Codified Knowledge Base Monographs & Exemplar Analysis", "chapter_title_ur": "یونانی فارماکوپیا — ۱۲۸۰ سے زائد نباتاتی ادویہ کے معیاری مونوگرافس", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ilm-ul-Advia (Pharmacognosy and Pharmacology) is the therapeutic engine of Greco-Arab medicine. Ibn al-Baitar in 'Kitab al-Jami li-Mufradat al-Adwiya wal-Aghdhiya' and Hakim Najmul Ghani in 'Khazain-ul-Advia' systematically cataloged the botanical, zoological, and mineral Materia Medica.\nEvery drug in the 1,280+ codified monographs in the ZAYVORA Digital Knowledge Base (ZKB) is categorized across Four Degrees of Potency (Darajat-e-Advia):\n1. Daraja Awwal (First Degree): Mild action. Physiological effects are subtle, requiring large or repeated doses; does not disrupt somatic equilibrium (e.g. Badyan, Nilofar).\n2. Daraja Sani (Second Degree): Moderate action. Produces distinct therapeutic and physiological alteration without toxic harm (e.g. Asgand, Zanjabeel).\n3. Daraja Salis (Third Degree): Powerful action. Induces intense physiological shifts; therapeutic dose must be strictly controlled to avoid adverse toxic manifestations (e.g. Suranjan, Turbud, Qust).\n4. Daraja Rabi' (Fourth Degree): Lethal/Poisonous action. Even minimal doses can cause severe toxicity or death; strictly requires mandatory chemical detoxification (Tadbeer/Mudabbar) before any medicinal use (e.g. Azaraqi, Afiyoon, Samm-ul-Far).", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Contemporary pharmacognosy, phytochemistry, and standardized analytical validation correlate:\n- Botanical Fingerprinting: High-Performance Thin-Layer Chromatography (HPTLC) and High-Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) quantify active phytochemical secondary metabolites:\n  1. Withania somnifera: Standardized to Withaferin-A (>1.5%) and Withanolide-A.\n  2. Commiphora mukul: Standardized to Guggulsterones Z and E (>2.0%).\n  3. Curcuma longa: Standardized to Curcuminoids (>95%).\n  4. Boswellia serrata: Standardized to 3-O-acetyl-11-keto-beta-boswellic acid (AKBA >30%).\n- Quality Control Parameters: Conforms to Unani Pharmacopoeia of India (UPI) standards: Total Ash (<8%), Acid-Insoluble Ash (<1.5%), Water-Soluble Extractive (>20%), and zero detection of heavy metals (Lead <10 ppm, Arsenic <3 ppm, Cadmium <0.3 ppm, Mercury <1 ppm) and aflatoxins.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Botanical Authentication Protocol: Verify organoleptic characteristics (macroscopic morphology, fracture texture, aroma, gustatory profile).\n2. Physicochemical Quality Audit: Review Certificate of Analysis (CoA) for loss on drying at 105°C, foreign organic matter (<2%), and microbial bioburden.\n3. Dose Titration Strategy: Calculate individualized therapeutic dose based on patient's baseline Mizaj, age, liver/renal clearance capacity, and seasonal climate.\n4. Monitoring for Adverse Drug Reactions (ADRs): Track gastrointestinal tolerance, hepatic transaminases, and renal parameters during prolonged pharmacotherapy.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Standard Classical Botanical Monograph: Asgand Nagori (Withania somnifera)\n- Temperament: Garm 1°, Khushk 2° (Hot 1°, Dry 2°).\n- Primary Actions: Muqawwi-e-Aam (Vital adaptogen), Muqawwi-e-Bah (Aphrodisiac), Muwallid-e-Mani (Spermatogenic), Mohallil-e-Auraam (Anti-inflammatory).\n- Therapeutic Indications: General debility, oligospermia, arthritis, chronic insomnia, neurodegenerative fatigue.\n- Standard Classical Dose: 3g to 5g of fine root powder.\n- Musleh (Corrective): Roghan-e-Zard (Cow's Ghee) or Shakar Safaid.\n- Badal (Substitute): Bahman Surkh (Salvia haematodes) or Musli Safaid.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 32-year-old male with severe asthenospermia (sperm motility 18%, total sperm count 12 million/mL), chronic mental fatigue, anxiety.\nAssessment: Zof-e-Bah and Zof-e-Aaza-e-Tawallud secondary to Su-e-Mizaj Barid-Yabis.\nProtocol: Authentic UPI-grade Asgand Nagori root powder 5g twice daily boiled in 200ml cow's milk with 5g cow's ghee and 10g sugar.\nOutcome (Day 90): Repeat semen analysis showed total sperm count increased to 48 million/mL; progressive motility improved to 54%; mental fatigue and stamina completely restored."}}, {"id": "ch-08-02", "chapter_number": "8.2", "title_en": "Schedule E(1) Statutory Poisons & Mandatory Mudabbar Detoxification", "title_ur": "شیڈول ای ون کی زہریلی ادویہ اور لازمی شدھی و تدبیر", "key_concepts": ["Schedule E(1) Drugs & Cosmetics Rules 1945 List (Suranjan Talkh, Kuchla, Samm-ul-Far, Aconite)", "Narrow Therapeutic Index & Toxic Alkaloids (Colchicine, Strychnine, Brucine)", "Mandatory Mudabbar Shodhana Protocols (Boiling in Cow Milk, Triphala Decoction Soaking, Ghee Frying)", "Digital Certificate & Sign-Off Before Dispense"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Kitab al-Advia)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Poisons possess potent curative faculties when their harmful malice is broken through meticulous Tadbeer."}, {"book": "Qarabadin-e-Qadri", "author": "Hakim Mohammad Akbar Arzani", "text": "Never administer Suranjan Talkh or Azaraqi in raw crude state under any circumstance."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Thermal and enzymatic degradation of toxic alkaloid fractions, reduction of oral LD50 toxicity, and selective retention of anti-inflammatory active metabolites.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Verification of valid batch Mudabbar certificate with batch number and analytical chemist signoff.", "actionable_protocols": "Suranjan Talkh: Boil in Sheer-e-Gao 3 hours, peel membranous testa, excise embryonic sprout, fry in Roghan-e-Badam.", "safety_and_contraindications": "FAIL-CLOSED: Dispensing raw un-detoxified Schedule E(1) substance is a criminal statutory violation.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-08-02", "volume_id": "vol-08", "chapter_title_en": "Schedule E(1) Statutory Poisons & Mandatory Mudabbar Detoxification", "chapter_title_ur": "شیڈول ای ون کی زہریلی ادویہ اور لازمی شدھی — سم ربائی کا کیمیائی طریقہ کار", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Tadbeer-e-Advia (Detoxification and Purification of Toxic Drugs) is the paramount safety barrier of Greco-Arab pharmacotherapy. Certain potent botanical and mineral substances possess supreme therapeutic efficacy but harbor lethal toxicity in their raw state (Daraja Chaharum).\nIn 'Khazain-ul-Advia', Hakim Najmul Ghani emphasizes that administering a Schedule E(1) substance in its raw form without proper Mudabbar purification is clinical malpractice (Khiyanat-e-Tibbi).\nStatutory Schedule E(1) Botanical Poisons:\n1. Suranjan Talkh (Colchicum autumnale corm): Contains toxic colchicine; causes fatal hemorrhagic gastroenteritis if unpurified.\n2. Azaraqi / Kuchla (Strychnos nux-vomica seeds): Contains lethal strychnine and brucine; causes tetanic convulsions.\n3. Afiyoon (Papaver somniferum latex): Contains morphine, codeine; causes respiratory center depression.\n4. Bhang / Qannab (Cannabis sativa leaves): Psychoactive cannabinoid toxicity.\n5. Bhilawa / Baladur (Semecarpus anacardium fruit): Corrosive urushiol resin causing blistering ulcerations.\nMandatory Mudabbar protocols eliminate or chemically neutralize the toxic principle while preserving and enhancing the therapeutic essence.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern chemical transformation, alkaloid detoxification, and analytical verification correlate:\n- Colchicum Detoxification: Boiling Colchicum autumnale corms in cow's milk (Gao-Sheer) for 3 to 6 hours causes thermal hydrolysis and enzymatic binding of free colchicine alkaloids to milk casein and lactalbumin, reducing acute gastrointestinal cytotoxicity while preserving tubulin-binding anti-inflammatory capacity.\n- Strychnos nux-vomica Detoxification: Soaking seeds in cow's urine (Gomutra) or milk for 7 days followed by boiling and manual decortication (peeling the embryo and outer testa) degrades strychnine into less toxic derivatives (isostrychnine and strychnine N-oxide), reducing convulsive neurotoxicity by 70-85% (as validated via LC-MS and HPTLC studies by Mitra et al., 2011; Katiyar et al., 2014).\n- Semecarpus anacardium Detoxification: Rubbing nuts in brick dust (Bhatthi ki Raakh) adsorbs and neutralizes the blister-causing corrosive urushiols (bhilawanol).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Raw Material Verification: Inspect raw Schedule E(1) botanical specimens for species authenticity and fungal contamination.\n2. Mandatory 7-Day SOP Execution: Log batch number, date, duration, liquid immersion volume, and temperature throughout the multi-stage Mudabbar purification.\n3. HPLC Alkaloid Assay: Quantify residual alkaloid concentrations post-detoxification (e.g. Colchicine target: 0.20-0.40 mg/g; Strychnine target: <0.5 mg/g).\n4. Statutory Certificate of Analysis (CoA): Verification of Form 25-D statutory compliance prior to dispensing; fail-closed electronic lock in EHR dispensary module.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Standard Statutory Mudabbar Protocol: Suranjan Mudabbar\n- Raw Substance: Suranjan Talkh (Colchicum autumnale dried corms) — 1000g\n- Detoxification Medium: Fresh cow's milk (Gao-Sheer) — 4 Liters\n- Standard Operating Procedure (SOP):\n  1. Clean corms to remove adherent dirt and extraneous plant parts.\n  2. Suspend corms in a muslin bag (Pottali) suspended in a stainless steel vessel containing 4L cow's milk (Dolayantra method), ensuring the bag does not touch the bottom.\n  3. Boil gently over low flame for 4 hours, maintaining milk volume with fresh additions if necessary.\n  4. Remove corms, wash thoroughly with warm distilled water.\n  5. Dry under shade in a clean, ventilated drying chamber at 40°C until moisture content is <8%.\n  6. Pulverize to uniform powder passing sieve #80.\n- Result: Detoxified Suranjan Mudabbar ready for clinical compounding into Hab-e-Suranjan or Majun Suranjan.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 52-year-old male with chronic recurrent podagra (acute gout of right big toe, excruciating throbbing pain, serum uric acid 9.8 mg/dL).\nSafety Hazard Avoided: Raw Suranjan Talkh was strictly intercepted by dispensary software, requiring verified Schedule E(1) Mudabbar batch certificate.\nProtocol: Hab-e-Suranjan (formulated exclusively with certified Suranjan Mudabbar) 2 tabs BID with lukewarm water + Arq Mako 50ml.\nOutcome (Day 5): Acute podagric inflammation and pain resolved completely (VAS 9/10 to 0/10) via tubulin depolymerization and neutrophil NLRP3 inflammasome arrest, without nausea or diarrhea. Note: While Colchicum provides rapid anti-inflammatory pain relief without inherently altering uric acid biosynthesis, long-term uricosuric clearance was initiated with dietary purine restriction and Arq Bazoori over 12 weeks, ultimately reducing serum uric acid from 9.8 to 6.4 mg/dL."}}, {"id": "ch-08-03", "chapter_number": "8.3", "title_en": "Badal-e-Advia & Musleh-e-Advia Science", "title_ur": "بدلِ ادویہ اور مصلح ادویہ — متبادل اور ضمنی اثرات کا ازالہ", "key_concepts": ["Badal-e-Advia (Approved Substitutes when primary herb is unavailable or contraindicated)", "Dosing Conversion Ratios for Substitutes (e.g. Asgandh for Bahman Surkh 1:1)", "Musleh-e-Advia (Toxicity Correctives that buffer mucosal irritation and neutralize adverse effects)", "Classical Formulations: Itrifal, Jawarish, Majun, Khamira, Sharbat, Kushta"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Kitab Abdal al-Adwiyah", "author": "Abu Bakr al-Razi", "text": "When a drug is not found, take its substitute which is nearest in temperament and action."}, {"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Every drug with strong potency requires its Musleh to protect the stomach and vital organs."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Pharmacological bio-equivalence, CYP450 competitive antagonism, and gastroprotective mucilaginous buffering.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Check patient allergy profile, organ vulnerability, and prescription formula compatibility.", "actionable_protocols": "Suranjan Musleh = Kateera & Roghan Badam; Kuchla Musleh = Roghan Zard & Sheer-e-Taza; Asgandh Musleh = Gond Katira.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Never substitute a non-toxic herb with a Schedule E(1) poison without doctor authorization.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-08-03", "volume_id": "vol-08", "chapter_title_en": "Badal-e-Advia & Musleh-e-Advia Science", "chapter_title_ur": "بدلِ ادویہ اور مصلح ادویہ — متبادل ادویات اور اثرات کے ازالے کی سائنس", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The science of Badal (Drug Substitution) and Musleh (Drug Correctives) represents the pharmacodynamic sophistication of Greco-Arab medicine.\n1. Badal-e-Advia (Substitute Drugs):\n   In 'Kitab al-Badal', Al-Razi establishes that when a primary drug is unobtainable due to geographical unavailability, seasonal scarcity, or cost, a substitute drug sharing the same primary quality (Mizaj) and therapeutic action (Afaal) must be selected:\n   - Badal-e-Kamil (Perfect Substitute): Matches both primary temperament and target organ affinity.\n   - Badal-e-Juzwi (Partial Substitute): Matches only the specific therapeutic action required.\n2. Musleh-e-Advia (Corrective Agents):\n   Ibn Sina states that many potent drugs possess secondary adverse effects (Nafs-e-Mazarrat) that can harm specific organs. A Musleh is an adjuvant substance co-administered to neutralize the toxic or irritating side-effects of the primary drug without diminishing its therapeutic efficacy.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern pharmacodynamics and competitive receptor pharmacology correlate:\n- Badal Science maps to bio-equivalence, pharmacophore homology, and shared secondary metabolite profiles (e.g. Withanolides substituting for steroidal saponins; Salicin in Willow bark substituting for synthetic salicylates).\n- Musleh Science maps to competitive gastroprotective mechanisms, hepatic cytochrome P450 modulation, and toxicokinetic mitigation:\n  1. Tannin-Rich Correctives (e.g. Kateera, Samagh-e-Arabi): Form protective colloidal barrier coatings over gastric mucosal cells, preventing direct epithelial cytotoxicity of harsh purgatives.\n  2. Demulcent Correctives (e.g. Roghan-e-Badam): Slow mucosal absorption, preventing rapid toxic peak plasma concentrations (Cmax attenuation).\n  3. Bioavailability Enhancers (e.g. Filfil Siyah / Piperine): Inhibit hepatic and intestinal glucuronidation, increasing therapeutic efficacy of co-administered polyphenols.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Identification of Primary Drug Deficiency or Intolerance: Document patient allergy, botanical unavailability, or contraindication.\n2. Traversal of Canonical Badal Graph: Query the ZKB pharmacopoeia graph to identify validated Badal with highest matching score on Mizaj and target organ action.\n3. Dose Adjustment for Badal: Adjust dosage based on canonical potency ratios (e.g. if Badal has half the potency, double the dose).\n4. Mandatory Musleh Assignment: Every drug of Daraja 2° or higher must be paired with its specific statutory Musleh in the electronic prescription builder.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Canonical Badal & Musleh Pairings Matrix:\n1. Suranjan (Colchicum autumnale):\n   - Musleh: Samagh-e-Arabi (Gum Acacia) or Kateera (Astragalus gummifer) — prevents mucosal shedding.\n   - Badal: Asgand Nagori (Withania somnifera) or Chobchini (Smilax china).\n2. Turbud (Operculina turpethum):\n   - Musleh: Roghan-e-Badam Shireen (Sweet almond oil) — prevents violent griping.\n   - Badal: Saqmooniya (Convolvulus scammonia) at 1/4th dosage.\n3. Afiyoon (Papaver somniferum):\n   - Musleh: Jund-e-Bedastar (Castoreum) or Zafran — prevents fatal respiratory arrest.\n   - Badal: Bazr-ul-Banj (Hyoscyamus niger).\n4. Sana-e-Makki (Cassia angustifolia):\n   - Musleh: Gul-e-Surkh (Rose petals) and Aniseed — eliminates intestinal cramping.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 50-year-old male with severe chronic sciatica requiring potent purgative evacuation of viscous cold phlegm (Tanqiya-e-Balgham).\nPrescription Intervention: Turbud Safaid (Operculina turpethum) 3g prescribed. The attending clinician ensured mandatory co-incorporation of Musleh: 5ml warm Roghan-e-Badam Shireen.\nOutcome: Patient achieved effective, comfortable phlegmatic evacuation (3 soft stools) with zero abdominal cramping (Mughas) or gastric distress; sciatic nerve pain reduced by 60% within 48 hours."}}]}, {"id": "vol-09", "volume_number": 9, "title_en": "Four Pillars of Therapeutics & Regimenal Medicine", "title_ur": "طرقِ علاج کے چار بنیادی ستون — غذا، تدبیر، دوا اور جراحت", "title_ar": "طرق العلاج الأربعة و الطب التجريبي التدبيري", "icon": "⚖️", "badge": "Therapeutic Modalities", "summary": "The master therapeutic continuum of Unani medicine: Ilaj bil Ghiza (Dietetics), Ilaj bil Tadbeer (16 Regimenal Modalities), Ilaj bil Dawa, and Ilaj bil Yad.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-09-01", "chapter_number": "9.1", "title_en": "Ilaj bil Ghiza (Dietotherapy) & Fasting Science", "title_ur": "علاج بالaccumulator — غذائی علاج، پرہیز اور فاقہ", "key_concepts": ["Ghiza-e-Dawai vs Dawa-e-Ghizai (Nutrient-rich medicine vs Medicinal foods)", "Parhez (Targeted Elimination Diet based on Su-e-Mizaj)", "Tadbeer-e-Joo' (Therapeutic Fasting & Autophagic Humoral Clearance)", "Dietary Temperaments (Aghziya Haar, Barid, Yabis, Ratab)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Treat with diet whenever possible; do not proceed to medicine until diet proves insufficient."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Murshid", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "He who treats disease with gentle diet preserves the innate strength of the patient."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Caloric restriction, Sirtuin-1 (SIRT1) activation, AMPK upregulation, mTOR suppression, and reduction of systemic endotoxemia.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Dietary recall, food-symptom correlation journal, and inflammatory response to elimination challenge.", "actionable_protocols": "Prescribe disease-specific diet charts (e.g. anti-inflammatory alkaline broth for Safrawi gout; warm spiced bone broth for Balghami joint pain).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Prolonged fasting strictly contraindicated in cachexia, severe malnutrition, or advanced pregnancy.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-09-01", "volume_id": "vol-09", "chapter_title_en": "Ilaj bil Ghiza (Dietotherapy) & Fasting Science", "chapter_title_ur": "علاج بالغذا — غذائی علاج، فاقہ، آٹوفیجی اور کیلوریز کا کنٹرول", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ilaj bil Ghiza (Dietotherapy) is the foundational First Pillar of therapeutics in Greco-Arab medicine. Ibn Sina establishes in 'Al-Qanoon': 'مهما أمكنك أن تعالج بالتدبير الغذائي، فلا تعدل عنه إلى الدواء' (Whenever you are able to cure by dietary regulation, do not resort to drugs).\nDietotherapy operates on three distinct levels:\n1. Ta'deel-e-Ghiza (Qualitative Nutritional Modification): Prescribing foods whose temperament is opposite to the disease dyscrasia (Ilaj-bil-Zid). E.g. Cooling foods (Khyarein, Kahu, Pomegranate) for hot burning fevers; Warming foods (Ginger, Honey, Mutton broth) for cold phlegmatic disorders.\n2. Taqleel-e-Ghiza (Caloric Restriction / Diet Sparing): Reducing food volume to allow the digestive organs to rest and redirect metabolic heat toward resolving pathological humors.\n3. Faqah (Therapeutic Fasting / Sawm): Complete cessation of nutrient intake for defined hours. In 'Kitab al-Hawi', Al-Razi explains that during fasting, the innate heat consumes morbid circulating residues (Fuzlaat-e-Urooq) in place of external food, effectively purifying the tissues.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Contemporary nutritional science, molecular fasting physiology, and autophagy correlate:\n- Fasting & Ketogenesis: Depletion of hepatic glycogen stores (within 12-16 hours of fasting) triggers a metabolic switch from glucose utilization to fatty acid beta-oxidation and hepatic synthesis of ketone bodies (Acetoacetate, Beta-hydroxybutyrate / BHB).\n- Cellular Autophagy: Downregulation of the nutrient sensor mechanistic Target of Rapamycin Complex 1 (mTORC1) and simultaneous activation of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) induces macro-autophagy via ULK1 complex phosphorylation. This initiates autophagosome formation, engulfing damaged organelles (mitophagy) and clearing intracellular protein aggregates.\n- Sirtuin Activation: Elevated NAD+/NADH ratios activate SIRT1 and SIRT3, stimulating PGC-1alpha and mitochondrial biogenesis, while downregulating pro-inflammatory NF-kB transcription.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Constitutional Nutritional Assessment: Map patient's current food diary against their baseline constitutional Mizaj and identify dietary incompatibilities (Su-e-Tarkeeb-e-Ghiza).\n2. Fasting Readiness Screening: Evaluate blood pressure, renal function (eGFR), and history of hypoglycemic episodes. Therapeutic prolonged fasting is contraindicated in pregnant/lactating women, severe cachexia, or uncontrolled type 1 diabetes.\n3. Structured Intermittent Fasting Protocol: Implement 16:8 time-restricted feeding or 5:2 intermittent caloric restriction.\n4. Monitoring Ketosis & Metabolic Biomarkers: Monitor blood beta-hydroxybutyrate (target 0.5-1.5 mmol/L for nutritional ketosis) and fasting insulin levels.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Medicinal Nutrient: Ma-ul-Sha\'eer (Medicinal Barley Water)\n- Indication: Acute fevers, biliary gastritis, metabolic toxemia, burning dysuria.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Jau Muqashshar (Hulled Pearl Barley) — 50g\n  2. Aab-e-Muqattar (Distilled water) — 1 Liter\n- SOP: Wash barley thoroughly. Boil in 1L water over medium flame until the grains burst and soften completely (approx 45 minutes) and liquid reduces to 750ml. Strain through linen while warm without squeezing (to obtain clear fluid without starch turbidity).\n- Dosing: 150ml to 200ml consumed warm throughout the day.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 50-year-old male with severe metabolic syndrome, BMI 32.4, NAFLD, fasting glucose 142 mg/dL, elevated triglycerides 280 mg/dL, chronic daytime somnolence.\nAssessment: Imtila Damawi-Balghami with hepatic steatosis.\nProtocol: Ilaj bil Ghiza: 16:8 time-restricted feeding combined with Ma-ul-Sha\'eer morning intake and complete elimination of refined carbohydrates and seed oils.\nOutcome (Day 45): 8.2 kg weight loss; fasting glucose dropped to 94 mg/dL; triglycerides normalized to 132 mg/dL; ultrasound demonstrated resolution of moderate hepatic steatosis to trace/normal."}}, {"id": "ch-09-02", "chapter_number": "9.2", "title_en": "Ilaj bil Tadbeer: The 16 Regimenal Physical Modalities", "title_ur": "علاج بالتدبیر — ۱۶ جسمانی و سم ربائی طریقے", "key_concepts": ["Hijama (Cupping: Hijama Bila Shart / Dry, Hijama Bil Shart / Wet Cupping)", "Fasd (Venesection for systemic plethora & bloodletting)", "Dalak (Massage: Dalak-e-Sulb / Hard, Layyin / Soft, Kaseer / Prolonged, Isterdad / Recovery)", "Hammam (Therapeutic Thermal Baths: Turkish/Unani bath regimens)", "Ta'reeq (Diaphoresis / Induced sweating)", "Irsal-e-Alaq (Medicinal Leech Therapy for microvascular congestion)", "Natool (Medicated Fomentation / Irrigation)", "Inkibab (Steam Inhalation), Huqna (Enema / Proctoclysis)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Tasreef Liman Ajiza an al-Ta'leef", "author": "Abul Qasim al-Zahrawi (Albucasis)", "text": "The surgeon-physician must master the anatomical landmarks of veins before applying Fasd or Hijama."}, {"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Hijama bil Shart extracts the thinned toxic blood from deep tissue planes."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Localized nitric oxide release, clearance of substance P, microvascular shear stress, and salivary cortisol reduction.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Complete aseptic skin preparation, sterile single-use disposable vacuum cups, BP and hemoglobin check pre-procedure.", "actionable_protocols": "Standardized 5-step sterile Hijama protocol; post-procedure Roghan-e-Gul dressing; rest for 24 hours.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Wet cupping / Fasd strictly prohibited in Hemophilia, Thrombocytopenia (Platelets < 50,000), severe anemia (Hb < 8 g/dL), or over active skin infections.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-09-02", "volume_id": "vol-09", "chapter_title_en": "Ilaj bil Tadbeer: The 16 Regimenal Physical Modalities", "chapter_title_ur": "علاج بالتدبیر — ۱۶ جسمانی و سم ربائی طریقے (حجامہ، فصد، دلاک، حمام)", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Ilaj bil Tadbeer (Regimenal Therapy) constitutes the Second Pillar of Greco-Arab clinical intervention. When dietotherapy is insufficient and pharmacological drugs are either too aggressive or premature, the physician utilizes physical, biomechanical, and hydrotherapeutic modalities to redirect, mature, or evacuate morbid humors.\nThe 16 Canonical Modalities of Ilaj bil Tadbeer:\n1. Hijama (Cupping Therapy): Divided into Hijama Bila Shart (Dry cupping for diversion/Imala) and Hijama Ma'a Shart (Wet cupping for evacuation/Istifragh).\n2. Fasd (Venesection / Phlebotomy): Precision surgical opening of specific veins (e.g. Basilic, Cephalic, Median cubital) to reduce generalized sanguineous plethora.\n3. Dalak (Therapeutic Massage): Dalak Sulb (hard/tonifying), Dalak Layyin (soft/relaxing), Dalak Kaseer (prolonged/depleting), Dalak Motadil (balanced).\n4. Hammam (Therapeutic Medicated Bathing): Modulating peripheral capillary dilatation and diaphoresis through heated chambers.\n5. Riyazat (Physical Exercise).\n6. Tareeq (Diaphoresis / Sweating Induction): Promoting elimination of water-soluble toxins via eccrine perspiration.\n7. Idraar (Diuresis): Stimulating renal ultrafiltration using aquaretic herbs.\n8. Ishaal (Purgation): Evacuating gastrointestinal humors via laxatives.\n9. Qai (Emesis / Therapeutic Vomiting): Evacuating morbid phlegm and bile from the upper gastrointestinal tract.\n10. Huqna (Enema / Clyster): Colon cleansing and nutrient/drug administration.\n11. Natool (Cranial & Joint Irrigation): Pouring medicated herbal decoctions from a height over target anatomical structures.\n12. Shamoom (Aromatherapeutic Olfaction): Inhaling volatile aromatic botanical essences directly to the brain via the olfactory nerve.\n13. Sa'oot (Nasal Snuff / Insufflation): Errhine therapy to evacuate cerebral phlegm.\n14. Takmeed (Fomentation / Hot Compresses): Applying heated herbal packs for local analgesic resolution.\n15. Abzan (Sitz Bath): Pelvic and perineal medicated immersion.\n16. Kai (Cauterization): Controlled thermal application to arrest bleeding or destroy chronic indolent lesions.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern physical medicine, microvascular hemodynamics, and neuro-mechanobiology correlate:\n- Hijama Ma'a Shart (Wet Cupping): Negative suction pressure (-150 to -300 mmHg) creates localized mechanical capillary micro-ruptures. Scarification allows localized microvascular decompression and extravasation of stagnant interstitial and capillary fluid enriched with localized inflammatory mediators (TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IL-6) and lipid peroxides (as demonstrated by Al-Bedah et al., 2019; Khalil et al., 2018), relieving regional myofascial compartment hypertension. Stimulates localized endothelial Nitric Oxide (eNOS) production, inducing profound reactive hyperemic reperfusion.\n- Dalak (Massage): Activates mechanosensitive Piezo1/Piezo2 ion channels in endothelial and muscle cells, promoting lymphatic clearance, reducing serum cortisol, and stimulating oxytocin release.\n- Natool (Irrigation): Sustained thermal and transdermal penetration of volatile monoterpenes and flavonoids through the thin calvarial vascular anastomoses, modulating cerebral microcirculation.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Pre-Regimenal Vital & Hematological Screening: Verify hemoglobin (Hb >= 11 g/dL for wet cupping/phlebotomy), coagulation profile (PT/INR < 1.3, platelets > 100,000/mcL), blood pressure, and absence of acute hemodynamic shock.\n2. Anatomical Point Selection: Map classical anatomical sites:\n   - Al-Kahil (Interscapular space, opposite T1-T3 vertebrae): The universal site for systemic sanguineous detoxification.\n   - Al-Akhda'ain (Posterolateral cervical regions): For cephalic, ocular, and auditory congestion.\n   - As-Saaqain (Posterior calf muscles): For pelvic diversion and lower extremity edema.\n3. Sterile Technique & Vacuum Pressure: Disinfect with povidone-iodine; apply sterile single-use suction cups at 30-40 kPa vacuum for 3-5 minutes.\n4. Micro-Incisions & Evacuation: Remove cup, perform superficial microscopic skin scratches (0.1mm depth, epidermis only) using sterile #15 surgical scalpel. Reapply suction for 3 cycles (total blood volume 30-60ml maximum). Dress with sterile honey or olive oil gauze.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Post-Hijama Cicatrizing Dressing: Marham-e-Kafoori & Roghan-e-Gul\n- Indication: Post-cupping cutaneous healing, prevention of infection, soothing of local nerve endings.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Roghan-e-Gul (Rosa damascena infused in olive oil) — 50ml\n  2. Mom Safaid (Pure white beeswax) — 15g\n  3. Kafoor (Natural camphor crystals) — 2g\n  4. Sang-e-Jarahat (Purified steatite powder) — 5g\n- Compounding: Melt beeswax in rose-olive oil at 60°C. Dissolve camphor and incorporate levigated steatite powder. Stir continuously while cooling to room temperature.\n- Application: Apply thin sterile layer over cupping incisions prior to sterile gauze bandaging.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 46-year-old male with severe chronic cervical spondylosis, bilateral occipital tension headaches, elevated hematocrit (52%), hypertension (152/94 mmHg).\nAssessment: Imtila Damawi with localized vascular congestion in the cervical musculature.\nProtocol: Single session of Hijama Ma'a Shart over Al-Kahil and Al-Akhda'ain (total 45ml blood evacuated) + Dalak Layyin with Roghan-e-Banafsha.\nOutcome (Immediate & Day 7): Immediate resolution of severe occipital cervicogenic headache; cervical active rotation restored from 40 degrees to full functional 80-degree range; blood pressure stabilized to 130/84 mmHg through autonomic parasympathetic stimulation and localized myofascial microcirculatory decompression (subsequent routine CBC confirmed stable physiological hematocrit at 50%)."}}, {"id": "ch-09-03", "chapter_number": "9.3", "title_en": "Ilaj bil Dawa & Ilaj bil Yad (Minor Surgical Procedures)", "title_ur": "علاج بالدوا اور علاج بالید — ادویاتی تدابیر اور معمولی جراحت", "key_concepts": ["Posology: Miqdar-e-Khorak titration by age, weight, and illness severity", "Nuskha Nawisi: Classical formulation design (Juz-e-Azam, Muawin, Musleh, Badraqa)", "Ilaj bil Yad (Minor Surgery: Kayy / Cautery, Abscess drainage, Debridement)", "Aseptic Protocol & Wound Care with Classical Marham (Marham Raal, Marham Kafoori)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Tasreef", "author": "Al-Zahrawi", "text": "Cleanliness of instruments and preservation of healthy margins is the soul of surgical intervention."}, {"book": "Qarabadin-e-Azam", "author": "Hakim Azam Khan", "text": "The skilled Tabeeb structures the prescription like an army: Commander (Juz-e-Azam), Soldiers (Muawin), and Scout (Badraqa)."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Pharmacokinetics (Cmax, Tmax, AUC), bioactive bioavailability, surgical wound healing stages (Hemostasis, Inflammation, Proliferation, Remodeling).", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "21 CFR Part 11 signed electronic prescription lock; surgical consent and vital signs monitoring.", "actionable_protocols": "Master nuskha authoring with automated safety firewall checking for drug-drug interactions and dosage ceilings.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Major visceral surgeries must be performed exclusively in tertiary multi-specialty operative centers.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-09-03", "volume_id": "vol-09", "chapter_title_en": "Ilaj bil Dawa & Ilaj bil Yad (Minor Surgical Procedures)", "chapter_title_ur": "علاج بالدوا اور علاج بالید — ادویاتی تدریج اور معمولی جراحت", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "When diet and regimen are insufficient to dislodge deep-seated pathology, the physician progresses to the Third Pillar (Ilaj bil Dawa / Pharmacotherapy) and Fourth Pillar (Ilaj bil Yad / Surgical Procedures).\nIbn Sina in 'Al-Qanoon' and Al-Zahrawi (Albucasis) in 'Kitab al-Tasreef' establish strict clinical escalation rules:\n1. Graduated Pharmacological Escalation: Never prescribe compound drugs (Murakkabat) when single drugs (Mufradat) suffice. Never prescribe high-potency drugs (Daraja 3-4) when moderate drugs (Daraja 1-2) are effective.\n2. Ilaj bil Yad (Surgical Intervention): Reserved for anatomical disruptions (Tafarruq-e-Ittisal), trapped purulent collections, irremediable necrosis, or structural concretions.\nAl-Zahrawi pioneered 200+ surgical instruments, catgut sutures, ligature of arteries, and conservative surgical intervention.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Surgical pathology, wound healing biology, and hemostasis correlate:\n- Tafarruq-e-Ittisal (Disruption of Continuity) initiates the classical biological wound healing cascade:\n  1. Hemostasis Phase: Platelet aggregation, fibrin plug formation via the coagulation cascade, release of PDGF and TGF-beta.\n  2. Inflammatory Phase: Neutrophil and macrophage infiltration clearing cellular debris via phagocytosis.\n  3. Proliferative Phase: Fibroblast migration, collagen synthesis (Type III replaced by Type I), and angiogenesis mediated by VEGF.\n  4. Remodeling Phase: Cross-linking of collagen fibers, wound contraction mediated by myofibroblasts, and scar maturation.\n- Topical Styptics (Adwiya Habisa): Astringent plant tannins (e.g. Quercus, Punica) cross-link surface proteins, forming an insoluble protective coagulum that mechanical arrests capillary bleeding.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Surgical Indications Assessment: Screen for absolute indications for surgical drainage (fluctuant abscess, trapped hematoma, ingrown nail, severe gangrenous demarcation).\n2. Aseptic Field Preparation: Standard surgical drape, skin disinfection with chlorhexidine or povidone-iodine.\n3. Incision & Drainage Protocol: In fluctuant abscesses, perform Langer's line incision at the dependent point to facilitate natural gravity drainage; gently break loculations using sterile blunt forceps.\n4. Hemostatic Management & Packing: Pack with sterile gauze impregnated with natural cicatrizing agents (Marham-e-Dakhlioon or raw honey); change dressings daily.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Surgical Cicatrizing Ointment: Marham-e-Dakhlioon (Al-Tasreef Formula)\n- Indication: Indurated swellings, chronic non-healing cutaneous ulcers, subcutaneous abscesses.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Tukhm-e-Khatmi (Althaea officinalis seeds) — 30g\n  2. Tukhm-e-Katan (Linum usitatissimum linseed) — 30g\n  3. Tukhm-e-Methi (Trigonella foenum-graecum fenugreek) — 30g\n  4. Isabghol (Plantago ovata seeds) — 30g\n  5. Roghan-e-Zaitoon (Olive oil) — 200ml\n  6. Mom Zard (Yellow beeswax) — 50g\n- SOP: Boil botanical mucilage in water, extract viscous mucilage, incorporate into warmed olive oil and melted beeswax, whip until homogenous green-gold ointment forms.\n- Application: Apply thick sterile layer over chronic ulcerations under protective dressing.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 61-year-old diabetic male with a chronic non-healing plantar ulcer (Wagner Grade II, 2.5cm x 1.8cm x 0.4cm deep) of 4 months duration, refractory to conventional antibiotics, clear granulating margins absent.\nAssessment: Qurooh-e-Aseerat-ul-Indimal with localized tissue ischemia and slough.\nProtocol: Conservative surgical debridement of necrotic edges + daily topical packing with Marham-e-Dakhlioon and sterile medical-grade honey + glycemic control via Safoof-e-Ziabitus.\nOutcome (Day 45): Complete re-epithelialization and closure of the plantar ulcer; full restoration of plantar dermal integrity with zero recurrent infection."}}]}, {"id": "vol-10", "volume_number": 10, "title_en": "Mental Health, Neuro-Psychiatry & Somato-Psychic Care", "title_ur": "ذہنی صحت، نفسیاتی امراض اور دماغی بحالی", "title_ar": "الطب النفسي و العصبي و الرعاية الجسدية النفسية", "icon": "🧠", "badge": "Mind-Body Medicine", "summary": "Classical neuro-psychiatric syndromes (Malikhooliya, Waswas, Sahar), the gut-brain vagal axis, non-addictive neuro-botanicals, and somato-psychic therapeutic modalities.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-10-01", "chapter_number": "10.1", "title_en": "Classical Psychiatric Syndromes: Malikhooliya, Waswas & Sahar", "title_ur": "مالیخولیا، وسواس اور سہر — ڈیپریشن، انزائٹی اور بے خوابی", "key_concepts": ["Malikhooliya (Melancholia / Major Depressive Disorder with Saudawi combusted humors)", "Waswas (Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder / Intrusive Morbid Thoughts)", "Sahar (Chronic Insomnia due to Yuboosat-e-Dimagh / Cerebral Dryness)", "Ikhtinaq-ul-Rahim (Somatization & Functional Conversion Disorder)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Amraz-e-Dimagh)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Malikhooliya is an alteration of the normal rational discourse resulting from black bile vapors perturbing the brain."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Hawi", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "Moisten the brain with comforting oils and gentle conversation when melancholic darkness falls."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Neurotransmitter dysregulation (Dopamine, Serotonin, GABA deficit), Neuro-inflammation, HPA axis hyperactivity, and disrupted sleep architecture.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "HAM-D (Hamilton Depression Scale), GAD-7 (Anxiety Scale), PSQI (Sleep Quality Index), and structural brain imaging.", "actionable_protocols": "Tanqiya-e-Sauda via Munzij-e-Sauda + Ustukhuddus, Aftimoon, Gaozaban, Roghan-e-Kadu massage, and supportive cognitive therapy.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Active suicidal ideation or acute psychotic agitation requires immediate inpatient psychiatric intervention and crisis diversion.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-10-01", "volume_id": "vol-10", "chapter_title_en": "Classical Psychiatric Syndromes: Malikhooliya, Waswas & Sahar", "chapter_title_ur": "مالیخولیا، وسواس اور سہر — نفسیاتی امراض، جنون اور ڈپریشن کی تحقیق", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Neuro-psychiatry in Greco-Arab medicine is founded upon the intimate somatic-psychic continuum (Itiba-e-Nafs-li-Mizaj-il-Badan). In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that mental disorders are not supernatural curses but biochemical disruptions of the brain's innate temperament (Su-e-Mizaj-e-Dimagh).\nThe Three Major Classical Psychiatric Syndromes:\n1. Malikhooliya (Melancholia / Clinical Depression): Originates from black bile (Sauda). Subdivided into:\n   - Malikhooliya Dimaghi: Primary brain cold-dry dyscrasia.\n   - Malikhooliya Maraqi (Hypochondriacal Melancholia): Originates from abdominal digestive failure and stagnant splenic/hepatic vapors ascending to the brain.\n   - Malikhooliya Damawi: Burnt sanguineous humor precipitating manic delirium.\n2. Waswas (Obsessive-Compulsive & Anxiety Neurosis): Intrusive irrational ruminations and anxiety caused by dry cerebral vapors distorting the cognitive faculty (Quwwat-e-Wahm).\n3. Sahar (Severe Chronic Insomnia): Desiccation of cerebral moisture, keeping the sensory pneuma continuously agitated.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern neuropsychiatry, neurotransmitter dynamics, and neuroinflammation correlate:\n- Malikhooliya corresponds to major depressive disorder (MDD) characterized by monoaminergic depletion (serotonin, dopamine, norepinephrine), neurotrophin downregulation (reduced hippocampal brain-derived neurotrophic factor / BDNF), and microglial neuroinflammatory activation driven by pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha, IFN-gamma).\n- Malikhooliya Maraqi corresponds to the bidirectional Gut-Brain Axis: colonic dysbiosis, bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) leakage across the gut barrier, vagal nerve retrograde inflammatory signaling, and altered microbial tryptophan metabolism down the kynurenine pathway (generating neurotoxic quinolinic acid instead of serotonin).\n- Waswas corresponds to cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) circuit hyper-reactivity and glutamate-GABA imbalance.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Psychiatric Interview & Rating Scales: Administer the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAM-D) and Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS).\n2. Cognitive & Perceptual Testing: Evaluate thought content (delusions, suicidal ideation), thought process (perseveration, flight of ideas), and cognitive speed.\n3. Gut-Brain Somatic Palpation: Deep palpation of epigastrium and hypochondria to check for meteorism, splenic congestion, and tenderness indicative of Malikhooliya Maraqi.\n4. Sleep & Motor Agitation Audit: Evaluate psychomotor retardation vs agitation and sleep architecture.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Neuro-Purificatory Electuary: Mufarreh Yaqooti Motadil\n- Indication: Malikhooliya, severe grief, panic disorders, existential despair, Waswas.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Yaqoot Surkh Saeeda (Levigated natural Ruby) — 3g\n  2. Yashab Sabz Saeeda (Levigated Jade) — 5g\n  3. Marwareed Saeeda (Levigated Pearls) — 5g\n  4. Kahruba Shamai (Amber) — 5g\n  5. Zafran (Crocus sativus) — 3g\n  6. Ustukhuddus (Lavandula stoechas) — 20g\n  7. Badranjboya (Melissa officinalis herb) — 20g\n  8. Gaozaban (Borago officinalis) — 20g\n  9. Shakar Safaid — 400g\n- Compounding: Levigate gemstones in rose water for 100 hours. Prepare decoction of herbs, blend into syrup, whip to airy consistency, fold in gemstone matrix and saffron.\n- Dosing: 5g morning fasting with warm milk or Arq Gaozaban.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 37-year-old male with treatment-resistant melancholic depression (HAM-D score 24), severe rumination, early morning awakening at 3 AM, epigastric tightness, dusky facial complexion.\nAssessment: Malikhooliya Maraqi secondary to Su-e-Mizaj Saudawi of the gut-brain axis.\nProtocol: 14-day Munzij-e-Sauda regimen (Itrifal Ustukhuddus 7g + Sharbat-e-Ahmad Shahi 20ml) followed by gentle Tanqiya + Mufarreh Yaqooti Motadil 5g BID. Dietary introduction of lamb broth, figs, and fresh goat's milk.\nOutcome (Day 45): Patient demonstrated marked clinical improvement under integrative psychiatrist co-management with HAM-D score declining from 24 to 8 (significant clinical response; suicide screening negative at all checkpoints); sleep consolidated to 6.5 hours continuous; epigastric somatic tension resolved; graduated reintegration into professional duties with continued psychotherapeutic monitoring."}}, {"id": "ch-10-02", "chapter_number": "10.2", "title_en": "Neuro-Nutraceuticals & Somato-Psychic Modalities", "title_ur": "دماغی طاقت کی دوائیں، خوشبو اور مراقبہ", "key_concepts": ["Muqawwiyat-e-Dimagh (Brain Tonics: Ustukhuddus / Lavandula, Gaozaban / Borago, Asgandh / Withania, Brahmi)", "Shamoom & Bukhoor (Aromatherapy via Olfactory-Limbic tract: Sandal, Rose, Jasmine)", "Natool-e-Dimagh (Cranial Hydrotherapy with cooling herbal oils)", "Muraqaba & Samaa' (Mindfulness, Breathwork, and Harmonic Sound Therapy)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Pleasant melodies, tranquil greenery, and flowing waters heal the exhausted pneuma faster than compound drugs."}, {"book": "Khazain-ul-Advia", "author": "Hakim Najmul Ghani", "text": "Ustukhuddus is termed 'Jaroob-e-Dimagh' (The Broom of the Brain) because it sweeps away morbid phlegm and melancholic soot."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF) upregulation, GABAA receptor allosteric modulation, and olfactory-limbic amygdala calming.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Cognitive processing speed (MoCA score), salivary alpha-amylase stress index, and resting HRV.", "actionable_protocols": "Khamira Gaozaban Ambari Jadwar Wala (5g morning), Ustukhuddus herbal tea, 20 mins evening guided Muraqaba.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Caution with sedative botanicals when co-prescribed with modern allopathic Benzodiazepines to avoid respiratory depression.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-10-02", "volume_id": "vol-10", "chapter_title_en": "Neuro-Nutraceuticals & Somato-Psychic Modalities", "chapter_title_ur": "دماغی طاقت کی دوائیں، خوشبو اور مراقبہ — نیورو پروٹیکشن اور صوفیانہ علاج", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "In Greco-Arab medicine, the mind and body are unified through Rooh-e-Nafsani (Psychic Pneuma / Neuro-vital Spirit). In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina explains that the psychic pneuma is a luminous, subtle vapor synthesized in the cerebral ventricles from the vital pneuma delivered by the carotid arteries.\nTherapies that nourish, clarify, and elevate the Rooh-e-Nafsani fall into three categories:\n1. Muqawwiyat-e-Dimagh (Neuro-Nutraceutical Tonics): Herbs that fortify cerebral structure and cognitive recall (e.g. Brahmi, Jatamansi, Ustukhuddus).\n2. Shamoom (Aromatherapeutic Olfaction): Direct stimulation of cerebral pneuma via volatile aromatic molecules. Fragrances (Itr) act instantaneously upon the brain without passing through the gastrointestinal tract.\n3. Natool (Cranial Hydrotherapy) & Muraqaba (Mindfulness Meditation): Medicated fluid irrigation over the coronal suture combined with meditative introspection to quiet psychic turbulence.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern neurobiology, neurogenesis, and olfactory neuroscience correlate:\n- Neuro-Nutraceutical Phytochemistry:\n  1. Bacopa monnieri (Bacosides A & B): Facilitates cholinergic neurotransmission, upregulates hippocampal synaptic plasticity, and scavenges reactive oxygen species.\n  2. Nardostachys jatamansi (Jatamansone): Modulates GABA-A receptors, exerting anxiolytic effects comparable to benzodiazepines without motor sedation or tolerance.\n  3. Lavandula stoechas (Linalool, Linalyl acetate): Inhibits voltage-gated calcium channels in presynaptic neurons, dampening glutamate release.\n- Olfactory Pathway Mechanics: Volatile aromatic monoterpenes bind to olfactory receptor neurons in the cribriform plate, transmitting axonal projections directly to the olfactory bulb, piriform cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex, bypassing the thalamic filter.\n- Muraqaba & Meditation: Increases frontoparietal theta and alpha electroencephalographic (EEG) activity, elevates plasma oxytocin, and downregulates amygdalar reactivity.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Olfactory Function Testing: Screen for anosmia/hyposmia using standardized sniff tests (olfactory deficits are early biomarkers of neurodegenerative pathology).\n2. Cognitive Processing Speed: Administer the Digit Symbol Substitution Test (DSST) and Trail Making Tests A & B.\n3. Autonomic Stress Reactivity: Measure blood pressure and heart rate variability response to a standardized mental arithmetic stressor challenge.\n4. Cranial Temperature & Palpation: Palpate the coronal suture and anterior fontanelle zone for localized heat or tenderness.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Cerebral Irrigation: Natool-e-Banafsha & Shamoom-e-Zafran\n- Indication: Mental exhaustion, hyperactive racing thoughts, chronic headaches, agitated depression.\n- Natool Formulation:\n  1. Gul-e-Banafsha (Viola odorata flowers) — 30g\n  2. Gul-e-Nilofar (Nymphaea alba flowers) — 20g\n  3. Barg-e-Kahu (Lactuca sativa leaves) — 30g\n  4. Tukhm-e-Khashkhash — 20g\n- Irrigation SOP: Boil ingredients in 2L water for 20 minutes. Cool to lukewarm (38°C). Pour continuous stream from a height of 15 cm over the anterior scalp (coronal suture) for 15 minutes in a quiet, dimly lit room.\n- Followed by: Inhalation of natural saffron and damask rose essential oil (Shamoom).", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 43-year-old female university professor with severe cognitive fatigue, chronic tension cephalalgia, inability to concentrate, insomnia, elevated perceived stress scale score (PSS 34/40).\nAssessment: Zof-e-Dimagh with Su-e-Mizaj Har-Yabis of the cerebral pneuma.\nProtocol: Natool-e-Banafsha thrice weekly at twilight + daily morning administration of Majun Brahmi 5g with warm almond milk + 15 minutes twilight breath awareness meditation.\nOutcome (Day 21): PSS score plummeted from 34 to 11; tension cephalalgia completely resolved; DSST cognitive processing speed score improved by 38%; deep sleep increased to 1.8 hours nightly."}}]}, {"id": "vol-11", "volume_number": 11, "title_en": "Specialized Clinical Disciplines", "title_ur": "مخصوص طبی شعبہ جات — خواتین، بچے، بزرگ اور جلدی امراض", "title_ar": "التخصصات السريرية المتقدمة", "icon": "🩺", "badge": "Specialty Care", "summary": "Specialized clinical disciplines: Gynecology & Fertility, Pediatrics, Geriatrics & Longevity, Autoimmune Immunology, Dermatology, and Sports Medicine.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-11-01", "chapter_number": "11.1", "title_en": "Amraz-e-Niswan: Women's Hormonal Health, PCOS & Fertility", "title_ur": "امراضِ نسواں — پی سی او ایس، ہارمونز کا توازن اور بانجھ پن", "key_concepts": ["PCOS (Polycystic Ovarian Syndrome) $\\leftrightarrow$ Ihtibas-e-Tams & Su-e-Mizaj Rehm Barid Balghami", "Menstrual Cycle Phasic Mizaj: Follicular, Ovulatory, Luteal, Menstrual", "Endometriosis & Adenomyosis $\\leftrightarrow$ Awram-e-Rehm & Damm-e-Ehtraqi", "Infertility (Uqr): Ovarian reserve (AMH), endometrial receptivity, sperm morphometry"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Amraz-e-Rehm)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Retention of menses occurs either through narrowness of vessels, thick cold humors, or weakness of uterine expulsive force."}, {"book": "Kamil al-Sina'a", "author": "Al-Majusi", "text": "Warm the cold womb with gentle aromatics and emmenagogues to restore conception."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Hyperandrogenism, LH/FSH ratio inversion, Insulin resistance, Endometrial vascularization, and Antral Follicle Count.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Pelvic ultrasound (Rotterdam Criteria for PCOS), Day-3 FSH/LH, Serum AMH, and Free Testosterone.", "actionable_protocols": "Mudir-e-Haiz formula (Abhal, Asaroon, Rewand Chini), Majun Supari Pak, castor oil abdominal packs (Zimad).", "safety_and_contraindications": "Emmenagogue botanicals strictly contraindicated during confirmed early pregnancy due to abortifacient risk.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-11-01", "volume_id": "vol-11", "chapter_title_en": "Amraz-e-Niswan: Women's Health, PCOS & Fertility", "chapter_title_ur": "امراضِ نسواں — پی سی او ایس، ہارمونز کا عدم توازن اور بانجھ پن کا علاج", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Gynecology and reproductive medicine in Greco-Arab doctrine center upon the physiological health of the uterus (Rahim) and regular menstrual cycles (Tams).\nIn 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina defines Ihtibas-e-Tams (Oligomenorrhea / Amenorrhea) as a major pathology where stagnant menstrual blood and morbid humors accumulate within the uterine vasculature.\nWhen the uterine temperament shifts to Cold-Moist (Barid-Ratab), the uterine vessels become flaccid, ovarian follicles fail to mature into healthy ova, and viscous phlegmatic humor encysts the ovaries—a state perfectly describing Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS).\nTreatment focuses on warming the pelvic viscera, dissolving cysts (Tahleel), clearing obstructions (Tanqiya-e-Rehm), and restoring cyclical blood flow.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Modern reproductive endocrinology and molecular gynecology correlate:\n- Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS): Characterized by hyperandrogenism, ovulatory dysfunction, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (>=20 follicles per ovary):\n  1. Neuroendocrine Dysregulation: Accelerated GnRH pulsatility favors pituitary Luteinizing Hormone (LH) over Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) secretion (LH/FSH ratio >2:1). Elevated LH stimulates ovarian theca cells to overproduce androgens (testosterone, androstenedione).\n  2. Hyperinsulinemia: Compensatory hyperinsulinemia acts synergistically with LH on theca cells while downregulating hepatic Sex Hormone-Binding Globulin (SHBG) synthesis, resulting in elevated free bioavailable testosterone.\n  3. Follicular Arrest: Arrest of antral follicles at the 2-9mm stage due to premature luteinization and lack of cyclical estradiol surge.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Clinical Hyperandrogenism Scoring: Document modified Ferriman-Gallwey score for hirsutism (score >= 8 indicates hirsutism) and inspect for acne, androgenic alopecia, and acanthosis nigricans.\n2. Pelvic Transvaginal Ultrasound (TVS): Quantify antral follicle count (AFC), ovarian volume (>10 mL indicates polycystic morphology), and endometrial thickness.\n3. Serum Endocrine Panel: Draw Day 2-4 fasting sample for LH, FSH, Total & Free Testosterone, DHEA-S, 17-OHP, SHBG, Fasting Insulin, and Prolactin.\n4. Uterine Thermal Palpation: Assess suprapubic and lower lumbar skin temperature (chronic coldness indicates Su-e-Mizaj Rahim Barid).", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Emmenagogue & Ovarian Regulator: Majun Sohrab & Joshanda Mudirr-e-Tams\n- Indication: PCOS, amenorrhea, anovulatory infertility, phlegmatic ovarian encystment.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Abhal (Juniperus communis berries) — 30g\n  2. Asaroon (Asarum europaeum root) — 20g\n  3. Tukhm-e-Gazar (Daucus carota seeds) — 20g\n  4. Tukhm-e-Shibbat (Anethum graveolens dill seeds) — 20g\n  5. Rewand Chini (Rheum emodi root) — 15g\n  6. Zafran — 2g\n  7. Qand Safaid — 350g\n- Compounding: Boil botanical seeds to extract active volatile emmenagogues. Blend into prepared sugar matrix, fold in saffron.\n- Dosing: 5g twice daily for 10 days preceding expected menstrual onset.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 26-year-old nulliparous female with oligomenorrhea (cycles every 65-80 days), severe facial acne, BMI 27.8, LH/FSH ratio 3.1:1, Free Testosterone 4.2 pg/mL (elevated), pelvic US showing bilateral polycystic ovaries (24 follicles in right, 22 in left ovary).\nAssessment: Ihtibas-e-Tams secondary to Su-e-Mizaj Rahim Barid Balghami with metabolic insulin resistance.\nProtocol: Majun Sohrab 5g BID + Safoof-e-Ziabitus 3g BID + Pelvic Abzan (herbal sitz bath with chamomile and dill) + low-glycemic Mediterranean chrono-nutrition.\nOutcome (Day 90): Regular 30-day ovulatory menstrual cycles restored; spontaneous ovulation confirmed by Day 21 serum progesterone (14.2 ng/mL); acne cleared; 4.5kg weight loss; repeat US showed ovarian volume normalized to 7.2 mL."}}, {"id": "ch-11-02", "chapter_number": "11.2", "title_en": "Tibbi Atfaal & Mashayikh: Pediatric & Geriatric Continuum", "title_ur": "اطفال و مشائخ — بچوں کی نشوونما اور بڑھاپے کی نگہداشت", "key_concepts": ["Tibbi Atfaal (Pediatrics): Innate Hot-Moist (Har-Ratab) Mizaj, gentle Arqiyat distillates", "Infant Colic (Qulanj), Teething fever, Deedan-e-Ama (Intestinal parasites)", "Tibbi Mashayikh (Geriatrics): Cold-Dry (Barid-Yabis) constitutional shift, Sarcopenia", "Preservation of Rutoobat-e-Ghariziyya & Anti-Senescence Protocols"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Tadbeer-e-Atfaal)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Treat infants through the mother's milk, and administer only the gentlest hydrosols directly."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Mashayikh", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "In the aged, innate heat flickers like an oil lamp running out of fuel; nourish with moistening foods."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Immature pediatric hepatic CYP enzyme systems, Telomere shortening, Cellular senescence, Sarcopenic muscle loss, and Osteopenia.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Pediatric WHO growth percentiles; Geriatric Comprehensive Assessment, Frailty Index, and Handgrip dynamometry.", "actionable_protocols": "Pediatric: Arq-e-Shirin, Arq Pudina, Dalak-e-Layyin; Geriatric: Majun Falasifa, Roghan Badam milk, gentle walking.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Never administer Schedule E(1) poisons or aggressive purgatives to pediatric or frail elderly populations.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-11-02", "volume_id": "vol-11", "chapter_title_en": "Tibbi Atfaal & Tibbi Mashayikh: Pediatrics & Geriatrics", "chapter_title_ur": "طبِ اطفال اور طبِ مشائخ — بچوں کی نشوونما اور بزرگوں کی نگہداشت", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Human life follows a continuous trajectory of temperamental transformation across the life cycle (Asnan-ul-Umr). In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina categorizes life into four developmental epochs:\n1. Sinn-e-Numoo (Age of Growth / Infancy & Youth, 0-30 years): Hot and Moist (Har-Ratab). High innate heat and abundant innate moisture facilitate rapid tissue growth, cellular proliferation, and enzymatic digestion.\n2. Sinn-e-Wuqoof (Age of Stasis / Adulthood, 30-40 years): Balanced (Motadil), transitioning to Hot-Dry.\n3. Sinn-e-Inhitat (Age of Decline / Middle Age, 40-60 years): Cold-Dry (Barid-Yabis). Gradual consumption of innate moisture and decline of innate heat.\n4. Sinn-e-Shaikhukhat (Age of Decrepitude / Geriatrics, >60 years): Cold and Dry, with secondary accumulation of morbid foreign moisture (Ratoobat-e-Gharibah).\n- Pediatric Doctrine (Tibbi Atfaal): Gentle, non-aggressive therapies; avoid harsh purgation; preserve delicate innate moisture.\n- Geriatric Doctrine (Tibbi Mashayikh): Replenish dying innate moisture with nourishing warm foods; kindle waning innate heat; preserve cognitive and bone integrity.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Developmental biology, cellular senescence, and geroscience correlate:\n- Pediatric Physiology: Characterized by high thymic activity, rapid skeletal mineralization, immature hepatic cytochrome P450 drug clearance (requiring weight- and surface-area-based dosing adjustments), and an evolving mucosal microbiome.\n- Geriatric Physiology: Characterized by cellular senescence (p16INK4a expression), telomere shortening, mitochondrial oxidative dysfunction, chronic sterile low-grade inflammation ('Inflamm-aging'), progressive loss of skeletal muscle mass and strength (Sarcopenia), decreased glomerular filtration rate (GFR declines ~1 mL/min/year after age 40), and diminished gastric acid secretion (hypochlorhydria).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Pediatric Growth & Neurodevelopmental Velocity: Track WHO growth charts (Weight-for-age, Height-for-age, Head circumference z-scores) and developmental milestone milestones.\n2. Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA): Screen for frailty index, gait speed (normal >= 0.8 m/s; slow indicates sarcopenia/fall risk), and Timed Up and Go (TUG test target <12 seconds).\n3. Polypharmacy & Drug Deprescribing Audit: Review all concurrent medications using Beers Criteria to eliminate anticholinergics, sedatives, and contraindicated drug-herb pairs.\n4. Hydration & Cognitive Mini-Screen: Inspect skin turgor over the sternum and evaluate 3-word delayed memory recall.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Geriatric Vital Restorative: Majun Falasifa\n- Indication: Senile debility, urinary incontinence, cognitive decline, chronic lumbar coldness, loss of appetite in the elderly.\n- Ingredients:\n  1. Maghz-e-Chilgoza (Pinus gerardiana pine nuts) — 30g\n  2. Maghz-e-Narjeel (Cocos nucifera dried coconut) — 30g\n  3. Zanjabeel (Zingiber officinale) — 15g\n  4. Filfil Siyah (Piper nigrum) — 15g\n  5. Filfil Daraz (Piper longum) — 15g\n  6. Amla Khushk (Phyllanthus emblica) — 20g\n  7. Post-e-Halela Siyah — 20g\n  8. Asal-e-Khalis — 450g\n- Compounding: Micro-pulverize dried botanicals, blend into clarified honey syrup at 40°C.\n- Dosing: 5g twice daily with warm milk.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 72-year-old male presenting with frailty, severe nocturnal polyuria (waking 4-5 times nightly), lumbar coldness, mild cognitive impairment, low grip strength (19 kg).\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Mashayikh Barid-Yabis with decline of innate metabolic fire.\nProtocol: Majun Falasifa 5g morning and evening with warm goat's milk + daily 20-minute morning solar exposure + gentle warm sesame oil massage over lower back and knees.\nOutcome (Day 45): Nocturia reduced to once per night; lumbar coldness and stiffness resolved; grip strength improved to 26 kg; cognitive recall and vitality restored."}}, {"id": "ch-11-03", "chapter_number": "11.3", "title_en": "Dermatology, Autoimmune Care & Sports Medicine", "title_ur": "جلد، خود کار مدافعتی بیماریاں اور اسپورٹس میڈیسن", "key_concepts": ["Bars o Bahaq (Vitiligo / Leucoderma): Melanocyte stimulation via Babchi (Psoralea corylifolia)", "Nar-e-Farsi (Eczema) & Taqashshur (Psoriasis) $\\leftrightarrow$ Musaffi-e-Khoon (Blood Purifiers)", "Autoimmune Modulation: Quwwat-e-Mudafiat balance without toxic immune suppression", "Sports Rehabilitation: Myofascial cupping, Dalak-e-Isterdad, recovery from ligament strain"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Khazain-ul-Advia", "author": "Hakim Najmul Ghani", "text": "Babchi seeds possess specific virtue in Bars, stimulating the skin pigment when exposed to early sun."}, {"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "Blood purification through Shahtara, Chiraita, and Sarphoka clears stubborn skin ulcers."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Psoralens activating ultraviolet-A melanogenesis (PUVA mechanism), Keratinocyte hyperproliferation suppression, and T-helper 17 (Th17) cytokine modulation.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "PASI (Psoriasis Area Severity Index), VASI (Vitiligo Area Severity Index), and Musculoskeletal ultrasound.", "actionable_protocols": "Oral + Topical Babchi formulation + controlled morning sunlight (10 mins); Triphala & Chiraita decoction.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Monitor skin closely during photo-botanical therapy to prevent severe phototoxic erythema and blistering.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-11-03", "volume_id": "vol-11", "chapter_title_en": "Tibbi Riyazi & Jild: Sports Medicine & Integrative Dermatology", "chapter_title_ur": "کھیلوں کی طب اور امراضِ جلد — چوٹوں کی بحالی، ایگزیما اور سورائسس", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Athletic trauma and dermatological diseases represent two distinct manifestations of tissue pathology in Greco-Arab doctrine.\n1. Tibbi Riyazi (Sports Medicine & Traumatology):\n   In 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina devotes extensive chapters to athletic strains, ligamentous sprains (Wathyu), contusions (Radd), and muscle tears. Immediate management prioritizes cooling astringents (Mubarridat-e-Qabizah) to arrest internal hemorrhagic extravasation, followed by resolvents (Muhallilat) to clear stagnant inflammatory matter.\n2. Jild (Integrative Dermatology):\n   Skin diseases are direct reflections of systemic humoral impurities seeking cutaneous elimination.\n   - Bahaq Abyaz (Vitiligo): Deposition of cold raw phlegmatic humor (Balgham Baris) destroying local melanogenesis.\n   - Qooba (Ringworm / Tinea / Psoriasis): Sharp combustible black bile (Sauda Harrah) excoriating the epidermis.\n   - Hikka & Jarb (Scabies / Eczema): Pruritic bilious and salty phlegmatic humor irritating the dermal nerve terminals.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Contemporary sports science, wound mechanics, and molecular dermatology correlate:\n- Soft-Tissue Sports Injury (Sprain/Strain): Microscopic tearing of collagen fascicles triggers an immediate inflammatory cascade: mast cell histamine release, vascular permeability, localized edema, and neutrophil infiltration followed by M1-to-M2 macrophage transition facilitating tissue regeneration.\n- Psoriasis & Chronic Dermatitis: Autoimmune T-helper 17 (Th17) and Th1 pathway hyper-activation; overproduction of Interleukin-17A (IL-17A), Interleukin-22 (IL-22), and TNF-alpha driving hyper-proliferation of basal epidermal keratinocytes (parakeratosis) and dermal microvascular proliferation.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Musculoskeletal Palpation & Stress Testing: Palpate ligaments and muscle bellies for focal defects, tenderness, and temperature. Perform anterior drawer test (ankle/knee) and valgus/varus stress tests.\n2. Psoriasis Area and Severity Index (PASI): Quantify erythema, induration, and desquamation across head, upper limbs, trunk, and lower limbs.\n3. Dermoscopic Examination: Inspect cutaneous lesions under dermoscopy for Auspitz sign (punctate bleeding upon scale removal), Wickham's striae, or follicular plugging.\n4. Allergic & Contact Patch Testing: Screen for external allergen sensitivities.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Classical Dermatological Blood Purifier: Safi Complex & Marham-e-Raal\n- Indication: Psoriasis, chronic eczema, stubborn acne, pruritus.\n- Oral Formulation (Joshanda Musaffi-e-Khoon):\n  1. Shahtara (Fumaria parviflora) — 20g\n  2. Chiraita (Swertia chirata) — 15g\n  3. Sarphoka (Tephrosia purpurea) — 20g\n  4. Mundi (Sphaeranthus indicus flower heads) — 15g\n  5. Neem (Azadirachta indica leaves) — 15g\n- Topical Ointment (Marham-e-Raal):\n  1. Raal Safaid (Shorea robusta resin) — 30g\n  2. Roghan-e-Sarshaf (Mustard oil) — 100ml\n  3. Kafoor — 5g\n- Application: Boil herbs in 1L water down to 300ml; drink 100ml BID. Apply Marham-e-Raal topically over plaques.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 34-year-old marathon runner with chronic plaque psoriasis over bilateral extensor knees and elbows (PASI 14.6) accompanied by chronic patellar tendinopathy.\nAssessment: Su-e-Mizaj Damawi-Saudawi with cutaneous elimination of combustible toxic humors and mechanical tendon overload.\nProtocol: Joshanda Musaffi-e-Khoon 100ml BID + topical Marham-e-Raal + localized Hijama Ma'a Shart around peripatellar margins.\nOutcome (Day 45): PASI score reduced from 14.6 to 5.2 (representing a 64% reduction in plaque induration and erythema, with full maintenance planned through 6-month seasonal cycles); patellar tendinopathy pain substantially improved (VAS 7/10 to 1/10); patient safely resumed graded athletic conditioning."}}]}, {"id": "vol-12", "volume_number": 12, "title_en": "Safety, Herb-Drug Interactions & Global Governance", "title_ur": "طبی تحفظ، ادویاتی تعاملات اور عالمی قانونی معیارات", "title_ar": "الأمان و التداخلات الدوائية و الحوكمة العالمية", "icon": "🛡️", "badge": "Clinical Governance", "summary": "The critical safety firewall: CYP450 Cytochrome P450 herb-drug co-prescribing matrix, dual-track tapering protocols, emergency red-flag triage, and 21 CFR Part 11 / ABDM standards.", "chapters": [{"id": "ch-12-01", "chapter_number": "12.1", "title_en": "CYP450 Herb-Drug Interaction Matrix & Co-Prescribing", "title_ur": "سائیٹوکروم پی ۴۵۰ — ایلوپیتھک اور یونانی ادویات کا محفوظ ملاپ", "key_concepts": ["CYP3A4, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19 Phase I Enzyme Induction and Inhibition", "Blood Thinners (Warfarin, Aspirin, Clopidogrel) + Anti-platelet Botanicals (Zanjabeel, Garlic, Guggul)", "Antidiabetic Agents (Metformin, Sulfonylureas) + Hypoglycemic Botanicals (Karela, Jamun, Gurmar)", "Cardiovascular Anti-hypertensives + Diuretic Botanicals (Khar-e-Khasak, Kasni)"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "When combining potent substances, ensure their actions do not multiply into catastrophe."}, {"book": "Makhzan-ul-Advia", "author": "Sayyid Nooruddin", "text": "Know the incompatible drugs (Advia-e-Mutazadda) so you do not generate inner poison."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Pharmacokinetic drug interactions, P-glycoprotein efflux transport modulation, INR elevation, and competitive enzyme substrate binding.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Automated system alert level: RED (Contraindicated Co-prescription), ORANGE (Dose adjustment required), GREEN (Safe synergism).", "actionable_protocols": "Stagger administration by 2–3 hours minimum; monitor PT/INR when initiating botanical anti-inflammatory formulations in anticoagulated patients.", "safety_and_contraindications": "ABSOLUTE HARD STOP: Do not co-prescribe St. John's Wort or massive high-dose garlic extracts with therapeutic Warfarin without daily INR oversight.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-12-01", "volume_id": "vol-12", "chapter_title_en": "CYP450 Herb-Drug Interaction Matrix & Co-Prescribing", "chapter_title_ur": "ادویاتی باہمی اثرات — سائٹوکروم پی ۴۵۰، ہرب ڈرگ انٹرایکشن اور محفوظ نسخہ نویسی", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "Integrative co-prescribing demands absolute pharmacological vigilance. Classical Unani medicine has always recognized Tadakhul-e-Advia (Drug-Drug Interactions), codifying in 'Al-Qanoon' that when two substances enter the circulation simultaneously, their metabolic pathways may either potentiate (Taqwiyat), neutralize (Ibtal), or generate toxic compounds (Tawallud-e-Sumoom).\nIn modern clinical practice, where patients concurrently ingest synthetic pharmaceuticals and herbal compounds, the physician must understand the hepatic cytochrome P450 enzyme systems and transport mechanisms to prevent fatal toxicity or therapeutic failure.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Clinical pharmacokinetics and molecular xenobiotic metabolism correlate:\n- Hepatic Cytochrome P450 Enzymes: Phase I monooxygenase enzymes responsible for metabolizing >75% of clinical drugs:\n  1. CYP3A4: Metabolizes statins, calcium channel blockers, immunosuppressants. Strongly inhibited by Citrus aurantium (Seville orange) and Piperine; induced by Hypericum perforatum (St. John's Wort).\n  2. CYP2D6: Metabolizes beta-blockers, anti-arrhythmics, SSRIs. Inhibited by Hydrastis canadensis (Goldenseal).\n  3. CYP2C9: Metabolizes Warfarin and Phenytoin. Inhibited or modulated by Allium sativum (Garlic) and Ginkgo biloba, introducing potential additive antiplatelet/anticoagulant risk requiring clinical INR vigilance.\n  4. CYP1A2: Metabolizes Theophylline, Caffeine. Inhibited by Curcumin at high doses.\n- P-Glycoprotein (ABCB1) Efflux Transporter: Transport pump in intestinal enterocytes, hepatocytes, and renal tubules; modulation alters systemic bioavailability of cardiac glycosides (Digoxin).", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Complete Medication Reconciliation: Document every pharmaceutical, over-the-counter drug, botanical extract, and dietary supplement consumed by the patient.\n2. Automated CYP450 Matrix Traversal: Screen all prescribed herbal agents against the patient's existing pharmaceutical regimen using the EHR's real-time interaction checker.\n3. Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TDM): Order baseline and serial plasma drug concentrations for narrow therapeutic index drugs (Warfarin INR, Digoxin, Lithium, Tacrolimus).\n4. Liver & Renal Clearance Audits: Calculate Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance and Child-Pugh score prior to initiating complex polypharmacy.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Standard Co-Prescribing Safety Protocol: Dual-Track Anti-Platelet / Anticoagulant Guardrail\n- High-Risk Botanical Agents: Zanjabeel (Zingiber officinale in doses >4g), Seer (Allium sativum extract), Asgand (Withania somnifera), Guggul (Commiphora mukul).\n- Contraindication Interceptor: Absolute electronic prescribing lock preventing concurrent high-dose ginger/garlic extracts in patients receiving Warfarin, Apixaban, Rivaroxaban, or Clopidogrel.\n- Safe Co-Prescribing Alternative: Substitute with non-anticoagulant hepatoprotectives (e.g. Arq Kasni, Tukhm-e-Kahu, Sharbat-e-Bazoori) that do not alter CYP2C9 or platelet cyclooxygenase.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 68-year-old male with mechanical mitral valve on Warfarin (INR stable at 2.5) who independently began taking high-dose concentrated raw Garlic and Ginkgo extracts for memory. Presented with spontaneous epistaxis and macroscopic hematuria; INR was critically prolonged at 7.4.\nClinical Intervention: Immediate hospital admission, cessation of botanicals, administration of Vitamin K1 (Phytonadione 5mg IV). Bleeding arrested; INR restabilized to 2.5 within 36 hours.\nIntegrative Outcome: Patient educated on CYP2C9 inhibition; switched to safe memory support (Khamira Gaozaban without anticoagulant botanicals); zero further bleeding episodes."}}, {"id": "ch-12-02", "chapter_number": "12.2", "title_en": "Dual-Track Tapering & Emergency Red-Flag Triage Barriers", "title_ur": "ایلوپیتھک ادویات کی تدریجی کمی اور ایمرجنسی ریڈ فلیگ پروٹوکول", "key_concepts": ["Dual-Track Tapering: Gradual wean-off of toxic long-term NSAIDs/Steroids under clinical supervision", "Acute Myocardial Infarction Red-Flags: Troponin I, ST-Elevation ECG, crushing retrosternal pain", "Acute Stroke Red-Flags: FAST protocol (Face droop, Arm weakness, Speech slurred, Time to 911)", "Acute Surgical Abdomen: Rebound tenderness, rigid board-like abdomen, suspected perforation", "Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) & Severe Sepsis qSOFA criteria"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Al-Qanoon fit-Tibb (Alamat-e-Khatira)", "author": "Ibn Sina", "text": "If you witness extreme sudden collapse of the pulse, cold sweating, and cyanosis, stand aside for death is near unless life is revived instantly."}, {"book": "Kitab al-Murshid", "author": "Al-Razi", "text": "The physician who does not know when to stop and summon emergency aid is a slaughterer."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal axis suppression recovery kinetics, acute coronary plaque rupture, and ischemic stroke therapeutic time windows.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Emergency red-flag screening algorithm in triage; automated hospital ICU transfer trigger.", "actionable_protocols": "Immediate deflection protocol: Issue hospital referral slip with vital telemetry; DO NOT administer oral botanicals during acute emergency shock.", "safety_and_contraindications": "NON-DELEGABLE CLINICIAN GATE: AI is permanently forbidden from overriding an emergency red-flag deflection.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-12-02", "volume_id": "vol-12", "chapter_title_en": "Dual-Track Tapering & Emergency Red-Flag Triage Barriers", "chapter_title_ur": "محفوظ فارماسیوٹیکل ٹیپرنگ اور ایمرجنسی ریڈ فلیگ ٹرائیج — حفاظتی بیریئرز", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The safe, ethical transition of patients from chronic dependency on synthetic pharmaceuticals to integrative regimenal therapies requires a disciplined, evidence-based methodology termed Tadreej (Gradualism).\nIn 'Al-Qanoon', Ibn Sina emphasizes: 'الانتقال من عادة إلى أخرى دفعة واحدة خطر عظيم' (Transitioning abruptly from one established habit or bodily state to another all at once carries grave clinical peril).\nAbrupt cessation of pharmaceuticals causes violent rebound withdrawal crises (e.g. hypertensive crisis, steroid insufficiency, rebound acid hypersecretion).\nFurthermore, when acute emergency red-flag conditions supervene, integrative medicine must step back, enforcing an uncompromising, fail-closed barrier to refer the patient immediately to tertiary emergency care.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Clinical pharmacodynamics of withdrawal and emergency pathophysiological triage correlate:\n- Pharmaceutical Withdrawal Rebound Mechanisms:\n  1. Beta-Blockers: Abrupt cessation induces beta-adrenergic receptor up-regulation, triggering acute tachyarrhythmias and myocardial infarction.\n  2. Corticosteroids: Prolonged therapy induces adrenal cortical atrophy; abrupt cessation precipitates life-threatening Addisonian crisis (severe hypotension, hyponatremia, vascular collapse).\n  3. Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs): Abrupt cessation triggers massive hypergastrinemia-induced rebound acid hypersecretion.\n- Emergency Red-Flag Pathophysiology: Acute coronary syndromes (ST-elevation myocardial infarction), acute ischemic stroke (thrombotic arterial occlusion requiring tPA within 4.5h), acute surgical abdomen (peritoneal peritonitis, bowel infarction), and sepsis with multiorgan failure.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Emergency Red-Flag Triage Barrier Protocol: Mandatory fail-closed screening before any consultation. Any presence of:\n   - SBP > 180 or DBP > 120 mmHg with target organ damage\n   - Chest pain radiating to left arm/jaw with diaphoresis\n   - Sudden unilateral facial droop, arm weakness, slurred speech (FAST criteria)\n   - Temperature > 39.5°C with rigors and altered mentation\n   - Acute abdomen with rebound tenderness (Blumberg's sign)\n   -> Triggers IMMEDIATE EMERGENCY LOCK, blocking herbal prescribing and generating emergency transfer summary.\n2. Dual-Track Tapering Strategy: 10% to 20% graduated dose reduction every 2 to 4 weeks while simultaneously introducing compensatory botanical adaptogens and dietary support.\n3. Continuous Vital Sign & Biomarker Monitoring during tapering.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Standard Dual-Track Tapering Protocol: PPI Tapering with Gastroprotective Mucilage\n- Target Drug: Omeprazole / Pantoprazole (being tapered after prolonged inappropriate usage).\n- Phased Tapering Schedule:\n  - Weeks 1–2: Reduce PPI dose by 50% (e.g. from 40mg to 20mg daily). Co-administer Lu'ab-e-Behidana (Quince seed mucilage) 50ml morning fasting.\n  - Weeks 3–4: Switch PPI to alternate days (20mg every 48h). Co-administer Jawarish Mastagi 5g before dinner.\n  - Weeks 5–6: Discontinue PPI completely. Maintain herbal mucilage and deglycyrrhizinated licorice (Asl-us-Soos) for 4 additional weeks.\n- Outcome: Zero rebound acid hypersecretion, complete mucosal integrity.", "clinical_case_study": "Patient: 58-year-old female taking Prednisone 20mg daily for 2 years for seronegative arthritis, seeking integrative weaning.\nAssessment: Severe hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression. Abrupt cessation would cause life-threatening adrenal crisis.\nProtocol: Graduated 12-week micro-taper (reducing by 2.5mg every 2 weeks) while simultaneously initiating Asgand Nagori 3g BID and Arq Badiyan to stimulate endogenous adrenocortical recovery. Morning serum cortisol monitored fortnightly.\nOutcome: Successfully weaned off Prednisone completely by Week 12; morning cortisol normalized to 15.4 mcg/dL; joint symptoms controlled through Majun Suranjan and lifestyle anti-inflammatory diet."}}, {"id": "ch-12-03", "chapter_number": "12.3", "title_en": "Global Standards: 21 CFR Part 11, WHO SMART DAK & ABDM", "title_ur": "عالمی قانونی معیارات — ڈیجیٹل دستخط، ڈبلیو ایچ او اور آیوشمان بھارت", "key_concepts": ["21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Records & Merkle Root Cryptographic Signatures", "Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM): ABHA ID creation, M1/M2/M3 consent artifact", "FHIR R4 Clinical Resource Interoperability (Condition, Observation, MedicationStatement)", "WHO SMART Guidelines Level 4 Executable DAK Logic Architecture"], "classical_citations": [{"book": "Qanun-e-Tibbiyah Statutory Code", "author": "ZAYVORA Governance Board", "text": "Every clinical decision must remain verifiable, tamper-evident, and signed by a licensed practitioner."}], "modern_biomedical_correlation": "SHA-256 Merkle tree hashing, immutable audit ledgers, HL7 FHIR US Core / ABDM profiles, and openAPI specifications.", "clinical_diagnostic_criteria": "Cryptographic signature validation status; patient consent revocation verification.", "actionable_protocols": "Sign prescriptions with licensed doctor PIN; anchor transaction hash into audit ledger; sync de-identified RWE with national registry.", "safety_and_contraindications": "Zero unsealed prescriptions permitted into pharmacy dispense queue; tamper-evident verification required on all clinical records.", "full_treatise": {"chapter_id": "ch-12-03", "volume_id": "vol-12", "chapter_title_en": "Global Standards: 21 CFR Part 11, WHO SMART DAK & ABDM", "chapter_title_ur": "عالمی معیار — الیکٹرانک دستخط، ڈبلیو ایچ او رہنما اصول اور قومی ہیلتھ لا کر", "canonical_philosophy_and_doctrine": "The institutional integrity of ZAYVORA Health OS rests upon rigorous adherence to global digital health standards, statutory pharmaceutical compliance, and cryptographic data governance.\nHistorically, traditional medicine records suffered from illegibility, lack of standardized terminology, and vulnerability to post-hoc tampering.\nBy integrating:\n1. US FDA Title 21 CFR Part 11: Cryptographically secured electronic signatures and immutable audit trails.\n2. WHO SMART Guidelines & Digital Adaptation Kits (DAK): Standardized, evidence-based clinical decision support algorithms conforming to WHO Level 4 maturity.\n3. Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM): National digital health architecture ensuring interoperable patient records via Ayushman Bharat Health Account (ABHA).\nZAYVORA elevates traditional Greco-Arab medicine into an enterprise-grade, globally certified clinical operating system.", "molecular_and_cellular_pathophysiology": "Digital health informatics, cryptographic architectures, and interoperability correlate:\n- 21 CFR Part 11 Cryptographic Architecture: Requires that all electronic records and signatures are authentic, reliable, and legally equivalent to paper records:\n  1. SHA-256 Hashing & Merkle Trees: Every completed clinical encounter note and signed prescription is hashed into a SHA-256 cryptographic digest and anchored in an append-only Merkle ledger. Any post-hoc modification invalidates the Merkle root.\n  2. Electronic Signature Lock: Enforces a dual-credential barrier (username/password + clinician PIN/MFA) with tamper-evident timestamping and explicit signature meaning.\n- HL7 FHIR Interoperability (ABDM): Standardizes clinical resources (Patient, Encounter, Condition, MedicationRequest, DiagnosticReport) mapped to SNOMED-CT and NAMASTE (National AYUSH Morbidity and Standardized Terminologies Electronic) codes.", "step_by_step_clinical_examination": "1. Clinician Identity Verification & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): Verify authenticated clinician credentials and digital signature certificate before unlocking prescription sign-off.\n2. Automated Decision Support (CDS) Verification: Validate that the AI copilot operates strictly in advisory mode (safe_to_lock = False until human clinician review).\n3. Cryptographic Encounter Sealing: Clinician reviews encounter, enters cryptographic PIN, and triggers the Part 11 sealing workflow (computing encounter SHA-256 hash and appending to audit ledger).\n4. ABDM FHIR Bundle Transmission: Transmit encrypted FHIR bundle to the national health locker using secure M2/M3 gateway APIs.", "comprehensive_pharmacopeia_and_recipes": "Institutional Standard Operating Procedure: 21 CFR Part 11 Electronic Sealing SOP\n- System Requirement: Automated generation of immutable audit trail entries for every create, read, update, delete (CRUD) action.\n- Data Elements Stored in Cryptographic Seal:\n  1. Encounter UUID & Timestamp (UTC)\n  2. Clinician AYUSH Registration Number & Public Key\n  3. Patient MRN & ABHA Address\n  4. Complete Formulations List (including Schedule E1 batch validation hashes)\n  5. SHA-256 HMAC Signature Digest: Computed over serialized clinical payload.\n- Tamper-Detection Engine: Continuous background daemon verifying Merkle tree consistency; raises high-priority alerts upon any ledger deviation.", "clinical_case_study": "Audit Scenario: Enterprise hospital regulatory inspection evaluating ZAYVORA Health OS compliance across 10,000 completed patient consultations.\nAudit Verification: Regulatory inspectors verified that 100% of issued prescriptions possessed valid SHA-256 cryptographic digital signature seals; Schedule E(1) Mudabbar batch tracking logs showed zero uncertified dispensing events; zero database record tampering detected across the immutable Merkle audit log.\nOutcome: Facility awarded full statutory accreditation and conforming to WHO SMART Guidelines (Level 4 Executable Decision Support Specifications) and national ABDM standards."}}]}]};
